WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:02.891 --> 00:00:07.800
- Let's call the public
board meeting to action

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.170
and welcome everybody.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.310
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.578
We have some apologies for
absence this afternoon.

00:00:14.578 --> 00:00:18.660
So both of the associate
NED Suresh Viswanathan

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.520
and Tanuj Kapilashrami

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.048
send their apologies.

00:00:22.048 --> 00:00:25.590
Stephen Powis has also sent his apologies,

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.870
but we're delighted to
have Claire Fuller here,

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:30.590
deputising for Steve and Jeremy Townsend

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:33.275
who is with us in this
morning's private board meeting,

00:00:33.275 --> 00:00:35.327
can't be here this afternoon.

00:00:35.327 --> 00:00:38.040
So they've sent apologies.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.960
Can I just go to declarations of interest?

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.520
Are there any changes

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.460
to colleagues' declarations of interest

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:45.963
since last registered?

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:48.420
Thank you very much.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.670
Can I then ask for approval of the minutes

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.640
of the board meeting held
on the 5th of December?

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.020
- Yeah, is that okay to take?

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.340
Thank you, no amendments.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:57.340
Thank you very much.

00:00:58.593 --> 00:01:03.000
If I just start with a chair's update,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.896
what I'd like to start with actually

00:01:04.896 --> 00:01:08.430
is just recognising,

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:09.570
giving personal thanks

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.190
and also thanks on behalf of the board

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.060
to two of our colleagues

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:18.540
for whom this is their last board meeting.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.100
So if I start, if I might with Wol

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.510
on my left, Wol Kolade.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:28.290
So Wol joined the board of NHS Improvement

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.750
in August 2018,

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.623
and then the board of NHS England in 2022.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.140
Wol is one of the deputy chairman,

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.060
the two deputy chairman
along with Andrew Morris.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.610
So actually the end of March,

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.723
actually I'm stepping down as
are the two deputy chairman.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.170
My huge thanks to Wol

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:52.842
for unstinting effort
on behalf of the board,

00:01:52.842 --> 00:01:56.790
the whole range of different committees

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:57.870
that Wol sits on,

00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:58.860
predominantly audit and risk,

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.243
but also NOMS Co, New Hospital Programme,

00:02:03.243 --> 00:02:06.239
Chief Financial Officer Advisory Group.

00:02:06.239 --> 00:02:09.300
But the one I'd like to just focus on

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.340
briefly and upfront

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.090
is while you chaired the
new NHS England Committee,

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.308
which was the committee that oversaw

00:02:17.308 --> 00:02:20.070
the combination of NHS England

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.530
with HEE, Health Education England,

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.270
and NHS Digital.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.610
And that following that NHS England

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.610
and NHS Improvement had already joined,

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.477
brought together at the top of the NHS

00:02:32.477 --> 00:02:34.653
in organisation structure,

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.540
over 24,000 people in the organisation.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:40.373
But if one thinks about it,

00:02:40.373 --> 00:02:43.770
it had to bring three substantive boards,

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.581
all of whom had very busy agendas,

00:02:46.581 --> 00:02:49.385
real objectives and real obligations,

00:02:49.385 --> 00:02:52.320
statutory duties that they had to pursue.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.760
And we had to merge those

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.970
along with all of the
staff working together

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.717
and then to bring that
into a concerted whole.

00:02:59.717 --> 00:03:01.410
And Wol, you led that

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:03.726
and you led it with huge
energy and diligence

00:03:03.726 --> 00:03:05.250
and real experience actually,

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:06.698
which you bring interest

00:03:06.698 --> 00:03:08.913
from the private sector.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.498
I would like to say that
actually NHS England

00:03:13.498 --> 00:03:17.490
with those entities within
it continue to operate,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.520
barely missing a beat actually
in terms of its governance

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.413
and its ability to focus and
maintain the agendas of those

00:03:23.413 --> 00:03:25.620
or the oversight of the agendas

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:26.850
of those three entities.

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:30.360
So you structured it, Wol, really well.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.390
We also did, again under your leadership,

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:35.290
the very difficult task of actually

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:36.825
as we bring people together,

00:03:36.825 --> 00:03:38.790
and we saw common functions.

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.353
Actually, we were able to reduce

00:03:41.353 --> 00:03:43.770
the head count of NHS England

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:46.530
from over 24,000 people

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:50.100
to just over 15,000 in that exercise.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:53.249
I think it is the singular,

00:03:53.249 --> 00:03:55.953
most sizable public sector transformation

00:03:55.953 --> 00:03:57.626
just in terms of those numbers of roles

00:03:57.626 --> 00:03:59.790
in one single exercise

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:03.750
that actually were removed.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:04.737
As I say, that was done

00:04:04.737 --> 00:04:07.470
and it saved almost 500 million pounds

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:09.870
that was actually then redirected

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:11.820
away from, if you like, head office

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.320
and into the front line.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:14.880
It was a very difficult exercise, Wol,

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.730
and you led it with consummate skill.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:18.900
Step away from that,

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:22.470
I would like to thank you
personally for your huge support.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:23.657
As deputy chairman,

00:04:23.657 --> 00:04:26.248
you with Andrew and me

00:04:26.248 --> 00:04:29.100
meet very regularly with Amanda and Julian

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.853
and continue to do that

00:04:30.853 --> 00:04:32.070
outside the rhythm of the board meetings.

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.380
It's a huge, huge engagement

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.970
and know colleagues on the board

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.130
give you their deep thanks
and actually we'll miss you.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.750
So thank you, Wol, very much.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:47.640
If I can turn to Andrew
now, Andrew Morris,

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.470
the other deputy chair.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:51.210
In very similar start point, I think,

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.160
so that in well,

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:56.550
different experiences from long back,

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:58.140
but actually August 2018,

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:00.450
you also joined the
board of NHS Improvement,

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.610
and then in '22,

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.100
joined the board of NHS England.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.530
And you have been an
absolute tower of strength

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.150
on the board, Andrew.

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:11.160
The amount of stuff that
you do for the board

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.710
in terms of the committees that you sit on

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.840
and people don't often
see the amount of work

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.290
that's done by a board
in its subcommittees

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:23.490
to make sure that all of the
agenda items are covered.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:27.767
And so Andrew, whether you
sit and you chair WTEC,

00:05:27.767 --> 00:05:30.660
so the Workforce, Training
and Education Committee,

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:32.280
you sit on PRC,

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.140
you sit on New Hospital Committee,

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:36.033
you sit on CFO Advisory Group,

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.048
you also have been the
FTSU Guardian champion.

00:05:41.048 --> 00:05:43.136
You've been our Maternity champion

00:05:43.136 --> 00:05:48.136
and actually one of the most
demanding calls on your time

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:50.220
has actually been when you sit

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:52.740
and you chair the board
to boards in the RSP.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:56.160
So our remediation
Recovery Support Programme

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.140
that we run with those trusts of ICBs,

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.810
which are under the most pressure.

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:03.652
And I think you chair
80% of those meetings

00:06:03.652 --> 00:06:06.420
and Andrew, so huge, huge thanks to you.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:08.190
And also on a personal level, again,

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:10.694
you know, working
actually as a triumvirate

00:06:10.694 --> 00:06:12.360
with Wol and with me

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.400
and I think it's been
quite a happy triumvirate.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.080
I say that, but I've got the microphone,

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:17.730
so that's all right.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:21.180
Actually, my personal thanks
to you for all you've done.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:24.210
I say that and then it's
also you get the chance

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:27.600
to hear your obituary early

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.430
because there is some
conversation ongoing as you know

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:32.910
with the Department of Health.

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:35.190
So we're exploring with
the Department of Health

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:38.670
whether there's a short
term extension for you

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:42.180
in which we quite like
to achieve, if we can,

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.340
to help manage the transition,

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.740
and frankly ensure continuity for the work

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.060
that you are leading

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:49.470
on behalf of the board.

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:54.360
And that's as we process
through one, the attraction

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:56.010
to the board of new non-executives

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.050
and clearly also a new chair.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.670
But Andrew, it may well be that you stay

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:01.440
for a shorter period

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:03.750
beyond the end of March,

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:05.520
but that doesn't stop in any way.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.440
My thanks to you on behalf of the board

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.003
for all that you have done.

00:07:11.663 --> 00:07:14.250
Let me go on

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:17.140
and just very quickly
cover a few highlights only

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.000
in relation to my own
activities since the last board.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.509
So actually since the last meeting,

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:27.150
Andrew and I have joined twice weekly

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:31.020
Winter Assurance calls with Sarah-Jane.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:34.920
So receiving reports back
on how actually the NHS

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.803
has been handling winter
and where the pressures were

00:07:36.803 --> 00:07:38.400
and the particular pressures,

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:39.950
and then the responses to them.

00:07:40.817 --> 00:07:43.110
Forgive me, I've been continuing

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:45.510
a personal focus on frailty,

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:47.433
so on the frail and elderly.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:51.535
So I went on a last week
to the Black Country

00:07:51.535 --> 00:07:56.280
and I went to the Midland
Metropolitan University Hospital

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:59.460
and also met the Black Country ICB,

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:02.700
where we had a very, it was
very insightful visit actually.

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:04.230
And their engagement,

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:07.349
particularly in the
Sandwell Frailty initiative;

00:08:07.349 --> 00:08:09.180
clear clues, I think,

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.242
as to how we might improve our engagement

00:08:12.242 --> 00:08:14.819
for the benefit of the
smooth running of the NHS

00:08:14.819 --> 00:08:16.410
to improve our engagement

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:19.710
with frail and elderly patients.

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:20.543
And then on Monday,

00:08:20.543 --> 00:08:22.110
I was at Eastbourne District Hospital,

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:26.506
again, also with the Sussex ICB.

00:08:26.506 --> 00:08:28.770
And again, most of the focus of that day

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:30.990
was on frail and elderly.

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:32.730
And what are the options

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.460
and what are the potential
choices we could make

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:38.223
in terms of how we change our offering.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:41.379
I had a very interesting meeting

00:08:41.379 --> 00:08:44.463
with a professor of economics
at the London Business School,

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:46.890
professor Andrew Scott,

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:48.870
who actually has a high focus on longevity

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:50.880
and actually a key founder
of The Longevity Forum.

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:53.614
So again, it was part
of me trying to improve,

00:08:53.614 --> 00:08:56.130
forgive me, my personal understanding

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:59.310
of the challenges in
offering proactive care

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:02.643
to our elderly and frail.

00:09:05.250 --> 00:09:07.320
I usually update the board on genomics.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:08.610
Let me do that very briefly.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:10.620
I've been in a couple of board meetings,

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:11.910
Genomics England board meetings

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:14.850
where I attend the board with Sue Hill,

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:15.800
professor Sue Hill.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:19.333
And actually Amanda and I were also,

00:09:19.333 --> 00:09:20.520
we also had the pleasure of attending

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:23.280
the NHS Genomics Medicine Service Summit

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:25.290
in December which was led by Sue.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:27.810
And actually it was that
presentation you made

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:29.343
actually at that summit, Sue,

00:09:30.219 --> 00:09:31.740
that prompted us to think,

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:33.110
would you come and do
the presentation today?

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:35.370
'Cause it's very interesting just to see

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:37.713
the extent of progress and also the extent

00:09:37.713 --> 00:09:40.410
of ambition we have for genomics.

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:42.450
I think it is that we would anticipate

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:43.740
that in 10 years time,

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:46.380
50% of health interventions will include,

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:49.890
involve some form of genomics analysis

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:53.553
or genomics, yes, genomics analysis.

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:57.090
Now as a board, we've discussed
the 10-year Health Plan.

00:09:57.090 --> 00:09:59.400
Last Friday morning, the board met

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:02.640
to have a further update

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:05.365
with the 10-year Health Plan team.

00:10:05.365 --> 00:10:06.570
On a personal note,

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:08.550
I've had a very good couple of sessions

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:09.720
with one of the co-chairs

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:11.970
to the physical
infrastructure working group,

00:10:12.923 --> 00:10:16.083
essentially discussing with him

00:10:16.083 --> 00:10:18.480
what potential plans might emerge

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:19.740
in terms of a 10-Year Plan

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:23.357
with regard to the NHS's
physical infrastructure.

00:10:23.357 --> 00:10:26.850
I also attended a public
deliberative event in London

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:28.050
where Andrew Gwynne, the minister,

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:30.837
was the lead for government.

00:10:30.837 --> 00:10:32.864
And I was struck actually
just by how engaged

00:10:32.864 --> 00:10:35.804
and what a good set of discussions

00:10:35.804 --> 00:10:39.354
was held in the room.

00:10:39.354 --> 00:10:42.270
And I'm going up later this month,

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:43.650
actually next week, I think,

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:45.480
to Birmingham to join a similar event,

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:47.460
but for staff, the NHS staff,

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:48.910
which I think is in Solihull.

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:51.600
Last week, we issued,

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:55.140
published the Planning and
Operational Guidance for '25/26,

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:57.390
which does see a significant streamlining

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:59.910
of priorities and measures of success.

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:01.170
And that's to help us focus

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:03.240
on what matters most to patients,

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:06.750
and also giving local
systems greater flexibility

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:08.340
to use their funding allocation

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:11.430
to best meet the health needs
of their local populations.

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:14.010
And I know, Amanda, that you'll address

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:15.180
that in your words, I'm sure.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.939
And Julian, probably also in his.

00:11:17.939 --> 00:11:19.740
And then just a couple of meetings,

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:22.380
so one with the Academy of
Medical Royal College presidents

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:24.690
and separately a meeting at the department

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:27.380
with other health ALB chairs

00:11:27.380 --> 00:11:29.730
where the Secretary of State also came.

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:31.620
And we had a very helpful conversation,

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:33.090
mainly focused actually

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:35.373
around the 10-year Health Plan.

00:11:36.330 --> 00:11:40.290
Now I am going to ask
actually for the board

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:41.760
to humour me a little bit

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:43.000
because I would like

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:48.175
just to reflect on actually,

00:11:48.175 --> 00:11:51.060
Amanda, what you and your executive team

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:53.040
actually have been delivering

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:55.890
over the last three years or so.

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:59.250
I do that because this is my
last board here in London.

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:01.050
And actually, I think
it's really important

00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:04.290
that almost for the record, actually,

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:09.290
that we recite just the
extent of the delivery,

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:12.000
Amanda, which you with Julian and Emily

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.370
and the rest of the executive
team around this table,

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:16.320
what you have achieved.

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:18.360
And forgive me, you can try and stop me,

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:20.110
but I often describe

00:12:21.450 --> 00:12:23.430
what you and your team do

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:25.950
as really excellent operational

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:28.110
and managerial delivery.

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:31.140
It's operational and it's managerial

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:33.030
and it requires real focus.

00:12:33.030 --> 00:12:33.863
At the same time,

00:12:33.863 --> 00:12:36.390
you have to engage wholly appropriately

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:39.330
into Whitehall, into the
Department of Health,

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:40.890
more broadly into Treasury,

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:44.910
and to handle the perfectly sensible often

00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:47.190
and appropriate requests we get

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:50.193
from Secretary of State.

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:53.010
But what I really focus on

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:55.258
is just how much delivery

00:12:55.258 --> 00:12:57.960
you and your team have achieved.

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.930
So if I gave some background
and thought, first of all,

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:04.500
about the fact that I think the NHS today

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:07.803
is providing absolute record
volumes of healthcare,

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:10.470
record volumes of healthcare

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:11.490
into the population.

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:14.610
So whether it's actually
just some recent stats,

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:15.990
whether it's the fact that last year,

00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:20.850
there were almost just under
380 million GP appointments,

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:23.460
primary care appointments in England.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:24.990
And if you think what that represents,

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:26.280
it represents the equivalent

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:29.040
of one in seven of the population

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.230
having an appointment in primary care

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:33.330
every single week.

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:34.350
And more to the point,

00:13:34.350 --> 00:13:36.030
it's 60 million more in a year

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:37.500
than it was pre-pandemic.

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:42.500
So, huge thanks to our primary
care practitioners, our GPs,

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:44.280
just for the sheer amount

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:49.130
of patient support that
they are providing.

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:51.174
In cancer, last year,

00:13:51.174 --> 00:13:53.790
there were 3 million cancer referrals.

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:57.330
That was 27% up on 2019 in fact.

00:13:57.330 --> 00:13:59.700
And it was more than double the number

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:01.980
of cancer referrals and diagnoses.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:04.920
They were actually carried out
only eight years before that.

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.500
So we are identifying, as
Peter Johnson often says,

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:10.143
we're identifying cancer earlier.

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:14.790
And that's helping us to get
treatment to people earlier.

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:16.290
We clearly still need to do better

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:17.640
on constitutional standards,

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:19.664
but 3 million cancer referrals,

00:14:19.664 --> 00:14:22.683
as I say last year, remarkable.

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:26.190
If I then think about what we've just seen

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:29.130
in this winter,

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:31.203
so we'll talk about it later on,

00:14:32.074 --> 00:14:34.890
the NHS throughout all of its operations,

00:14:34.890 --> 00:14:38.130
has handled, I think
it's 7.4% higher volume

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:39.780
than the winter before.

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:42.690
So in the winter '24 to
the beginning of '25,

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.430
compared with '23/24,

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:46.560
we've seen over 7% higher volume

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:49.920
in terms of patient presentations
and have handled that.

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.890
And we'll hear later on, I think
we've handled that actually

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:55.410
very, I mean really strongly.

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:57.597
I know we've got, we can do better,

00:14:57.597 --> 00:15:00.240
but actually we're handling
ever greater demand

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:02.100
and holding to standards,

00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:04.203
if not slightly improving them.

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:07.540
What about delivery?

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:09.810
Just a few things.

00:15:09.810 --> 00:15:11.580
So community diagnostic centres,

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:14.724
a foundation actually for the
governments recently announced

00:15:14.724 --> 00:15:17.714
focus on increasing and
improving diagnostics.

00:15:17.714 --> 00:15:19.950
So in the last two years,

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:21.644
the NHS has doubled

00:15:21.644 --> 00:15:23.700
the number of community diagnostic centres

00:15:23.700 --> 00:15:25.560
from 89 to 180.

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:29.970
We've rolled out same-day
emergency care, SDECs,

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:32.683
which now operate in
about 88% of our trusts.

00:15:32.683 --> 00:15:36.300
A huge increase in the
rollout of that capability

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:38.130
over the last couple of years.

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:40.230
Urgent treatment centres, the same.

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:42.210
You then go to virtual wards

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:43.530
and I just said this morning,

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.301
we talked about virtual wards a little bit

00:15:45.301 --> 00:15:47.133
and for me, it's a very,

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:52.800
very brilliant step forward for the NHS.

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:55.320
Although I'm frustrated that
we stopped where we stopped.

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:57.060
So when I first was appointed,

00:15:57.060 --> 00:15:59.280
it coincided with an announcement

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:00.540
that we were going to roll out

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:05.370
between 40,000 and 50,000
virtual wards hospitals at home.

00:16:05.370 --> 00:16:07.170
And actually, we started a little slowly,

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:09.090
but then we built them out really fast

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:10.680
and staffed them really fast.

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:13.050
And what they are, of
course, is technological,

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:15.420
technology-based monitoring of patients,

00:16:15.420 --> 00:16:18.780
essentially at home or in a home.

00:16:18.780 --> 00:16:20.730
So it's exactly in line with the shift,

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:24.330
which is to move care out of the hospital

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:25.620
into the community.

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:28.770
And then, we stopped
at 12,400 virtual wards

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:31.050
and we stopped for a variety of reasons.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:33.210
But if it were my personal choice

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:34.760
and we had the resource for it,

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.500
then I actually would
continue that programme.

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:40.200
And whether 40,000 is the
right number to get to

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:41.970
or whether it's 30,000,

00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:43.440
personally I certainly feel

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:45.399
it's more than the 12,400.

00:16:45.399 --> 00:16:47.010
But the important thing, Amanda,

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:49.320
is that you and the team built those out

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:50.550
and we saw real benefit.

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:54.870
I think they operate
about 85% utilisation.

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:56.670
What else? I mean that sounds quite a lot,

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:58.710
but the lot first Long Term Workforce Plan

00:16:58.710 --> 00:16:59.970
produced a couple of years ago,

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:01.070
first Long Term Workforce Plan

00:17:01.070 --> 00:17:03.480
in 75 years history of the NHS,

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:05.670
we did a Long Term Capital Plan

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:08.550
called the Long Term Infrastructure
Strategy, not published.

00:17:08.550 --> 00:17:11.280
But given to the Department
and given to the Treasury,

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:15.539
it was based on properly
sourced foundational fact base

00:17:15.539 --> 00:17:19.560
about the physical estate
that the NHS operates through.

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:22.260
And I do think it's a
seminal document actually

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:24.480
for the physical
infrastructure working group

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:25.890
that I was referring to earlier

00:17:25.890 --> 00:17:27.870
as they look to the 10-year Health Plan.

00:17:27.870 --> 00:17:29.013
So that was produced.

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:31.050
We certainly shouldn't forget

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:34.230
because it's a seismic change
the way the NHS operates.

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:36.515
But we gave birth, not we gave birth,

00:17:36.515 --> 00:17:41.515
but the ICBs emerged actually in July,

00:17:41.670 --> 00:17:43.860
almost three years or so ago now.

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:48.690
And they are a mechanism
for devolution of health

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:50.010
closer the community

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:52.110
and closer to the local population.

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:54.210
And in many cases, they're showing,

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:56.040
you know, excellent steps forward.

00:17:56.040 --> 00:17:57.630
Some have more challenges,

00:17:57.630 --> 00:18:00.390
but actually the ICBs have emerged

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:02.190
in that period.

00:18:02.190 --> 00:18:05.377
And I referred separately to,
while this was all going on,

00:18:05.377 --> 00:18:07.530
actually, NHS England was combining

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:09.240
with Health Education England.

00:18:09.240 --> 00:18:11.018
NHS Digital had absorbed NHS Improvement.

00:18:11.018 --> 00:18:13.710
And we shouldn't forget NHSX,

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:17.629
and was actually reducing
its workforce by 35%

00:18:17.629 --> 00:18:19.890
and continuing to manage

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:21.429
all of those board agendas.

00:18:21.429 --> 00:18:23.217
So significant exercise

00:18:23.217 --> 00:18:25.380
of restructuring and merger

00:18:25.380 --> 00:18:27.213
in that same period.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:29.191
I did say

00:18:29.191 --> 00:18:31.473
you'd have to humour me.

00:18:32.554 --> 00:18:34.350
I did an interview recently,

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:35.820
in fact yesterday,

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:36.720
and I was asked,

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:39.030
what has surprised me
when I came into the NHS?

00:18:39.030 --> 00:18:41.910
And one of the things I said
was actually I was surprised

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:45.060
by just how innovative it is.

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:46.560
And I hadn't realised,

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:49.470
I hadn't realised just
how innovative the NHS is.

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:51.360
So whether it's in genomics

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:53.970
where last year we did 800,000 tests,

00:18:53.970 --> 00:18:56.820
either whole genome panel or single tests.

00:18:56.820 --> 00:18:58.890
And we've now scanned for 200 cancers,

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:01.417
7,000 rare diseases,
remarkable, remarkable.

00:19:01.417 --> 00:19:04.799
And we are one of the world leaders.

00:19:04.799 --> 00:19:09.799
We've started the Newborn
Programme in genomics

00:19:10.590 --> 00:19:11.850
and we finished and completed

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:14.643
on the 100,000 whole Genome Project.

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:18.312
Last year, cancer vaccinations,

00:19:18.312 --> 00:19:20.010
Cancer Vaccine platform,

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:22.980
liquid biopsies, absolutely remarkable.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:24.720
AI, we use AI,

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:25.590
we've been using AI

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:27.330
for a number of years

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:28.830
in screening and scanning

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:30.705
whether it's for breast cancer

00:19:30.705 --> 00:19:31.800
or brain scans.

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:33.960
We use it in dermatology.

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:35.836
We use it in our ophthalmics.

00:19:35.836 --> 00:19:39.450
So we're using AI as a new way

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:41.640
to deal with volume scanning.

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:44.460
And actually, that's been
running for some time.

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:47.040
And then actually, I've had teachings

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:48.860
on robotics and nanorobotics.

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:51.840
So nanorobotics, which are
capable of single-cell cure

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:56.190
or single-cell or a brain aneurysm.

00:19:56.190 --> 00:19:57.720
you know, it's not generally available,

00:19:57.720 --> 00:19:59.010
but we now have the potential

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:01.320
and have achieved that.

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:05.850
Imagine the innovation
that that is promising

00:20:05.850 --> 00:20:07.260
for the future.

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:08.820
But going back to the Cancer Vaccine,

00:20:08.820 --> 00:20:10.620
I have often said,

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:12.930
actually having heard about
the cancer vaccinations

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:14.460
that we gave for bowel cancer

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:17.460
and lung cancer last year,

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:19.950
we can now conceive a world

00:20:19.950 --> 00:20:23.070
where actually we could
see cancer vaccination

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:24.660
as a fourth armament

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:26.550
with which to fight the scourge of cancer.

00:20:26.550 --> 00:20:29.317
So that we use surgery and
radiotherapy and chemotherapy.

00:20:29.317 --> 00:20:32.070
But imagine, and we can conceive it

00:20:32.070 --> 00:20:33.900
that for a number of cancers,

00:20:33.900 --> 00:20:36.360
not every cancer but
for a number of cancers,

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:37.989
we may well have a new armament,

00:20:37.989 --> 00:20:41.223
which would be vaccination.

00:20:42.234 --> 00:20:45.243
And then, I'm going to
talk about technology.

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:49.560
The NHS App now has 35
million subscribers.

00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:52.410
It has much improved functionality.

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:53.580
It is transformational

00:20:53.580 --> 00:20:55.350
to the way the NHS interacts

00:20:55.350 --> 00:20:57.420
with the population and society.

00:20:57.420 --> 00:20:59.306
And there's much more that we can do

00:20:59.306 --> 00:21:02.310
and it can be used essentially
partly to rationalise

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:04.860
and drive through in
connection to legacy systems,

00:21:04.860 --> 00:21:07.170
but also we can improve its functionality

00:21:07.170 --> 00:21:08.730
and what it offers patients

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:12.600
in terms of their engagement with the NHS.

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:14.220
The Federated Data Platform

00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:16.470
was awarded about a couple of years ago,

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.750
a very significant procurement

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:20.820
and the FDP is a translation layer

00:21:20.820 --> 00:21:22.470
that sits across all our hospitals,

00:21:22.470 --> 00:21:23.910
all our secondary data,

00:21:23.910 --> 00:21:26.280
and allows us to translate their data

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:28.440
without them all having to recode

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:30.150
their underlying machine code.

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:33.324
So again, the NHS app, FDP.

00:21:33.324 --> 00:21:34.710
When I first arrived,

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:37.020
I remember Amanda and Julian and others,

00:21:37.020 --> 00:21:38.550
so Amanda Doyle and others here

00:21:38.550 --> 00:21:42.265
talking about our primary care practises

00:21:42.265 --> 00:21:45.240
and just some of the basic
problems we had in telephony.

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:46.620
So actually you invested in

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:48.750
and rolled out digital telephony,

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:53.010
which now is in 98% of
primary care practises

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:55.800
and it is a major step
forward in improving access.

00:21:55.800 --> 00:21:57.120
But actually, it took investment,

00:21:57.120 --> 00:21:59.484
it took decision and prioritisation.

00:21:59.484 --> 00:22:03.030
Electronic patient records
now in primary care

00:22:03.030 --> 00:22:05.460
and/or in hospital trusts,

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:08.910
remarkable growth in electronic
patient record take up

00:22:08.910 --> 00:22:10.890
throughout our, as I say, primary care

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:13.110
and our trust in the 90 percents,

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:16.020
high 90 percents in terms of EPR

00:22:16.020 --> 00:22:17.580
now being operational.

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:19.560
Now we've got to make them connect better.

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:21.090
But the first step is there,

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:23.430
it's followed real focus.

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:25.380
And we still now with Vin,

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:27.104
we look at ambient documentation,

00:22:27.104 --> 00:22:30.570
ambient documentation
where we are essentially

00:22:30.570 --> 00:22:31.890
voice-to-text,

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:34.770
potentially saving 30 to
40% of a clinician's time,

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:37.770
improving the interaction of
the clinician with the patient

00:22:37.770 --> 00:22:40.500
in terms of the quality
of that engagement.

00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:42.660
And you are running a
number of pilots in that.

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:44.775
So you look at innovation in technology,

00:22:44.775 --> 00:22:47.384
I looked at, I mentioned
earlier in terms of genomics

00:22:47.384 --> 00:22:49.097
and in cancer vaccine.

00:22:49.097 --> 00:22:52.320
And it just goes to show
the NHS is innovative

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:53.640
and continues to change

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:55.743
and continues to improve all the time.

00:22:56.790 --> 00:22:58.260
But we still have to fix our basics.

00:22:58.260 --> 00:23:00.960
We absolutely understand that
we have to fix our basics.

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:02.730
But Amanda, to you and the team,

00:23:02.730 --> 00:23:03.780
what you have delivered,

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:07.020
I think in this period
that I've had the privilege

00:23:07.020 --> 00:23:10.910
of being alongside you,
I think is remarkable

00:23:10.910 --> 00:23:12.630
and is often missed.

00:23:12.630 --> 00:23:16.410
And you deliver that through
operational managerial focus.

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:19.320
And you've also delivered it
whilst Julian, my compliments,

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:21.570
you balancing the financials,

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:24.510
which is a huge task with
lots of different pressures

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:26.010
where it's been managed in a period

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:28.620
where we've seen substantial
industrial action

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:31.500
and some periods of steepening inflation.

00:23:31.500 --> 00:23:34.446
So actually, to handle
the numbers and to produce

00:23:34.446 --> 00:23:36.840
the performance that you
have against that background,

00:23:36.840 --> 00:23:39.450
I think, is even more remarkable.

00:23:39.450 --> 00:23:41.580
And then, there are just two
other things I would refer to,

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:43.155
but only very briefly

00:23:43.155 --> 00:23:45.180
and they are a couple of pilots

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:47.520
and they're actually on the
agenda we had this morning.

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:49.326
So one is the primary
care, I call it a pilot,

00:23:49.326 --> 00:23:52.950
but it's the primary care
exploration of new models

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:54.240
to operate where Amanda,

00:23:54.240 --> 00:23:56.684
you and your team are engaged with GPs

00:23:56.684 --> 00:23:58.980
looking at how can we help,

00:23:58.980 --> 00:24:01.916
what's such an important part of the NHS

00:24:01.916 --> 00:24:04.860
taking and managing a lot
of the risks for the NHS,

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:07.860
our GPs and our primary care practitioners

00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:08.910
are there better ways,

00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:10.680
either through, are there better ways

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:14.100
that we can help them run their practises.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:16.080
And then separately,
there's a frailty pilot,

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:17.490
there's a frailty and the elderly pilot.

00:24:17.490 --> 00:24:20.250
We discussed it again
in the private board.

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:22.500
It's on the agenda for this afternoon.

00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:24.630
And the board will know from
my earlier conversation,

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:27.480
I think, that is one
of the, if it were me,

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:29.760
one of the most important areas

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:31.410
on which we should focus

00:24:31.410 --> 00:24:32.790
in the 10-year Health Plan

00:24:32.790 --> 00:24:35.910
in terms of what should we
think about implementing first

00:24:35.910 --> 00:24:37.410
that would have most impact

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:41.030
upon a relatively small
cohort of the population,

00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:44.850
but a high intense
utilisation of our capacity.

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:46.530
So it's the frequent flyers

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:48.780
or the intense users of our capacity

00:24:48.780 --> 00:24:51.314
are in many cases are frail

00:24:51.314 --> 00:24:53.460
and are elderly.

00:24:53.460 --> 00:24:55.408
So Amanda, I wanted to to list,

00:24:55.408 --> 00:24:56.850
and I'm sorry for that

00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:58.860
for being a little bit tedious,

00:24:58.860 --> 00:24:59.820
but I sometimes think

00:24:59.820 --> 00:25:03.240
that what you and your team have delivered

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:05.160
over the last three years actually,

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:09.660
I mean, it's just worthy of
reflecting on it actually,

00:25:09.660 --> 00:25:10.800
really worthy of reflecting on it.

00:25:10.800 --> 00:25:12.293
I think it's a huge,

00:25:12.293 --> 00:25:15.388
a huge delivery agenda
that you have achieved.

00:25:15.388 --> 00:25:17.700
So, thank you very much.

00:25:17.700 --> 00:25:19.260
Now at that point

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:21.460
and before you even respond to that,

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:23.100
which you shouldn't,

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:25.890
I'd like you to run on

00:25:25.890 --> 00:25:27.519
to the chief execs update,

00:25:27.519 --> 00:25:29.190
but there's no need to
comment on anything.

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:30.023
So I've just said,

00:25:30.023 --> 00:25:32.070
but if you would just like to give us

00:25:32.070 --> 00:25:33.820
your CEO's update, that'd be great.

00:25:35.010 --> 00:25:36.733
- Thank you very much indeed, Richard,

00:25:36.733 --> 00:25:41.100
and I recognise everything
you've just said.

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:42.960
But I know you've said
I don't need to respond

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:45.000
but I would like to just add,

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:47.430
if I may, two things.

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:50.760
One, anything that we have achieved

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:52.846
is because of how we work
with partners and colleagues

00:25:52.846 --> 00:25:55.302
and particularly NHS staff

00:25:55.302 --> 00:25:58.249
and the leadership
community within the NHS.

00:25:58.249 --> 00:26:00.780
So the credit is theirs.

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:03.390
And two, we all know and have discussed

00:26:03.390 --> 00:26:05.220
extensively as a board

00:26:05.220 --> 00:26:07.290
that despite everything you've said,

00:26:07.290 --> 00:26:09.370
things are not nearly good enough,

00:26:09.370 --> 00:26:11.490
- Yeah.
- for our patients.

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:14.310
So that is why we are really ambitious

00:26:14.310 --> 00:26:17.610
about scaling up and
spreading what's worked best

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:19.620
in parts of the NHS over recent years,

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:21.930
lots of which you've described

00:26:21.930 --> 00:26:23.640
and working with government

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:26.190
as they develop the 10-year Health Plan

00:26:26.190 --> 00:26:29.610
to set out how we will
radically transform the NHS,

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:32.460
building particularly on the
opportunities we now have

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:35.793
with technology, with
data, with life sciences.

00:26:37.830 --> 00:26:40.500
So on behalf of the executive,

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:42.720
I'm really conscious
you don't want me to say

00:26:42.720 --> 00:26:44.760
what you have said again,

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:48.240
but it would be remiss of
us, I think, not to just

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:49.530
thank Wol,
- Yeah.

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:50.850
- For his time as deputy chair

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:53.970
because Wol support from
my executive colleagues

00:26:53.970 --> 00:26:57.720
I know has been enormously
appreciated as well,

00:26:57.720 --> 00:27:00.720
Wol, as the extensive experience
that you have brought us

00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:03.121
from the private sector and elsewhere.

00:27:03.121 --> 00:27:06.540
And as you said, the
amount of effort that, Wol,

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:09.180
you've put into the new
NHS England Programme

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:11.970
and thank you particularly for that.

00:27:11.970 --> 00:27:13.530
I'm conscious of what you've just said

00:27:13.530 --> 00:27:15.863
about a potential extension for Andrew.

00:27:15.863 --> 00:27:17.370
But just in case,

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:18.420
I don't have the opportunity,

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:19.447
- He's like the windmill theatre,

00:27:19.447 --> 00:27:21.227
he never closes or something like that.

00:27:21.227 --> 00:27:23.493
- I would just also like to make sure

00:27:23.493 --> 00:27:25.500
that we have properly thanked Andrew,

00:27:25.500 --> 00:27:26.760
again on behalf of the executive,

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:28.830
for the expertise that, Andrew,

00:27:28.830 --> 00:27:31.350
you have provided on the
board of NHS Improvement

00:27:31.350 --> 00:27:33.262
and more latterly NHS England.

00:27:33.262 --> 00:27:37.103
But those are obviously
just the most recent years

00:27:37.103 --> 00:27:40.200
of what has been a distinguished career

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:42.300
spanning five decades.

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:44.430
And I think it's fair to say there are few

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:45.840
who have made the kind of mark

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:48.180
on the NHS that Andrew has

00:27:48.180 --> 00:27:49.620
and we would be poorer,

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:51.993
will be poorer without your wisdom.

00:27:53.490 --> 00:27:55.962
So let's hope we don't lose it too soon.

00:27:55.962 --> 00:27:57.930
- Can I just also, I'm
going to come back with you.

00:27:57.930 --> 00:27:59.811
You are quite right too.

00:27:59.811 --> 00:28:02.730
And I'd like to pick up
the emphasis and just say,

00:28:02.730 --> 00:28:05.397
in spite of that list of achievements,

00:28:05.397 --> 00:28:06.990
we do absolutely recognise

00:28:06.990 --> 00:28:11.146
we need to get better in a
number of our basic provisions.

00:28:11.146 --> 00:28:12.990
And secondly, you are quite right.

00:28:12.990 --> 00:28:15.420
You know, I have the
privilege of going out

00:28:15.420 --> 00:28:16.650
into the frontline a lot

00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:18.390
and people host me a lot

00:28:18.390 --> 00:28:19.860
and I always come back.

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:21.480
One of the best parts of the job

00:28:21.480 --> 00:28:22.770
I've been asked to do actually

00:28:22.770 --> 00:28:25.080
is the ability to meet the frontline

00:28:25.080 --> 00:28:26.010
and not just the frontline

00:28:26.010 --> 00:28:28.170
but the managers throughout our system

00:28:28.170 --> 00:28:29.790
and our frontline staff

00:28:29.790 --> 00:28:31.170
who are passionate and committed,

00:28:31.170 --> 00:28:33.450
and I think having a huge
burden and do a great job.

00:28:33.450 --> 00:28:34.411
So, thank you

00:28:34.411 --> 00:28:35.244
for reminding me.
- Great.

00:28:35.244 --> 00:28:37.323
Thank you, Andrew, and on that very note,

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:40.740
it has been a couple of
months since we last met,

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:42.210
but I did want to start

00:28:42.210 --> 00:28:44.460
by talking about the
report published yesterday

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:45.510
by the Midlands team

00:28:45.510 --> 00:28:48.330
into the care and
treatment of Valdo Calocane

00:28:48.330 --> 00:28:52.440
by Nottinghamshire Healthcare
NHS Foundation Trust.

00:28:52.440 --> 00:28:55.260
The first thing to say
is that my thoughts,

00:28:55.260 --> 00:28:57.600
our thoughts continue
to be with the families

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:00.690
and loved ones of Ian
Coates, Grace O'Malley-Kumar,

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:03.480
and Barnaby Webber who have all suffered

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:07.380
just heartbreaking and
immeasurable personal loss.

00:29:07.380 --> 00:29:12.000
The report confirms that the
system including the NHS,

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:14.810
failed Valdo Calocane and therefore failed

00:29:14.810 --> 00:29:16.860
Ian, Grace, and Barnaby.

00:29:16.860 --> 00:29:20.430
And I want to add my full
apology to the families

00:29:20.430 --> 00:29:22.470
for the unacceptable failings

00:29:22.470 --> 00:29:25.470
by the NHS in this case.

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:28.530
The report helpfully sets out
a number of recommendations

00:29:28.530 --> 00:29:31.500
for the Trust, the ICB,
and for NHS England,

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:33.300
which we will be taking forward

00:29:33.300 --> 00:29:34.500
in addition to the actions

00:29:34.500 --> 00:29:36.990
that have already been taken
over the last 18 months

00:29:36.990 --> 00:29:40.020
to improve how those services work.

00:29:40.020 --> 00:29:41.580
But as ever, we are clear

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:43.530
that where there's
learning for one system,

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:46.020
there will be learning for others.

00:29:46.020 --> 00:29:49.290
So we've written to the
whole NHS to urge colleagues

00:29:49.290 --> 00:29:51.030
to read the report

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:53.340
and develop their own action plans

00:29:53.340 --> 00:29:55.800
with their local partners,
including the police,

00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:57.210
to better manage risk

00:29:57.210 --> 00:29:59.700
for patients with severe mental illness.

00:29:59.700 --> 00:30:01.170
Again, that's on top of actions.

00:30:01.170 --> 00:30:03.840
We've already taken an NHS-wide level,

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:06.506
particularly on how
services manage patients

00:30:06.506 --> 00:30:08.613
who disengage with care.

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:13.440
Lastly, it's worth saying
that we heard the concerns

00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:14.730
shared by families

00:30:14.730 --> 00:30:16.980
about the approach to
publishing this report,

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:21.180
and I apologise for any upset this caused.

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:23.220
When you're talking about
people's medical care,

00:30:23.220 --> 00:30:25.140
there are complex legal

00:30:25.140 --> 00:30:27.257
and confidentiality issues to navigate,

00:30:27.257 --> 00:30:31.470
especially in sharing more
widely than with the families.

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:33.870
So what this case reinforces
is the need for us

00:30:33.870 --> 00:30:36.330
to work with the Department
of Health and Social Care

00:30:36.330 --> 00:30:37.890
and partners to agree

00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:40.710
how future independent mental
health homicide reports

00:30:40.710 --> 00:30:42.030
should be published

00:30:42.030 --> 00:30:44.850
to ensure that we comply
with our legal obligations

00:30:44.850 --> 00:30:47.761
whilst also being
transparent and supportive

00:30:47.761 --> 00:30:51.033
with affected parties.

00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:53.790
I know, Richard, that's one
we're going to want to come back

00:30:53.790 --> 00:30:54.720
and talk about more,

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:56.700
but it's immediate-
- Yeah.

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:58.710
- with the reports only out immediately.

00:30:58.710 --> 00:31:02.550
We don't have a substantive
item on the board today.

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:04.750
No doubt we'll want to
for the next meeting.

00:31:06.120 --> 00:31:07.380
If I may move on,

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:09.390
the other thing, of
course, since we last met

00:31:09.390 --> 00:31:10.530
is we have faced,

00:31:10.530 --> 00:31:13.080
the NHS has faced a
very challenging winter

00:31:13.080 --> 00:31:15.000
where a particular impact felt from higher

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:18.570
than usual levels of
patients hospitalised by flu,

00:31:18.570 --> 00:31:21.930
Norovirus and RSV as well as COVID.

00:31:21.930 --> 00:31:23.861
So combined with discharge delays

00:31:23.861 --> 00:31:26.670
that has meant capacity
has been stretched,

00:31:26.670 --> 00:31:29.701
leading to flow issues
manifesting in trolley waits

00:31:29.701 --> 00:31:33.066
and ambulance handover delays.

00:31:33.066 --> 00:31:35.400
Our national and regional teams

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:36.780
have been working intensively

00:31:36.780 --> 00:31:39.600
with systems and providers
to tackle those issues.

00:31:39.600 --> 00:31:41.220
Again, thank you to them

00:31:41.220 --> 00:31:42.630
and to all of the hard work

00:31:42.630 --> 00:31:44.607
that colleagues have done across the NHS

00:31:44.607 --> 00:31:46.980
and I'm sure we'll discuss that work

00:31:46.980 --> 00:31:49.530
in the section on operational performance.

00:31:49.530 --> 00:31:51.559
But as I say, on behalf of the board,

00:31:51.559 --> 00:31:54.960
I do want to say a huge thank you

00:31:54.960 --> 00:31:57.450
to everyone who has been working so hard

00:31:57.450 --> 00:32:00.270
to deliver the safest possible care

00:32:00.270 --> 00:32:03.723
in some really very
challenging situations.

00:32:03.723 --> 00:32:05.880
Alongside that operational challenge

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:07.500
we've been working intensively

00:32:07.500 --> 00:32:08.760
over the past couple of months

00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:11.550
with the Department to
square the budget settlement

00:32:11.550 --> 00:32:14.460
and the wider impacts of the
chancellor's Autumn Budget

00:32:14.460 --> 00:32:17.310
with government priorities for delivery.

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:20.118
So firstly, that was to be able to update

00:32:20.118 --> 00:32:22.101
the system on in-year adjustments

00:32:22.101 --> 00:32:24.060
as we enter quarter four,

00:32:24.060 --> 00:32:26.280
and secondly to agree and publish

00:32:26.280 --> 00:32:27.360
the Operational Priorities

00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:30.150
and Planning Guidance for 2025/26,

00:32:30.150 --> 00:32:31.590
which as Richard says,

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:34.260
we did following the laying
of the government's mandate

00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:38.820
to NHS England and
Parliament last Thursday.

00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:41.085
I won't go through it all in detail here.

00:32:41.085 --> 00:32:42.972
We have discussed it as a board,

00:32:42.972 --> 00:32:45.480
but suffice to say that we've put

00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:47.520
a huge amount of work

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:49.380
into making this Planning Guidance

00:32:49.380 --> 00:32:51.450
look and feel markedly different

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:52.620
for the wider NHS

00:32:52.620 --> 00:32:55.830
as part of our broader direction
of travel towards systems,

00:32:55.830 --> 00:32:58.140
having a greater degree of autonomy

00:32:58.140 --> 00:33:00.987
alongside clear accountability

00:33:00.987 --> 00:33:02.850
and with support from NHS England

00:33:02.850 --> 00:33:05.910
within a clearer
operating model framework.

00:33:05.910 --> 00:33:08.091
So what does that mean in practise?

00:33:08.091 --> 00:33:10.410
Four things worth pulling out.

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:13.170
Firstly, it means giving
systems greater freedom

00:33:13.170 --> 00:33:14.790
to manage constrained budgets,

00:33:14.790 --> 00:33:17.190
transferring a higher
proportion of funding

00:33:17.190 --> 00:33:19.350
than ever before directly to local systems

00:33:19.350 --> 00:33:21.023
and minimising ring-fencing,

00:33:21.023 --> 00:33:23.444
allowing local leaders maximum flexibility

00:33:23.444 --> 00:33:27.600
to plan better and more
efficient services.

00:33:27.600 --> 00:33:30.270
Secondly, and reflecting
the mandate from government.

00:33:30.270 --> 00:33:33.600
We have significantly honed
down national priorities

00:33:33.600 --> 00:33:35.910
to increase local autonomy.

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:38.520
Thirdly, as we set out
in one of the annexes

00:33:38.520 --> 00:33:39.780
to planning guidance,

00:33:39.780 --> 00:33:43.530
we are streamlining the
planning process itself,

00:33:43.530 --> 00:33:45.300
providing more practical support,

00:33:45.300 --> 00:33:48.480
providing, you know,
asking for fewer returns

00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:51.120
and giving space for systems and providers

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:52.860
to engage more thoroughly with each other

00:33:52.860 --> 00:33:53.940
and their local partners

00:33:53.940 --> 00:33:56.580
to develop plans which are deliverable

00:33:56.580 --> 00:33:59.550
and which everyone is signed up to.

00:33:59.550 --> 00:34:03.202
And lastly, the annex I just
referenced also sets out

00:34:03.202 --> 00:34:05.400
that at the end of the planning process,

00:34:05.400 --> 00:34:08.010
we will agree a compact with systems

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:10.227
which sets out what each
organisation will do

00:34:10.227 --> 00:34:13.980
and the support they will get
from NHS England to deliver.

00:34:13.980 --> 00:34:15.210
Three other things, if I may,

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:17.853
just to mention in relation
to Planning Guidance.

00:34:18.720 --> 00:34:21.270
Firstly and quickly,

00:34:21.270 --> 00:34:23.801
we publish guidelines
to support local systems

00:34:23.801 --> 00:34:27.720
developing neighbourhood health services.

00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:29.070
So that work that this board

00:34:29.070 --> 00:34:30.780
and that you personally, Richard,

00:34:30.780 --> 00:34:33.330
have championed strongly
over your time as chair

00:34:33.330 --> 00:34:35.340
and there's an opportunity to discuss that

00:34:35.340 --> 00:34:37.200
more when we get to the agenda item

00:34:37.200 --> 00:34:40.135
on supporting people living with frailty.

00:34:40.135 --> 00:34:42.360
Secondly, key to the support offer

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:44.701
is our delivering on key pieces of work

00:34:44.701 --> 00:34:46.710
over the coming months.

00:34:46.710 --> 00:34:50.346
That includes the NHS Improvement
and Assessment Framework.

00:34:50.346 --> 00:34:53.610
But also our approach
to financial incentives

00:34:53.610 --> 00:34:55.379
for organisations and individuals,

00:34:55.379 --> 00:34:57.450
how we support our most capable leaders

00:34:57.450 --> 00:34:59.400
to take on challenged organisations

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:02.760
and more broadly how we upskill
colleagues at all levels

00:35:02.760 --> 00:35:06.480
to drive clinical and
operational improvement.

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:09.480
And thirdly, it's about what that means

00:35:09.480 --> 00:35:13.800
for NHS England as an organisation.

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:18.501
So couple more things
I wanted to talk about,

00:35:18.501 --> 00:35:20.040
but I do want to just
spend a moment, if I may,

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:21.233
on NHS England.
- Yeah.

00:35:22.470 --> 00:35:24.165
- So our last board meeting,

00:35:24.165 --> 00:35:27.750
I talked about how as we
develop a new operating model

00:35:27.750 --> 00:35:29.637
and give greater autonomy to systems,

00:35:29.637 --> 00:35:34.560
the role and size of NHS
England would reduce.

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:35.790
In the Planning Guidance,

00:35:35.790 --> 00:35:38.520
we are taking the next on that journey.

00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:39.540
But at the same time,

00:35:39.540 --> 00:35:42.397
we're asking the frontline
to make difficult choices

00:35:42.397 --> 00:35:45.690
about where they prioritise
finite resources.

00:35:45.690 --> 00:35:48.600
So you know, we should
not exempt ourselves

00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:50.550
from that same challenge.

00:35:50.550 --> 00:35:54.570
So it's with that sort of twin
ambition of both maximising,

00:35:54.570 --> 00:35:56.370
you talked about this
a moment ago, Richard,

00:35:56.370 --> 00:35:58.470
but maximising the amount of NHS funding

00:35:58.470 --> 00:36:00.180
which reaches the frontline

00:36:00.180 --> 00:36:03.347
and continuing to change our organisation

00:36:03.347 --> 00:36:05.790
so that we are more clearly focused

00:36:05.790 --> 00:36:08.190
on where we add value.

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:09.787
So in line with our operating model

00:36:09.787 --> 00:36:12.240
and where we support frontline delivery

00:36:12.240 --> 00:36:15.390
in line with the government's priorities.

00:36:15.390 --> 00:36:17.490
So what that means in practise

00:36:17.490 --> 00:36:19.410
is that we're going to
be taking a good look

00:36:19.410 --> 00:36:22.650
at some of our functions and
some of the ways that we work

00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:26.790
as part of our planning
process for this year.

00:36:26.790 --> 00:36:29.640
And it means we are setting out explicitly

00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:32.120
with the aim of delivering more savings

00:36:32.120 --> 00:36:33.900
to support the frontline

00:36:33.900 --> 00:36:34.950
over the coming year.

00:36:34.950 --> 00:36:36.885
And that's on top of
the recurring benefits

00:36:36.885 --> 00:36:38.790
from the new NHS England programme.

00:36:38.790 --> 00:36:40.950
And we've set ourselves the ambition

00:36:40.950 --> 00:36:44.358
of saving 150 million of non-pay resources

00:36:44.358 --> 00:36:46.320
and reducing our staffing costs

00:36:46.320 --> 00:36:48.281
by another 175 million.

00:36:48.281 --> 00:36:51.470
So that is the equivalent of about 15%

00:36:51.470 --> 00:36:53.793
of the new NHS England structure.

00:36:54.720 --> 00:36:56.320
On our staff call earlier this week,

00:36:56.320 --> 00:36:57.930
Steve and I acknowledged

00:36:57.930 --> 00:37:01.141
that this comes really pretty quickly

00:37:01.141 --> 00:37:03.900
after the new NHS England programme,

00:37:03.900 --> 00:37:05.790
which was a very challenging process

00:37:05.790 --> 00:37:07.890
for lots of us and lots of our colleagues.

00:37:09.060 --> 00:37:10.830
And we said, I think explicitly,

00:37:10.830 --> 00:37:14.550
we absolutely recognise
change can be tough

00:37:14.550 --> 00:37:16.920
even when it's for the right reasons.

00:37:16.920 --> 00:37:20.400
And fundamentally, we are all
here to make a real difference

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:22.800
where it matters most for patients.

00:37:22.800 --> 00:37:24.810
But all of us, I think
around this board table

00:37:24.810 --> 00:37:26.010
and certainly in the exec group,

00:37:26.010 --> 00:37:28.350
are very clear we need to do that at pace

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:30.360
so that we can give people clarity.

00:37:30.360 --> 00:37:32.400
And that's what we'll be
doing in the coming weeks

00:37:32.400 --> 00:37:34.680
with national and regional
directors working together

00:37:34.680 --> 00:37:37.860
to identify a first
draft of which functions

00:37:37.860 --> 00:37:40.117
we should make targeted changes to

00:37:40.117 --> 00:37:40.950
'cause that's a crucial point here.

00:37:40.950 --> 00:37:42.518
It's got to be targeted

00:37:42.518 --> 00:37:45.240
in order to deliver the
ambitions I've set out.

00:37:45.240 --> 00:37:47.910
So we can then discuss
as a group after that,

00:37:47.910 --> 00:37:50.740
and then give directorates and teams

00:37:51.720 --> 00:37:53.550
feedback as quickly as we can

00:37:53.550 --> 00:37:56.190
about the broad shape of those plans.

00:37:56.190 --> 00:37:57.023
So in the meantime,

00:37:57.023 --> 00:37:58.530
we're going to give
everyone the opportunity

00:37:58.530 --> 00:37:59.940
to share their thoughts and ideas

00:37:59.940 --> 00:38:03.090
on where NHS England adds
to the most value to the NHS

00:38:03.090 --> 00:38:04.980
and where it can be strengthened further,

00:38:04.980 --> 00:38:06.690
but also where we add less value

00:38:06.690 --> 00:38:08.160
and where we duplicate work

00:38:08.160 --> 00:38:10.320
and where there are
things that we need to do

00:38:10.320 --> 00:38:13.710
in a different and more
effective and efficient way.

00:38:13.710 --> 00:38:15.600
So we will, and I think again,

00:38:15.600 --> 00:38:18.630
Steve and I told colleagues
this very explicitly

00:38:18.630 --> 00:38:20.461
when we were on the
call earlier this week,

00:38:20.461 --> 00:38:24.450
but we will continue the
important work on our culture.

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:26.850
So we're not pulling that back at all

00:38:26.850 --> 00:38:28.620
and we're continuing to do the work

00:38:28.620 --> 00:38:30.867
on developing our internal ways of working

00:38:30.867 --> 00:38:33.540
and also on developing people skills,

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:35.313
so alongside that change.

00:38:36.300 --> 00:38:39.548
Other developments, I promise
I'm on the home straight now,

00:38:39.548 --> 00:38:41.459
but I did just want to briefly mention

00:38:41.459 --> 00:38:44.610
a few other developments
since we last met.

00:38:44.610 --> 00:38:46.380
So you've referenced it already, Richard,

00:38:46.380 --> 00:38:47.580
but around a month ago,

00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:50.040
we launched the elective reform plan,

00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:52.349
setting out the interventions
agreed with government

00:38:52.349 --> 00:38:55.950
which will contribute to
meeting the manifesto commitment

00:38:55.950 --> 00:38:57.101
and priority to return

00:38:57.101 --> 00:38:59.670
to the constitutional standard

00:38:59.670 --> 00:39:02.412
on 18 weeks by 2029.

00:39:02.412 --> 00:39:07.200
That was a very important moment for us

00:39:07.200 --> 00:39:09.480
because I think it encapsulated
what I said earlier

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:11.835
about really wanting both to build

00:39:11.835 --> 00:39:13.720
on what's working really well

00:39:13.720 --> 00:39:15.930
in some parts of the NHS,

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:18.652
but also a real commitment
and evidence of us

00:39:18.652 --> 00:39:21.210
now taking those important first steps

00:39:21.210 --> 00:39:24.080
in really transformational change.

00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:26.430
Earlier this week on World Cancer Day,

00:39:26.430 --> 00:39:28.230
the Department also launched a call

00:39:28.230 --> 00:39:29.250
for views from the public

00:39:29.250 --> 00:39:32.070
on priorities for a new cancer plan,

00:39:32.070 --> 00:39:35.040
which is due to be
published later this year.

00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:38.910
Again, I just wanted to share
with the board at this point

00:39:38.910 --> 00:39:40.500
that Dame Cally Palmer,

00:39:40.500 --> 00:39:43.920
who is the National Cancer
Director at NHS England

00:39:43.920 --> 00:39:45.570
has announced to her team

00:39:45.570 --> 00:39:48.090
that she will be stepping
back from the role

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:51.000
after nearly a decade of service.

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:52.440
Cally's led the implementation

00:39:52.440 --> 00:39:53.790
of a number of previous plans

00:39:53.790 --> 00:39:56.340
and been absolutely
pivotal in transforming

00:39:56.340 --> 00:39:59.010
and accelerating cancer care in England.

00:39:59.010 --> 00:40:01.560
At all times, she's
ensure we focus as much

00:40:01.560 --> 00:40:06.540
on how those living with and
beyond cancer experience care

00:40:06.540 --> 00:40:10.350
as well as how the NHS
can deliver more of it.

00:40:10.350 --> 00:40:13.230
And on her watch, the NHS has delivered

00:40:13.230 --> 00:40:15.210
a huge expansion in the number

00:40:15.210 --> 00:40:17.430
of urgent cancer checks
delivered every month,

00:40:17.430 --> 00:40:21.090
contributing to more cancers
being caught at an early stage

00:40:21.090 --> 00:40:25.110
and the highest cancer survival
rates in England's history.

00:40:25.110 --> 00:40:27.690
Loads more to do and we're really welcome

00:40:27.690 --> 00:40:30.840
the launch now of that
to call for evidence

00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:34.350
and the commitment to a
new National Cancer Plan.

00:40:34.350 --> 00:40:36.520
But we're immensely grateful
for the contribution

00:40:36.520 --> 00:40:38.430
that Cally has made nationally,

00:40:38.430 --> 00:40:39.960
and of course, will continue to make

00:40:39.960 --> 00:40:42.003
as chief executive of the Royal Marsden.

00:40:43.260 --> 00:40:45.360
And lastly, I wanted to highlight a couple

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:47.760
of the announcements we've made recently

00:40:47.760 --> 00:40:51.643
in case people hadn't fully clocked them.

00:40:51.643 --> 00:40:52.828
The first is the fact

00:40:52.828 --> 00:40:55.200
that the NHS Jewish
BRCA Testing Programme,

00:40:55.200 --> 00:40:56.850
which was launched two years ago,

00:40:56.850 --> 00:41:01.770
has now processed around
11,000 at-home saliva tests

00:41:01.770 --> 00:41:05.110
with 235 people testing positive

00:41:05.110 --> 00:41:06.840
for a BRCA gene mutation,

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:08.790
which increases the risk of breast,

00:41:08.790 --> 00:41:10.650
ovarian, and prostate cancer,

00:41:10.650 --> 00:41:12.630
enabling them to seek early access

00:41:12.630 --> 00:41:15.420
to screening and preventative options;

00:41:15.420 --> 00:41:18.510
and of course, thousands
of others given reassurance

00:41:18.510 --> 00:41:22.140
that they do not give
carry the genetic risk.

00:41:22.140 --> 00:41:24.000
And secondly, we were able to announce

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:25.380
that we reached a deal

00:41:25.380 --> 00:41:28.560
to make a revolutionary gene
editing treatment available

00:41:28.560 --> 00:41:31.710
for people with a severe
form of sickle-cell disease,

00:41:31.710 --> 00:41:35.340
which trials concluded
provides a functional cure

00:41:35.340 --> 00:41:38.490
for more than 96% of patients.

00:41:38.490 --> 00:41:39.600
And I share those

00:41:39.600 --> 00:41:41.130
because they're a good reminder

00:41:41.130 --> 00:41:42.840
of the impact of the work we do here

00:41:42.840 --> 00:41:44.490
on behalf of the NHS

00:41:44.490 --> 00:41:47.232
that brings real benefits to patients

00:41:47.232 --> 00:41:50.580
and examples of some of the
really transformational things

00:41:50.580 --> 00:41:54.750
that we can and are still
really ambitious about

00:41:54.750 --> 00:41:55.583
for the future.

00:41:55.583 --> 00:41:57.327
And I know we'll be hearing
a lot more about that

00:41:57.327 --> 00:41:59.550
later on when we get onto the presentation

00:41:59.550 --> 00:42:01.413
on the NHS Genomic Medicine Service.

00:42:02.490 --> 00:42:03.363
- Amanda, thank you.

00:42:03.363 --> 00:42:05.520
Are there any comments, questions,

00:42:05.520 --> 00:42:07.840
reflections to Amanda, Wol?

00:42:07.840 --> 00:42:09.330
- Well, just do one.

00:42:09.330 --> 00:42:10.500
Would you probably expect me to comment

00:42:10.500 --> 00:42:13.410
on the further restructuring

00:42:13.410 --> 00:42:15.270
that's going to be going on.

00:42:15.270 --> 00:42:17.730
You talk about the support

00:42:17.730 --> 00:42:19.710
and ongoing work around culture,

00:42:19.710 --> 00:42:21.030
which is one of the sort of bits

00:42:21.030 --> 00:42:22.920
of unfinished business,
- Yeah.

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:26.504
- that we left post the
new NHS England Programme.

00:42:26.504 --> 00:42:27.600
That's the first thing,

00:42:27.600 --> 00:42:29.894
just sort of maybe amplify that a bit more

00:42:29.894 --> 00:42:30.810
because I think that's going
to be really important,

00:42:30.810 --> 00:42:34.140
giving colleagues reassurance
that has been done.

00:42:34.140 --> 00:42:35.850
The second thing is that, again,

00:42:35.850 --> 00:42:38.343
if we're going to reduce
numbers even further,

00:42:39.570 --> 00:42:41.850
then just giving colleagues the assurance

00:42:41.850 --> 00:42:45.030
that it's not about
squeezing more out of them,

00:42:45.030 --> 00:42:46.960
it's about what are we
not going to be doing?

00:42:46.960 --> 00:42:48.403
- Yeah, yeah
- Thank you.

00:42:48.403 --> 00:42:50.113
- Thank you, Wol. I'll let Steve

00:42:50.113 --> 00:42:51.750
come in on the culture work

00:42:51.750 --> 00:42:54.090
'cause Steve's been leading
that within the organisation.

00:42:54.090 --> 00:42:55.890
But just to say that
has actually come across

00:42:55.890 --> 00:42:58.500
really strongly even
from initial feedback.

00:42:58.500 --> 00:43:00.462
And I think we are absolutely clear

00:43:00.462 --> 00:43:01.997
that that is what we're
talking about here.

00:43:01.997 --> 00:43:04.500
We are talking about stopping stuff,

00:43:04.500 --> 00:43:07.350
not trying to just have fewer people

00:43:07.350 --> 00:43:10.710
do exactly the same
amount of work, but Steve?

00:43:10.710 --> 00:43:12.200
- Thanks Amanda and thanks Wol.

00:43:12.200 --> 00:43:14.460
So that you'll remember

00:43:14.460 --> 00:43:16.020
partly coming out of the
NHS England committee

00:43:16.020 --> 00:43:18.150
and then into the People
and Remuneration Committee,

00:43:18.150 --> 00:43:20.280
we developed the OD plan

00:43:20.280 --> 00:43:21.900
for the first couple of years

00:43:21.900 --> 00:43:25.200
with a heavy focus on
developing line managers,

00:43:25.200 --> 00:43:28.920
so new sets of appraisal
tools, giving feedback,

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:31.917
and a four-module line
manager development programme

00:43:31.917 --> 00:43:34.980
over the course of about
six or seven months.

00:43:34.980 --> 00:43:36.750
Module one of that has run,

00:43:36.750 --> 00:43:41.130
it was run up to just after
the beginning of January.

00:43:41.130 --> 00:43:42.450
Module two is about to start

00:43:42.450 --> 00:43:44.190
and we've said that we're working

00:43:44.190 --> 00:43:45.630
with an external partner on that.

00:43:45.630 --> 00:43:46.950
We've said that we'll continue

00:43:46.950 --> 00:43:48.360
alongside all of our other work

00:43:48.360 --> 00:43:50.970
on development and inclusion and so on.

00:43:50.970 --> 00:43:52.890
So we're not changing the plan,

00:43:52.890 --> 00:43:54.150
it's kind of steady as you go

00:43:54.150 --> 00:43:55.473
and we keep going with it.

00:43:57.540 --> 00:43:59.853
- Any other comments, reflections?

00:44:00.900 --> 00:44:04.200
Okay, so I'm going to
move us on in the agenda

00:44:04.200 --> 00:44:05.220
and when we look at the agenda,

00:44:05.220 --> 00:44:08.310
we can clearly see that we're
going to have the update,

00:44:08.310 --> 00:44:11.460
the normal update from Emily

00:44:11.460 --> 00:44:13.901
and from Julian on finance.

00:44:13.901 --> 00:44:15.900
We're going to look at NHS productivity,

00:44:15.900 --> 00:44:18.030
children and young people update.

00:44:18.030 --> 00:44:19.470
We've got a presentation

00:44:19.470 --> 00:44:21.960
on the Genomics medicine Service, Sue.

00:44:21.960 --> 00:44:23.550
And also we're going to have a look

00:44:23.550 --> 00:44:26.730
at transforming the Frailty Pathway.

00:44:26.730 --> 00:44:28.620
And then, there's some
delegations we're going to do

00:44:28.620 --> 00:44:30.150
around some of the commissioning functions

00:44:30.150 --> 00:44:33.210
which are further important evolution

00:44:33.210 --> 00:44:34.856
or delegation, sorry,

00:44:34.856 --> 00:44:36.540
into the ICBs

00:44:36.540 --> 00:44:39.570
away from NHS England.

00:44:39.570 --> 00:44:42.000
And this morning's
board, the private board,

00:44:42.000 --> 00:44:43.890
we also had very good reports

00:44:43.890 --> 00:44:47.250
from each of the committees that oversee

00:44:47.250 --> 00:44:50.038
the underlying work of
the NHS England board.

00:44:50.038 --> 00:44:52.980
So Digital Data and Technology Committee,

00:44:52.980 --> 00:44:54.750
Workforce, Training and Education group,

00:44:54.750 --> 00:44:56.400
the Quality Group,

00:44:56.400 --> 00:44:59.850
the CFO Advisory Group,

00:44:59.850 --> 00:45:01.980
and then People and
Remuneration committee.

00:45:01.980 --> 00:45:04.131
So we had those updates

00:45:04.131 --> 00:45:06.974
showing actually the
substantial work underway

00:45:06.974 --> 00:45:09.495
in the intervening period

00:45:09.495 --> 00:45:14.100
between when the board itself meets.

00:45:14.100 --> 00:45:16.710
We also had an in-depth discussion,

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:18.000
Julian, led by you,

00:45:18.000 --> 00:45:19.050
actually looking forward

00:45:19.050 --> 00:45:24.050
into next year's financial
position and planning

00:45:24.240 --> 00:45:27.210
and also the planning
guidelines and the interaction.

00:45:27.210 --> 00:45:30.600
So let's get on with this
afternoon's public board agenda.

00:45:30.600 --> 00:45:32.580
So Emily, can I hand to you

00:45:32.580 --> 00:45:35.280
for an operational performance update?

00:45:35.280 --> 00:45:37.110
- Thank you, Chair.

00:45:37.110 --> 00:45:38.940
So today, I'll take you

00:45:38.940 --> 00:45:41.490
through our performance
across elective recovery,

00:45:41.490 --> 00:45:43.710
cancer diagnostics and mental health.

00:45:43.710 --> 00:45:45.510
I'll turn to Sarah-Jane for an update

00:45:45.510 --> 00:45:46.830
on UEC in winter

00:45:46.830 --> 00:45:48.623
before just offering
one thought in closing

00:45:48.623 --> 00:45:50.223
before we go to questions.

00:45:51.300 --> 00:45:53.430
I think my key message
as with I think probably

00:45:53.430 --> 00:45:56.160
the last four boards now,
I'm trying to remember,

00:45:56.160 --> 00:45:59.883
is as Steve and Amanda
have already reflected,

00:46:00.870 --> 00:46:03.336
the NHS is doing extraordinary
things in delivery every day

00:46:03.336 --> 00:46:05.527
and looking to improve
services and innovate.

00:46:05.527 --> 00:46:08.484
And we have huge distances still to cover

00:46:08.484 --> 00:46:10.890
to make sure we are absolutely delivering

00:46:10.890 --> 00:46:12.630
the services we would like to deliver

00:46:12.630 --> 00:46:15.000
at the quality we'd like
to deliver every day.

00:46:15.000 --> 00:46:16.200
So I'll try and pull out

00:46:16.200 --> 00:46:19.096
some of those challenges as I go through.

00:46:19.096 --> 00:46:21.687
Firstly on elective care,

00:46:21.687 --> 00:46:24.990
the good news is that the
total wait list is reducing.

00:46:24.990 --> 00:46:26.370
Between October and November,

00:46:26.370 --> 00:46:30.660
it fell by over 61,000
patients to 7.48 million,

00:46:30.660 --> 00:46:34.770
which is lower than the 7.6
million this time last year.

00:46:34.770 --> 00:46:35.787
Long waits are reducing

00:46:35.787 --> 00:46:38.550
and we continue to see
improvements month on month.

00:46:38.550 --> 00:46:41.850
For example, 78-week waits
from October to November

00:46:41.850 --> 00:46:45.456
were down 16% to 2051

00:46:45.456 --> 00:46:47.610
and 52-week waits were down

00:46:47.610 --> 00:46:51.960
five and a half percent to 221,889.

00:46:51.960 --> 00:46:54.132
But as you can tell from those numbers,

00:46:54.132 --> 00:46:57.090
we need to continue to
innovate and improve.

00:46:57.090 --> 00:46:59.730
And of course, as signalled
in the elective reform plan

00:46:59.730 --> 00:47:01.140
and in planning guidance,

00:47:01.140 --> 00:47:04.500
the system is now pivoting
to focus on the target

00:47:04.500 --> 00:47:06.616
to return to the constitutional standard

00:47:06.616 --> 00:47:10.110
on referral to treatment by 2029

00:47:10.110 --> 00:47:12.210
and the target for '25/26

00:47:12.210 --> 00:47:14.071
to deliver 65% of patients waiting

00:47:14.071 --> 00:47:16.381
no longer than 18 weeks for treatment

00:47:16.381 --> 00:47:19.020
by March '26.

00:47:19.020 --> 00:47:21.150
So our regional teams in particular,

00:47:21.150 --> 00:47:23.100
supported by the national programme teams,

00:47:23.100 --> 00:47:24.720
are working trust by trust,

00:47:24.720 --> 00:47:26.700
specialty by specialty

00:47:26.700 --> 00:47:29.310
to drive delivery through
targeted interventions

00:47:29.310 --> 00:47:31.080
that improve patient experience

00:47:31.080 --> 00:47:33.108
and deliver the timely high quality care

00:47:33.108 --> 00:47:35.493
that is our collective ambition.

00:47:36.600 --> 00:47:39.630
On cancer, all three
of our cancer standards

00:47:39.630 --> 00:47:40.980
have improved in November

00:47:40.980 --> 00:47:42.750
when comparing year on year.

00:47:42.750 --> 00:47:46.020
The faster diagnostic standard hit 77.4%,

00:47:46.020 --> 00:47:47.760
which exceeded the target

00:47:47.760 --> 00:47:50.070
and is up 5.6 percentage points

00:47:50.070 --> 00:47:54.330
from 71.8% in November the previous year.

00:47:54.330 --> 00:47:57.236
62-day performance improved to 69.4%,

00:47:57.236 --> 00:48:01.740
which is a 3.9% percentage
point increase on last year

00:48:01.740 --> 00:48:05.820
and is on track to meet the
70% target by March 2025,

00:48:05.820 --> 00:48:09.960
on the way to recovering the
constitutional standard of 85%.

00:48:09.960 --> 00:48:11.910
31-day treatment standards

00:48:11.910 --> 00:48:14.160
is effectively stable at 91%,

00:48:14.160 --> 00:48:17.410
which is up from 90.2%
in the previous November

00:48:18.630 --> 00:48:21.540
On diagnostics, we are
delivering more tests than ever.

00:48:21.540 --> 00:48:24.090
Nearly 2.44 million diagnostic tests

00:48:24.090 --> 00:48:25.740
were carried out in November '24,

00:48:25.740 --> 00:48:30.000
which is up from 2.38 million in October.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:33.990
The waiting list for diagnostic
tests is now 1.615 million

00:48:33.990 --> 00:48:35.520
with six-week wait performance

00:48:35.520 --> 00:48:37.081
improving to 19.9%

00:48:37.081 --> 00:48:39.900
from 23.3% in November '23,

00:48:39.900 --> 00:48:43.143
which is the best
position since March 2020.

00:48:43.980 --> 00:48:46.320
But we have to maintain the pace on this.

00:48:46.320 --> 00:48:48.720
We are, in diagnostics,

00:48:48.720 --> 00:48:51.681
barely keeping pace with demand growth.

00:48:51.681 --> 00:48:54.150
And as the board will be well aware,

00:48:54.150 --> 00:48:56.130
diagnostics underpin elective recovery

00:48:56.130 --> 00:48:58.140
including cancer diagnosis.

00:48:58.140 --> 00:48:59.940
So we're working closely with systems

00:48:59.940 --> 00:49:01.560
through the Planning Guidance and beyond

00:49:01.560 --> 00:49:04.710
to sustain high levels
of productive activity

00:49:04.710 --> 00:49:07.194
and to make sure that the
considerable additional capacity

00:49:07.194 --> 00:49:09.300
that has been funded is being used

00:49:09.300 --> 00:49:12.123
to best and most effective
effect for patients.

00:49:13.200 --> 00:49:14.070
On mental health,

00:49:14.070 --> 00:49:16.170
I think the summary would
be we are continuing

00:49:16.170 --> 00:49:19.410
to expand access but pressures remain.

00:49:19.410 --> 00:49:22.050
On perinatal and maternal
mental health services,

00:49:22.050 --> 00:49:26.340
for example, we saw a 12.9%
increase from last year

00:49:26.340 --> 00:49:28.695
to 55,561, sorry,

00:49:28.695 --> 00:49:33.695
or 55,561 in November '23.

00:49:35.340 --> 00:49:37.676
We saw for children, young
people accessing services

00:49:37.676 --> 00:49:40.830
that was up 6.4% year on year.

00:49:40.830 --> 00:49:42.570
Although we are really conscious

00:49:42.570 --> 00:49:44.159
that that's mixed across pathways.

00:49:44.159 --> 00:49:47.730
So the children, young people's
eating disorder pathways

00:49:47.730 --> 00:49:49.397
continue to be challenged.

00:49:49.397 --> 00:49:50.580
So for example,

00:49:50.580 --> 00:49:52.813
83.3% of urgent referrals

00:49:52.813 --> 00:49:54.991
were seen within one week.

00:49:54.991 --> 00:49:57.121
That is an improvement from October,

00:49:57.121 --> 00:49:59.769
but still a way short of the 95% access

00:49:59.769 --> 00:50:02.555
in waiting time standard.

00:50:02.555 --> 00:50:05.580
And finally, on an area,

00:50:05.580 --> 00:50:07.140
enables me to transition to Sarah-Jane,

00:50:07.140 --> 00:50:08.610
mental health 12-hour waits

00:50:08.610 --> 00:50:10.860
in A&amp;E remain too high

00:50:10.860 --> 00:50:12.990
with around one in five
mental health patients

00:50:12.990 --> 00:50:15.990
experiencing a long wait in A&amp;E.

00:50:15.990 --> 00:50:18.069
So with on that note,
Sarah-Jane, let me turn to you

00:50:18.069 --> 00:50:19.380
for an update on winter,

00:50:19.380 --> 00:50:20.607
and then I'll finish off.

00:50:20.607 --> 00:50:21.990
- Thank you, Emily.

00:50:21.990 --> 00:50:25.050
Yeah, so as Amanda and
Emily have both referred to,

00:50:25.050 --> 00:50:26.370
it's been a challenging period

00:50:26.370 --> 00:50:27.600
for all of our colleagues, I think,

00:50:27.600 --> 00:50:29.193
across health and social care.

00:50:31.170 --> 00:50:33.420
Obviously, the winter
respiratory virus peak

00:50:33.420 --> 00:50:34.253
comes every year,

00:50:34.253 --> 00:50:36.780
but we are never exactly
sure when that's going to be.

00:50:36.780 --> 00:50:39.960
And this time, it came
to perfectly coincide

00:50:39.960 --> 00:50:42.720
with the period between
Christmas and New Year,

00:50:42.720 --> 00:50:44.514
which I think added to some

00:50:44.514 --> 00:50:46.680
of the challenges that we faced.

00:50:46.680 --> 00:50:49.250
At its peak, we saw 5,800 patients

00:50:49.250 --> 00:50:51.690
in hospital with flu

00:50:51.690 --> 00:50:52.770
and the highest figure

00:50:52.770 --> 00:50:56.250
that we'd been at in the
previous year was 2,500.

00:50:56.250 --> 00:50:59.357
So it was a shorter, sharper peak

00:50:59.357 --> 00:51:02.610
that brought with a huge
range of challenges.

00:51:02.610 --> 00:51:05.640
At the same time, we also
had significant Norovirus,

00:51:05.640 --> 00:51:09.870
and in fact, that is continuing
now to be a challenge

00:51:09.870 --> 00:51:12.390
for many of our clinical teams.

00:51:12.390 --> 00:51:16.350
The response of everyone in
the system was remarkable,

00:51:16.350 --> 00:51:18.413
clinical and operational leadership.

00:51:18.413 --> 00:51:20.190
Though we were in the holiday period,

00:51:20.190 --> 00:51:22.564
it did feel for many of our teams

00:51:22.564 --> 00:51:24.420
right across NHS and Care,

00:51:24.420 --> 00:51:25.770
there was little time for holiday

00:51:25.770 --> 00:51:28.080
because everybody was all hands on deck

00:51:28.080 --> 00:51:31.560
really working throughout
to keep patients safe.

00:51:31.560 --> 00:51:34.050
And we used system level,

00:51:34.050 --> 00:51:36.210
our system gold meetings

00:51:36.210 --> 00:51:39.120
and proactive use of critical instance

00:51:39.120 --> 00:51:41.100
for short periods of time

00:51:41.100 --> 00:51:42.854
to help organisations and systems

00:51:42.854 --> 00:51:44.700
to absolutely prioritise

00:51:44.700 --> 00:51:47.760
on that decongestion of the front door

00:51:47.760 --> 00:51:50.223
and be able to get services flowing again.

00:51:51.150 --> 00:51:54.270
I think some of the things I
would just like to point to

00:51:54.270 --> 00:51:58.279
as things that I think
went quite well for us.

00:51:58.279 --> 00:51:59.880
And then, I'll just say a few things

00:51:59.880 --> 00:52:02.190
about the challenges before
handing back to Emily.

00:52:02.190 --> 00:52:06.093
I think the things that worked
particularly well were 111.

00:52:07.080 --> 00:52:08.640
Normally over a winter period,

00:52:08.640 --> 00:52:10.980
we often hear concerns raised about 111.

00:52:10.980 --> 00:52:12.990
It held up remarkably well.

00:52:12.990 --> 00:52:17.727
It did an additional
267,959 clinical assessments

00:52:19.879 --> 00:52:21.867
compared to the previous year.

00:52:21.867 --> 00:52:23.790
And we know that's massively helpful

00:52:23.790 --> 00:52:26.790
because if people get that
conversation with a clinician,

00:52:26.790 --> 00:52:30.104
they are more reassured about
the next steps in their care.

00:52:30.104 --> 00:52:32.624
That meant that only 3.5% of people

00:52:32.624 --> 00:52:36.270
that contacted 111 over the period

00:52:36.270 --> 00:52:38.130
abandoned their call

00:52:38.130 --> 00:52:43.130
compared to 10.5% in 2023

00:52:43.260 --> 00:52:47.880
and 40.8% in December 2022.

00:52:47.880 --> 00:52:49.620
If 111 works well,

00:52:49.620 --> 00:52:50.970
that can become the bedrock

00:52:50.970 --> 00:52:53.190
of how the rest of the
system then builds around it.

00:52:53.190 --> 00:52:54.570
So I think we need to acknowledge

00:52:54.570 --> 00:52:56.850
that that was a really great performance

00:52:56.850 --> 00:52:58.530
from a range of services.

00:52:58.530 --> 00:53:01.920
We also saw a huge step up
from our ambulance services

00:53:01.920 --> 00:53:05.130
in terms of the delivery
of the left shift.

00:53:05.130 --> 00:53:06.840
So using hear and treat

00:53:06.840 --> 00:53:08.670
and see and treat really effectively

00:53:08.670 --> 00:53:10.857
to not take patients to hospital

00:53:10.857 --> 00:53:13.050
unless it was absolutely necessary.

00:53:13.050 --> 00:53:15.480
And part, Chair, you quoted

00:53:15.480 --> 00:53:19.350
the over 7% increase in incidents

00:53:19.350 --> 00:53:21.660
and part of the way, we
were able to manage that

00:53:21.660 --> 00:53:23.700
was to give that advice
either over the phone

00:53:23.700 --> 00:53:25.680
or face-to-face in people's homes

00:53:25.680 --> 00:53:29.040
and not need to take all of
those patients into hospital.

00:53:29.040 --> 00:53:32.190
We also managed to get our
bed occupancy down to 92%.

00:53:32.190 --> 00:53:34.177
We set ourselves that as our standard

00:53:34.177 --> 00:53:36.270
and we achieved it in the days

00:53:36.270 --> 00:53:38.010
leading up to Christmas Eve

00:53:38.010 --> 00:53:42.150
and we had more of G&amp;A beds
open over the period as well.

00:53:42.150 --> 00:53:43.477
And I think the thing we know

00:53:43.477 --> 00:53:48.000
from when we talk to
patients and families,

00:53:48.000 --> 00:53:49.860
that the most important thing of all

00:53:49.860 --> 00:53:51.453
that we are able to do in UEC

00:53:51.453 --> 00:53:55.020
is to bring an ambulance
when you really need one.

00:53:55.020 --> 00:53:56.408
And although we did have a couple

00:53:56.408 --> 00:53:59.310
of individually challenging days,

00:53:59.310 --> 00:54:01.050
we had two individual days

00:54:01.050 --> 00:54:03.630
where our performance went
over an hour for category two,

00:54:03.630 --> 00:54:06.900
which is a significant
improvement on previous years.

00:54:06.900 --> 00:54:09.150
And although we did have a
couple of challenging weeks

00:54:09.150 --> 00:54:11.400
when we were in the sort of 40 minutes,

00:54:11.400 --> 00:54:15.371
we really were able to recover
our performance quickly

00:54:15.371 --> 00:54:19.200
and get back down to
around the 36, 37 minutes

00:54:19.200 --> 00:54:21.411
that we are running to at present,

00:54:21.411 --> 00:54:24.914
which is still too long

00:54:24.914 --> 00:54:27.210
but, I think, is
something that we all feel

00:54:27.210 --> 00:54:29.610
is a priority to focus on.

00:54:29.610 --> 00:54:32.160
Part of the way that we
delivered that though

00:54:32.160 --> 00:54:35.964
was to really be very flexible

00:54:35.964 --> 00:54:38.220
about the way that some of the front doors

00:54:38.220 --> 00:54:40.680
of our organisations worked

00:54:40.680 --> 00:54:43.335
and lots of hospitals were very, very full

00:54:43.335 --> 00:54:45.360
in the emergency departments,

00:54:45.360 --> 00:54:48.090
in the medical assessment
units, in the wards;

00:54:48.090 --> 00:54:49.950
and unfortunately in instances

00:54:49.950 --> 00:54:52.470
in temporary escalation spaces.

00:54:52.470 --> 00:54:54.755
And whilst our four-hour performance

00:54:54.755 --> 00:54:57.330
continued to be better than last year,

00:54:57.330 --> 00:54:59.730
our 12-hour performance is the thing

00:54:59.730 --> 00:55:01.860
that we really need to
focus all of our attention.

00:55:01.860 --> 00:55:06.030
And I think as we go into the year ahead,

00:55:06.030 --> 00:55:07.750
I think we know for
some of those patients,

00:55:07.750 --> 00:55:11.250
it's not only the waiting
which nobody wants to do,

00:55:11.250 --> 00:55:12.360
but the experience of doing

00:55:12.360 --> 00:55:15.480
that in temporary escalation
spaces can be poor.

00:55:15.480 --> 00:55:18.766
And Duncan and team put out
some guidance to systems,

00:55:18.766 --> 00:55:21.420
which I know lots of people found helpful,

00:55:21.420 --> 00:55:23.160
but I think we've still
got more work to do there

00:55:23.160 --> 00:55:25.664
to absolutely ensure that we maintain

00:55:25.664 --> 00:55:27.902
the safety and dignity of patients

00:55:27.902 --> 00:55:29.970
if we are not able to admit them

00:55:29.970 --> 00:55:31.980
in a timely fashion.

00:55:31.980 --> 00:55:34.768
So hopefully, we are
through the other side.

00:55:34.768 --> 00:55:37.317
We have weekly meetings with the UK HSA

00:55:37.317 --> 00:55:39.210
and the signs are looking positive.

00:55:39.210 --> 00:55:42.210
We still have got some Norovirus,

00:55:42.210 --> 00:55:43.890
colleagues have also mentioned COVID.

00:55:43.890 --> 00:55:46.380
We can't see any signs of
it coming at the moment,

00:55:46.380 --> 00:55:48.294
but we never know.

00:55:48.294 --> 00:55:51.108
So we may see a COVID spike

00:55:51.108 --> 00:55:53.430
over the course of the
next couple of months,

00:55:53.430 --> 00:55:56.190
but we are now moving into
our lessons learnt phase,

00:55:56.190 --> 00:55:58.980
so quickly working with our systems

00:55:58.980 --> 00:56:01.920
and our regions to understand
the things that went well,

00:56:01.920 --> 00:56:03.750
the things that we could have done better

00:56:03.750 --> 00:56:05.820
and that we're able to capture those.

00:56:05.820 --> 00:56:07.050
We've already partly done that

00:56:07.050 --> 00:56:08.520
through the Planning Guidance,

00:56:08.520 --> 00:56:09.690
but we will do that further

00:56:09.690 --> 00:56:11.790
through the UEC improvement plan,

00:56:11.790 --> 00:56:14.040
which we will be publishing in due course.

00:56:14.040 --> 00:56:14.890
Thank you, Emily.

00:56:15.810 --> 00:56:19.620
- Thank you, so just final
thoughts then, Richard.

00:56:19.620 --> 00:56:22.830
I think it's clear we've
continued to make progress

00:56:22.830 --> 00:56:24.828
and need to continue to improve.

00:56:24.828 --> 00:56:26.940
On elective care, it's improving,

00:56:26.940 --> 00:56:28.590
but we need to accelerate the move

00:56:28.590 --> 00:56:31.440
towards restoring the
constitutional standard in RTT

00:56:31.440 --> 00:56:34.050
and reducing long waits at the same time.

00:56:34.050 --> 00:56:35.640
Urgent care, as you've
heard from Sarah-Jane,

00:56:35.640 --> 00:56:37.860
is under daily pressure
and we need to do more

00:56:37.860 --> 00:56:39.395
to make sure good practise,

00:56:39.395 --> 00:56:41.880
including caring for
patients with dignity,

00:56:41.880 --> 00:56:45.240
is supported everywhere in
the system at all times.

00:56:45.240 --> 00:56:47.310
Diagnostics and cancer
performance are improving,

00:56:47.310 --> 00:56:48.690
but building further improvement

00:56:48.690 --> 00:56:50.310
and productivity in is essential,

00:56:50.310 --> 00:56:51.606
particularly as part of, again,

00:56:51.606 --> 00:56:55.620
the return to the RTT
constitutional standard.

00:56:55.620 --> 00:56:57.570
And as I'm sure, it'll be reflecting

00:56:57.570 --> 00:56:58.590
some of the other papers today,

00:56:58.590 --> 00:57:01.230
we experience every day that delivering

00:57:01.230 --> 00:57:03.330
is the bread and butter of the NHS

00:57:03.330 --> 00:57:05.357
through in all of our services.

00:57:05.357 --> 00:57:07.890
But with increasing demand and acuity,

00:57:07.890 --> 00:57:10.560
it isn't enough to just deliver.

00:57:10.560 --> 00:57:12.720
And our work with systems this coming year

00:57:12.720 --> 00:57:13.553
will therefore focus

00:57:13.553 --> 00:57:15.840
on not just how we manage the pressures,

00:57:15.840 --> 00:57:17.730
but on improving the service,

00:57:17.730 --> 00:57:20.345
continuing to support staff
to bring the best of the NHS

00:57:20.345 --> 00:57:23.460
to the rest of the NHS, thank you.

00:57:23.460 --> 00:57:25.680
- Thanks, so I'm going to,
before we bring Julian in,

00:57:25.680 --> 00:57:28.270
pause and see if colleagues
want to ask any questions.

00:57:28.270 --> 00:57:31.350
Can I just, if I can
start with one actually,

00:57:31.350 --> 00:57:33.600
and it's partly I want to build

00:57:33.600 --> 00:57:36.330
on the earlier tribute, Andrew, to you.

00:57:36.330 --> 00:57:37.680
'Cause again, when I first arrived,

00:57:37.680 --> 00:57:41.400
you were always hammering on the message

00:57:41.400 --> 00:57:43.650
of early winter planning.

00:57:43.650 --> 00:57:45.690
The early we plan for winter,

00:57:45.690 --> 00:57:48.630
the early we get at the
necessary enablement.

00:57:48.630 --> 00:57:52.470
And I think that I'd just
like to see with Sarah-Jane,

00:57:52.470 --> 00:57:53.831
I think that did make, again,

00:57:53.831 --> 00:57:56.242
an additional difference
in this last winter.

00:57:56.242 --> 00:57:58.020
And can I just pick up that point

00:57:58.020 --> 00:58:01.463
about there's an urgent emergency care,

00:58:01.463 --> 00:58:02.973
you know, lessons learned.

00:58:04.380 --> 00:58:05.940
Further plan emerging. you said,

00:58:05.940 --> 00:58:07.170
that will come out in due course.

00:58:07.170 --> 00:58:08.760
Do you know roughly when
that's going to come out?

00:58:08.760 --> 00:58:10.740
There's effect that is picking up,

00:58:10.740 --> 00:58:12.750
I think, even earlier winter planning.

00:58:12.750 --> 00:58:14.700
But for each of the last couple of years,

00:58:14.700 --> 00:58:16.920
we've attempted to do
that ever earlier, but-

00:58:16.920 --> 00:58:18.540
- Yeah, we don't have an exact date,

00:58:18.540 --> 00:58:20.580
but it will be over coming weeks

00:58:20.580 --> 00:58:23.670
and it will be arriving
with systems in time

00:58:23.670 --> 00:58:25.830
for people to be able to
take a look at the content

00:58:25.830 --> 00:58:27.870
as they're going through
the planning guidance.

00:58:27.870 --> 00:58:29.040
But it will not be setting

00:58:29.040 --> 00:58:32.066
any new targets or measures over and above

00:58:32.066 --> 00:58:34.350
those that are already
in the planning guidance.

00:58:34.350 --> 00:58:37.619
It's about supporting people
in the delivery of them.

00:58:37.619 --> 00:58:39.420
- And then, I would just,
it's not a question,

00:58:39.420 --> 00:58:41.710
I would just also like
to comment and thank

00:58:43.110 --> 00:58:44.880
you and Emily and the whole team,

00:58:44.880 --> 00:58:46.313
and then all of the systems actually,

00:58:46.313 --> 00:58:48.660
I mean, is such an intense period

00:58:48.660 --> 00:58:50.850
of so many different steps they get taken

00:58:50.850 --> 00:58:52.590
to try and prepare us

00:58:52.590 --> 00:58:56.160
for the surge of demand that
comes through this period.

00:58:56.160 --> 00:58:58.043
And you referenced one
point in it where you said,

00:58:58.043 --> 00:58:59.670
I think just before Christmas,

00:58:59.670 --> 00:59:03.990
you talked bed occupancy
in our acute down to 92%

00:59:03.990 --> 00:59:05.280
and that it gives us some

00:59:05.280 --> 00:59:07.800
and enables some greater capacity

00:59:07.800 --> 00:59:09.930
for what I think you've taught me,

00:59:09.930 --> 00:59:10.800
or Emily has taught me,

00:59:10.800 --> 00:59:13.860
which is actually we get a
real peak pressure coming

00:59:13.860 --> 00:59:15.570
pretty much immediately after Christmas

00:59:15.570 --> 00:59:16.943
and into the first week

00:59:16.943 --> 00:59:18.811
of the new year.

00:59:18.811 --> 00:59:20.670
And actually, we're just getting ourselves

00:59:20.670 --> 00:59:22.230
ready for that surge,

00:59:22.230 --> 00:59:25.830
and it strikes me that is
always a good preparatory step.

00:59:25.830 --> 00:59:27.930
But I just want to give
my thanks to everybody

00:59:27.930 --> 00:59:30.090
who's battled so hard through this winter

00:59:30.090 --> 00:59:31.050
looking after patients

00:59:31.050 --> 00:59:33.300
and a higher, higher volume of patients

00:59:33.300 --> 00:59:34.530
who come to us.

00:59:34.530 --> 00:59:35.363
So let's go.

00:59:35.363 --> 00:59:36.690
Robert, I'll work around this way.

00:59:36.690 --> 00:59:38.390
So Robert, then Mark, then Duncan.

00:59:39.300 --> 00:59:42.570
- Yeah, thanks very much,
Emily, for all of that.

00:59:42.570 --> 00:59:45.813
Just zoning in on community
diagnostic centres,

00:59:46.890 --> 00:59:48.390
which I think are terrific innovation

00:59:48.390 --> 00:59:51.300
and having had a session
with the person leading that,

00:59:51.300 --> 00:59:52.530
I think they're doing a brilliant job.

00:59:52.530 --> 00:59:54.050
But it's far from national coverage yet.

00:59:54.050 --> 00:59:56.979
It's not at the scale that we would wish.

00:59:56.979 --> 00:59:58.904
Really important 'cause it should impact

00:59:58.904 --> 01:00:00.570
on cancer waits obviously.

01:00:00.570 --> 01:00:04.260
And it should have an impact
on the elective waiting list.

01:00:04.260 --> 01:00:05.811
So, two questions.

01:00:05.811 --> 01:00:08.610
One is, can you evidence the fact

01:00:08.610 --> 01:00:10.710
that they are doing those things?

01:00:10.710 --> 01:00:11.790
They're having those benefits

01:00:11.790 --> 01:00:14.190
to make the case for further rollout.

01:00:14.190 --> 01:00:17.239
And in theory, over time,

01:00:17.239 --> 01:00:19.080
this should be kind of cost neutral

01:00:19.080 --> 01:00:20.460
because you should be able to reduce

01:00:20.460 --> 01:00:23.010
the hospital-based diagnostic facilities

01:00:23.010 --> 01:00:24.630
as you expand the community base.

01:00:24.630 --> 01:00:28.234
So I'm just interested in how
we can best make the arguments

01:00:28.234 --> 01:00:30.480
for expanding this capacity?

01:00:30.480 --> 01:00:31.885
- And Emily and Sarah-Jane,

01:00:31.885 --> 01:00:33.743
let's batch the questions up.

01:00:33.743 --> 01:00:37.143
We do technique if possible, so Mark?

01:00:38.640 --> 01:00:40.803
- Clearly a very, very difficult winter.

01:00:41.940 --> 01:00:43.743
There's a productivity paper later.

01:00:45.480 --> 01:00:48.591
The numerator and the denominator
are moving all the time.

01:00:48.591 --> 01:00:51.870
What's the actual real demand growth

01:00:51.870 --> 01:00:52.890
that you are seeing

01:00:52.890 --> 01:00:55.950
and what should we be
factoring into a forward look?

01:00:55.950 --> 01:00:57.450
Because at the moment,

01:00:57.450 --> 01:01:00.090
it's intuitively quite hard to understand

01:01:00.090 --> 01:01:02.730
the underlying pressure on the system

01:01:02.730 --> 01:01:04.211
that is coming

01:01:04.211 --> 01:01:06.663
and is going to come in future years.

01:01:07.860 --> 01:01:08.693
- [Richard] Duncan?

01:01:08.693 --> 01:01:10.020
- Yeah, probably more, excuse me,

01:01:10.020 --> 01:01:12.010
more of a statement
than a question, Chair.

01:01:12.010 --> 01:01:16.866
But I think just the RCN
report on corridor care,

01:01:16.866 --> 01:01:19.320
I think, brought into stark focus

01:01:19.320 --> 01:01:21.930
not only the experiences of
patients who are being cared for

01:01:21.930 --> 01:01:23.610
in temporary escalation
spaces or corridors,

01:01:23.610 --> 01:01:24.630
but also how difficult

01:01:24.630 --> 01:01:25.795
it's for staff
- Yeah.

01:01:25.795 --> 01:01:28.467
- who want to turn up every single day

01:01:28.467 --> 01:01:30.180
and do their very best for patients.

01:01:30.180 --> 01:01:32.200
So I just wanted to recognise that

01:01:32.200 --> 01:01:33.663
and how important it is

01:01:33.663 --> 01:01:35.220
that our continual work

01:01:35.220 --> 01:01:37.564
on making sure privacy, dignity, safety

01:01:37.564 --> 01:01:41.550
is paramount for patients
in that situation.

01:01:41.550 --> 01:01:42.690
And myself and Steve Powis

01:01:42.690 --> 01:01:44.010
are going to be working with our regions

01:01:44.010 --> 01:01:46.260
to see what else we can
do to help support that.

01:01:46.260 --> 01:01:48.150
And clearly part of the
work with Sarah-Jane

01:01:48.150 --> 01:01:49.989
on the UEC plan.

01:01:49.989 --> 01:01:51.450
We'll focus on that.

01:01:51.450 --> 01:01:53.070
- Mary, you made that point very strongly

01:01:53.070 --> 01:01:55.320
in this morning's board
actually about the importance

01:01:55.320 --> 01:01:56.940
of, you know, patient safety,

01:01:56.940 --> 01:01:57.773
really, really important.

01:01:57.773 --> 01:02:00.776
The patient dignity is
also really important

01:02:00.776 --> 01:02:03.960
- And I would echo exactly
what Duncan has said

01:02:03.960 --> 01:02:06.231
and applaud the trusts
that have recognised

01:02:06.231 --> 01:02:10.560
that they need extra
nurses in certain areas

01:02:10.560 --> 01:02:14.067
because they are exceeding
the number of patients

01:02:14.067 --> 01:02:16.989
that they ever anticipated in their A&amp;E.

01:02:16.989 --> 01:02:20.340
And that should result in better care

01:02:20.340 --> 01:02:23.220
for anybody that is in corridor.

01:02:23.220 --> 01:02:25.565
- Thank you, I'm going to go, Wol?

01:02:25.565 --> 01:02:27.310
- Sarah-Jane, I'm interested

01:02:28.146 --> 01:02:30.150
in the lessons you've learned

01:02:30.150 --> 01:02:31.487
and where the stresses and strains came

01:02:31.487 --> 01:02:34.020
in the plan you put together

01:02:34.020 --> 01:02:36.870
sort of summer last year
to deal with winter.

01:02:36.870 --> 01:02:39.720
'Cause of course, the situations
change, conditions change,

01:02:39.720 --> 01:02:42.000
the assumptions about
the state of the state,

01:02:42.000 --> 01:02:43.903
the vaccine, the efficacy of the vaccine,

01:02:43.903 --> 01:02:44.736
all that sort of stuff.

01:02:44.736 --> 01:02:45.930
So going into this year's planning,

01:02:45.930 --> 01:02:48.000
are you able to give some sort of things

01:02:48.000 --> 01:02:48.833
that you're looking and going?

01:02:48.833 --> 01:02:51.450
Actually this is an issue
that might be an issue.

01:02:51.450 --> 01:02:53.490
This is how we do better, thank you.

01:02:53.490 --> 01:02:54.447
- And then, I think there's one more

01:02:54.447 --> 01:02:57.090
if we can go to quite a sizable batch,

01:02:57.090 --> 01:02:58.353
which is Andrew, I think?

01:02:59.427 --> 01:03:01.350
- Thank you, so a couple
of points from me.

01:03:01.350 --> 01:03:04.386
One is take up of the
flu vaccine this year

01:03:04.386 --> 01:03:07.743
and has that made much of a difference.

01:03:08.730 --> 01:03:10.980
And the other one is around activity.

01:03:10.980 --> 01:03:14.201
So we see around 2 and 2.3 million people

01:03:14.201 --> 01:03:16.560
through the emergency departments

01:03:16.560 --> 01:03:18.990
in England every month.

01:03:18.990 --> 01:03:20.460
How many of those people

01:03:20.460 --> 01:03:21.930
could go to other settings?

01:03:21.930 --> 01:03:24.960
Because I think we've
still got a big job to do

01:03:24.960 --> 01:03:26.940
around directing the public

01:03:26.940 --> 01:03:30.120
to all the alternatives that Sarah-Jane,

01:03:30.120 --> 01:03:32.857
you've kind of, you know, planned

01:03:32.857 --> 01:03:36.870
and we've still got a lot of
behavioural change to enact

01:03:36.870 --> 01:03:38.430
'cause people still think

01:03:38.430 --> 01:03:40.303
if I'm ill and it's out of hours,

01:03:40.303 --> 01:03:41.820
I go to A&amp;E,

01:03:41.820 --> 01:03:44.400
and there are lots of
alternatives available.

01:03:44.400 --> 01:03:47.234
Just give us a sense of
the scale of, you know,

01:03:47.234 --> 01:03:48.996
people who you think

01:03:48.996 --> 01:03:51.960
are pitching up inappropriately.

01:03:51.960 --> 01:03:54.720
- Okay, so Emily, do you want
to pick up a couple of those?

01:03:54.720 --> 01:03:58.080
And then Sarah-Jane, there's
some specifics coming at you.

01:03:58.080 --> 01:04:00.690
- I'll pick up a couple and
Sarah-Jane will pick up I'm sure

01:04:00.690 --> 01:04:02.280
the ones about winter.

01:04:02.280 --> 01:04:05.966
So on CDCs, just on the capacity point,

01:04:05.966 --> 01:04:07.170
at the moment,

01:04:07.170 --> 01:04:10.440
we are basically using
all the capacity we had.

01:04:10.440 --> 01:04:14.580
So there isn't a displacement
going on from trust.

01:04:14.580 --> 01:04:16.193
There is enormous growth in demand

01:04:16.193 --> 01:04:18.543
and the CDCs are helping
to deal with that.

01:04:20.100 --> 01:04:22.470
In terms of your point
about so how do we evaluate

01:04:22.470 --> 01:04:23.670
whether it's meeting the needs

01:04:23.670 --> 01:04:25.479
of local populations, et cetera,

01:04:25.479 --> 01:04:27.185
there's evaluation work ongoing

01:04:27.185 --> 01:04:29.910
and I will bring it back to the board

01:04:29.910 --> 01:04:31.290
when we have something on that,

01:04:31.290 --> 01:04:32.760
given the increase in demand

01:04:32.760 --> 01:04:36.083
is quite hard to separate that
out from different effects.

01:04:36.083 --> 01:04:40.230
One of the innovations
that I've seen some CDCs

01:04:40.230 --> 01:04:41.850
being able to do particularly
after sort of six

01:04:41.850 --> 01:04:43.050
or nine months of operation

01:04:43.050 --> 01:04:45.930
is to change their booking
systems, for example,

01:04:45.930 --> 01:04:48.780
to make it more accessible
to the local population;

01:04:48.780 --> 01:04:50.160
as opposed to often when it starts,

01:04:50.160 --> 01:04:52.304
it is mandated by the local system.

01:04:52.304 --> 01:04:54.999
Like you have them an
appointment at X Centre.

01:04:54.999 --> 01:04:57.000
But some CDCs pointed out to me

01:04:57.000 --> 01:04:58.650
that that system doesn't know

01:04:58.650 --> 01:05:00.620
how people tend to drive
to work, for example,

01:05:00.620 --> 01:05:01.740
or you know.

01:05:01.740 --> 01:05:05.730
So I think we collectively,
locally and nationally,

01:05:05.730 --> 01:05:08.210
have work to do to make sure
we're making the most now

01:05:08.210 --> 01:05:10.470
of this more dispersed
network of services.

01:05:10.470 --> 01:05:13.350
But at the moment, we
need all the capacity

01:05:13.350 --> 01:05:14.750
all of the time effectively.

01:05:16.080 --> 01:05:20.970
On your point about underlying
pressures and demand,

01:05:20.970 --> 01:05:24.972
we are, obviously as we plan services

01:05:24.972 --> 01:05:27.600
and I mean ICSs in their commissioning

01:05:27.600 --> 01:05:29.540
will be looking at this as well,

01:05:29.540 --> 01:05:31.860
all of the models have built in

01:05:31.860 --> 01:05:33.874
expected demographic changes, et cetera,

01:05:33.874 --> 01:05:37.530
increases in multiple comorbidities.

01:05:37.530 --> 01:05:40.710
What we are, the couple
of things we need to do

01:05:40.710 --> 01:05:43.320
more on that, the first is
we often, at the moment,

01:05:43.320 --> 01:05:46.950
considering them with related assumptions

01:05:46.950 --> 01:05:47.970
but in different models.

01:05:47.970 --> 01:05:51.357
So for cancer, for UEC,
for elective, et cetera

01:05:51.357 --> 01:05:54.030
and one of the things we
are doing at the moment

01:05:54.030 --> 01:05:56.220
with Vin's team support, Vin's there,

01:05:56.220 --> 01:05:58.680
is to use more advanced
data science techniques

01:05:58.680 --> 01:06:00.855
to try and put together all
of those different assumptions

01:06:00.855 --> 01:06:02.010
and the different changes we're seeing

01:06:02.010 --> 01:06:03.570
in different modalities

01:06:03.570 --> 01:06:06.270
to make sure we're really
predicting that accurately

01:06:06.270 --> 01:06:07.920
and we can build off of that

01:06:07.920 --> 01:06:12.330
and to consider other
effects of local performance

01:06:12.330 --> 01:06:14.040
including, you know, the structure

01:06:14.040 --> 01:06:16.080
of local infrastructure, et cetera,

01:06:16.080 --> 01:06:17.640
which is in its infancy

01:06:17.640 --> 01:06:19.530
but I think will be helpful

01:06:19.530 --> 01:06:21.450
as we try and build that strategically

01:06:21.450 --> 01:06:23.610
and less in terms of next winter,

01:06:23.610 --> 01:06:26.103
but more in terms of the
longer term planning.

01:06:27.122 --> 01:06:28.620
- So, do you want to come in, Julian?

01:06:28.620 --> 01:06:29.670
- Just on the demand growth?
- Yeah?

01:06:29.670 --> 01:06:32.058
- I mean on average across the NHS,

01:06:32.058 --> 01:06:35.190
it's about 3% a year

01:06:35.190 --> 01:06:37.440
of which this number isn't precisely right

01:06:37.440 --> 01:06:39.600
and people debate exactly what it is,

01:06:39.600 --> 01:06:41.800
but it's like about half is demographic

01:06:42.930 --> 01:06:45.990
and half is actually
treatment and technology.

01:06:45.990 --> 01:06:49.118
- And that's compounding
out into the future.

01:06:49.118 --> 01:06:51.300
- That's compounding out is the future.

01:06:51.300 --> 01:06:52.950
So when we plan for the future,

01:06:52.950 --> 01:06:54.075
the thing you've got to work out

01:06:54.075 --> 01:06:55.590
is how you deal with that

01:06:55.590 --> 01:06:56.730
and that's like then your funding

01:06:56.730 --> 01:06:58.330
and your productivity challenge.

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:01.260
- Sarah-Jane, a couple of points?

01:07:01.260 --> 01:07:02.550
- Yeah, I just got three quick areas

01:07:02.550 --> 01:07:04.543
and if I could maybe ask Steve

01:07:04.543 --> 01:07:06.028
to say something on the flu vaccines.

01:07:06.028 --> 01:07:07.320
- [Richard] Yeah, of course.

01:07:07.320 --> 01:07:09.893
- Yeah, he's a highly
knowledgeable colleague.

01:07:09.893 --> 01:07:14.190
So just a quick run through
the lessons learned.

01:07:14.190 --> 01:07:16.350
We've got lots of things

01:07:16.350 --> 01:07:18.540
working across all the individual parts

01:07:18.540 --> 01:07:20.820
of the pathway really effectively,

01:07:20.820 --> 01:07:23.250
but there's a lack of consistency.

01:07:23.250 --> 01:07:26.310
So we've got in a sort
of fragments of services

01:07:26.310 --> 01:07:27.330
that people have developed

01:07:27.330 --> 01:07:28.170
and when they're open,

01:07:28.170 --> 01:07:29.670
they work really effectively

01:07:29.670 --> 01:07:31.650
and we can look after patients really well

01:07:31.650 --> 01:07:33.193
at home or in alternative settings

01:07:33.193 --> 01:07:35.130
to taking them to hospital

01:07:35.130 --> 01:07:37.440
but they can sometimes close at 7:00

01:07:37.440 --> 01:07:39.278
or run out of capacity on a weekend,

01:07:39.278 --> 01:07:41.248
or you know, those kind of things.

01:07:41.248 --> 01:07:43.800
And therefore, while
patients don't always need

01:07:43.800 --> 01:07:46.902
that hospital care,

01:07:46.902 --> 01:07:49.830
those services need to be
there in a more consistent way.

01:07:49.830 --> 01:07:50.663
And that's one of the things

01:07:50.663 --> 01:07:52.320
the neighbourhood health guidelines

01:07:52.320 --> 01:07:53.490
that are part of the Planning Guidance

01:07:53.490 --> 01:07:55.110
is really trying to drive at is getting

01:07:55.110 --> 01:07:57.340
that more consistency
into the neighbourhood

01:07:58.530 --> 01:07:59.853
sort of health offer.

01:08:01.290 --> 01:08:03.210
I think also within hospitals,

01:08:03.210 --> 01:08:04.980
colleagues have touched
on it here as well,

01:08:04.980 --> 01:08:07.980
but it's that getting the
clinical decision making

01:08:07.980 --> 01:08:10.380
as quickly as we possibly can in pathways

01:08:10.380 --> 01:08:12.690
and really the organisations
that sort of geared up

01:08:12.690 --> 01:08:14.520
to get that clinical decision making

01:08:14.520 --> 01:08:15.780
as close the front door

01:08:15.780 --> 01:08:18.268
in the medical assessment
units as possible

01:08:18.268 --> 01:08:20.197
tend to fare better
over the winter period.

01:08:20.197 --> 01:08:22.110
And then, it's that partnership working

01:08:22.110 --> 01:08:23.790
with health and social care.

01:08:23.790 --> 01:08:26.370
And I think, obviously Julian's answered

01:08:26.370 --> 01:08:29.490
the sort of overall demand question,

01:08:29.490 --> 01:08:31.527
but we really encourage systems

01:08:31.527 --> 01:08:33.810
and we need to continue to do more of this

01:08:33.810 --> 01:08:36.360
to do their own version of the demand

01:08:36.360 --> 01:08:38.880
and capacity planning
in their local context.

01:08:38.880 --> 01:08:40.710
So what capacity do we need

01:08:40.710 --> 01:08:42.660
in every part of the system

01:08:42.660 --> 01:08:43.493
for this to flow.

01:08:43.493 --> 01:08:45.300
Because when one part breaks down,

01:08:45.300 --> 01:08:46.650
patients end up somewhere else,

01:08:46.650 --> 01:08:48.960
which is often not the right place.

01:08:48.960 --> 01:08:50.579
So that remains really important.

01:08:50.579 --> 01:08:52.983
And then just on the acuity,

01:08:54.540 --> 01:08:56.160
it's really difficult I think sometimes,

01:08:56.160 --> 01:08:58.046
sorry, on the patients appropriate to be

01:08:58.046 --> 01:09:00.060
or are there alternatives

01:09:00.060 --> 01:09:02.430
to being in an emergency department.

01:09:02.430 --> 01:09:04.200
It's a really difficult
question to answer this

01:09:04.200 --> 01:09:06.780
because services are
organised very differently

01:09:06.780 --> 01:09:07.980
across the country, aren't they?

01:09:07.980 --> 01:09:11.220
And sort of, you know, how visible UTC

01:09:11.220 --> 01:09:13.200
and other services are.

01:09:13.200 --> 01:09:15.570
But as I think we've talked about before,

01:09:15.570 --> 01:09:18.210
when we've done the acuity
work with organisations,

01:09:18.210 --> 01:09:20.130
so we look at the presenting symptoms

01:09:20.130 --> 01:09:23.100
of every patient that comes
through the front door

01:09:23.100 --> 01:09:24.930
and that's a proper assessment is done

01:09:24.930 --> 01:09:28.170
by a clinical team member,

01:09:28.170 --> 01:09:32.070
around 40% of patients are scoring

01:09:32.070 --> 01:09:34.620
with an acuity score of four or five,

01:09:34.620 --> 01:09:37.957
which is more suggestive of minor illness,

01:09:37.957 --> 01:09:39.979
things that could be done in a UTC,

01:09:39.979 --> 01:09:42.360
minor injury unit, et cetera.

01:09:42.360 --> 01:09:44.610
Now in lots of organisations,

01:09:44.610 --> 01:09:47.550
we can stream those
patients into those services

01:09:47.550 --> 01:09:49.650
so they find their way
from the front door.

01:09:49.650 --> 01:09:52.080
Because of the way some of
our organisations are set up,

01:09:52.080 --> 01:09:53.580
that is less possible.

01:09:53.580 --> 01:09:57.450
But it suggests that around
40% of those patients,

01:09:57.450 --> 01:10:00.180
if those services are available elsewhere,

01:10:00.180 --> 01:10:02.128
consistently through the day

01:10:02.128 --> 01:10:04.800
do not need all the support

01:10:04.800 --> 01:10:07.170
of a full-type one emergency department.

01:10:07.170 --> 01:10:08.640
- [Wol] Okay, can I just slight come back

01:10:08.640 --> 01:10:12.480
'cause you phrased
something very carefully

01:10:12.480 --> 01:10:13.313
in your response.

01:10:13.313 --> 01:10:16.703
- I'm glad you noticed.
- I didn't.

01:10:16.703 --> 01:10:19.950
- So, availability of
clinical decision making,

01:10:19.950 --> 01:10:20.970
- Sorry?
- Availability

01:10:20.970 --> 01:10:22.710
of clinical decision making.
- Yes, yeah.

01:10:22.710 --> 01:10:23.940
- If I did my words,

01:10:23.940 --> 01:10:25.050
which would be about layman's,

01:10:25.050 --> 01:10:28.347
which is sort of more
consultants at the front door.

01:10:28.347 --> 01:10:30.049
- So yeah, I mean-
- At the right times?

01:10:30.049 --> 01:10:33.180
- Yes and it's about, it's not just,

01:10:33.180 --> 01:10:36.150
it is the appropriate consultant

01:10:36.150 --> 01:10:37.170
for the group of patients

01:10:37.170 --> 01:10:38.010
that are coming through.
- Yeah.

01:10:38.010 --> 01:10:40.320
- So we've often got our
emergency department colleagues

01:10:40.320 --> 01:10:42.567
and our acute physicians
at the front door.

01:10:42.567 --> 01:10:45.570
But as soon as patients are identified

01:10:45.570 --> 01:10:47.340
as needing to go to a cardiology ward

01:10:47.340 --> 01:10:49.260
or a respiratory ward or whatever,

01:10:49.260 --> 01:10:51.569
those organisations that
can get that consultant

01:10:51.569 --> 01:10:54.390
or that senior clinical decision maker

01:10:54.390 --> 01:10:56.640
to that patient as quickly as possible

01:10:56.640 --> 01:10:59.640
started on their pathway
and treatment of care

01:10:59.640 --> 01:11:01.440
pulled through to the ward,

01:11:01.440 --> 01:11:03.873
tend to have a lower length of stay,

01:11:04.770 --> 01:11:06.360
be more productive and efficient,

01:11:06.360 --> 01:11:08.310
but also get a better
outcome for the patient

01:11:08.310 --> 01:11:09.720
because that's the thing that we need

01:11:09.720 --> 01:11:12.522
to continue to work with
organisations and clinical-

01:11:12.522 --> 01:11:14.607
- [Wol] A bigger payoff overall

01:11:14.607 --> 01:11:16.080
for the trust involved, right?

01:11:16.080 --> 01:11:17.790
'Cause the flow works
better, et cetera, et cetera.

01:11:17.790 --> 01:11:22.790
So in your plan for winter
coming '26 or '25/26,

01:11:23.070 --> 01:11:24.707
how do we get,

01:11:24.707 --> 01:11:26.160
how do ensure that happens

01:11:26.160 --> 01:11:27.480
more consistently across the piece?

01:11:27.480 --> 01:11:28.313
So I'm just pushing on this

01:11:28.313 --> 01:11:30.332
because I think it's a really
important intervention point

01:11:30.332 --> 01:11:32.010
and then really key intervention.

01:11:32.010 --> 01:11:34.269
- So there are some things
that we can do to support

01:11:34.269 --> 01:11:36.810
and our GIRFT getting it right first time

01:11:36.810 --> 01:11:39.150
and our ECIST teams, they have got tools

01:11:39.150 --> 01:11:41.220
that they use to work with organisations

01:11:41.220 --> 01:11:43.080
to look at consultant deployment,

01:11:43.080 --> 01:11:45.780
consultant job planning
and so on and so forth.

01:11:45.780 --> 01:11:49.020
And there are usually
improvements that can be made.

01:11:49.020 --> 01:11:50.418
In some other organisations

01:11:50.418 --> 01:11:53.010
from the smaller DGH, for example,

01:11:53.010 --> 01:11:56.820
it can be difficult to
have enough people on rotas

01:11:56.820 --> 01:11:59.940
to make sure people can
put all the time necessary

01:11:59.940 --> 01:12:01.650
into urgent and emergency care,

01:12:01.650 --> 01:12:04.437
outpatients, diagnostics,
you know, et cetera.

01:12:04.437 --> 01:12:07.230
And so we do find ourselves
sometimes working with places

01:12:07.230 --> 01:12:09.990
where that is more challenging to achieve.

01:12:09.990 --> 01:12:10.890
- [Richard] Thank you,
did you want to bring

01:12:10.890 --> 01:12:13.290
Steve on vaccinations?

01:12:13.290 --> 01:12:14.597
Sorry Vin, did you want to comment?

01:12:14.597 --> 01:12:15.930
- Yeah, I was just going to comment in

01:12:15.930 --> 01:12:17.033
on the point, Chair, thank you.

01:12:17.033 --> 01:12:18.720
And Andrew asked as well

01:12:18.720 --> 01:12:21.810
about patients attending
emergency departments

01:12:21.810 --> 01:12:22.740
who don't necessarily,

01:12:22.740 --> 01:12:23.820
whose needs aren't best served

01:12:23.820 --> 01:12:25.230
by an emergency departments.

01:12:25.230 --> 01:12:27.750
So I think we have done
quite a lot about on this

01:12:27.750 --> 01:12:29.910
over the course of the last year or two.

01:12:29.910 --> 01:12:32.138
So firstly, you know, what
we say to all patients

01:12:32.138 --> 01:12:33.690
is that if you're unwell,

01:12:33.690 --> 01:12:35.550
you should contact your
general practitioner first.

01:12:35.550 --> 01:12:37.440
You can do that via telephone.

01:12:37.440 --> 01:12:40.170
We put in place digital
telephony now to make it easier

01:12:40.170 --> 01:12:41.962
and stop the 8:00 AM rush.

01:12:41.962 --> 01:12:44.460
We have also put in
place the availability.

01:12:44.460 --> 01:12:46.615
You can request a digital
consultation online

01:12:46.615 --> 01:12:48.540
over the last year

01:12:48.540 --> 01:12:50.860
into general practises across the country.

01:12:50.860 --> 01:12:54.510
Thirdly, there's a thing called
the directory of services,

01:12:54.510 --> 01:12:56.970
which is available
through the 111 service,

01:12:56.970 --> 01:12:59.370
which is what 111 call handlers use

01:12:59.370 --> 01:13:00.900
in order to direct patients

01:13:00.900 --> 01:13:03.030
to the service that best meets their need.

01:13:03.030 --> 01:13:04.617
That has had a big technology update

01:13:04.617 --> 01:13:07.467
and we've encouraged all
integrated care boards to update

01:13:07.467 --> 01:13:10.350
so they have accurate
directories of service.

01:13:10.350 --> 01:13:12.270
And then, of course, in all
our public communications,

01:13:12.270 --> 01:13:13.650
we encourage people they need to use

01:13:13.650 --> 01:13:16.050
emergency departments and 999

01:13:16.050 --> 01:13:18.570
when they're worried they've
got a life-threatening illness

01:13:18.570 --> 01:13:20.640
and actually to use their
general practitioner

01:13:20.640 --> 01:13:21.840
in the main or 111

01:13:21.840 --> 01:13:23.910
if they're not able to contact
their general practitioner,

01:13:23.910 --> 01:13:25.331
for example, out of hours.

01:13:25.331 --> 01:13:28.320
- Very good, thank you
very much, very helpful.

01:13:28.320 --> 01:13:29.190
Vaccination, Steve?

01:13:29.190 --> 01:13:30.859
- Yep, thanks, Richard.

01:13:30.859 --> 01:13:32.730
So just to remind colleagues,

01:13:32.730 --> 01:13:34.447
we ran this year three

01:13:34.447 --> 01:13:37.380
respiratory seasonal vaccine campaigns.

01:13:37.380 --> 01:13:40.070
So RSV, flu, and COVID.

01:13:40.070 --> 01:13:41.910
We just concentrate on flu

01:13:41.910 --> 01:13:43.290
to the point that was raised

01:13:43.290 --> 01:13:45.930
against probably just
worth reminding colleagues,

01:13:45.930 --> 01:13:48.388
we started the flu campaign

01:13:48.388 --> 01:13:51.690
a month later than historically.

01:13:51.690 --> 01:13:53.673
That was deliberate, it was planned.

01:13:54.630 --> 01:13:56.850
And that is on the
basis of clinical advice

01:13:56.850 --> 01:13:58.593
to kind of maximise protection.

01:14:00.360 --> 01:14:01.440
And then, if you just think

01:14:01.440 --> 01:14:04.680
about the sort of uptake
point that you raised, Andrew,

01:14:04.680 --> 01:14:08.850
so we did, uptake is broadly
the same as last year.

01:14:08.850 --> 01:14:12.600
There are sort of four main cohorts.

01:14:12.600 --> 01:14:17.340
So in the 65+ cohort, the uptake,

01:14:17.340 --> 01:14:19.860
so the proportion of
people taking up the offer

01:14:19.860 --> 01:14:21.410
is about the same as last year.

01:14:22.290 --> 01:14:23.704
It's broadly the same

01:14:23.704 --> 01:14:27.058
in the clinical at-risk
group as last year.

01:14:27.058 --> 01:14:29.700
And it is slightly better than last year

01:14:29.700 --> 01:14:31.770
in the two to three-year-old group

01:14:31.770 --> 01:14:33.876
and in the school-age group.

01:14:33.876 --> 01:14:35.926
It's like 1, 2%,

01:14:35.926 --> 01:14:37.833
but it's not unimportant.

01:14:39.480 --> 01:14:42.903
And I think all that said,

01:14:44.500 --> 01:14:47.342
kind of the clinical advice on this is,

01:14:47.342 --> 01:14:48.600
and this is what we're starting

01:14:48.600 --> 01:14:51.470
to think about for the coming winter.

01:14:51.470 --> 01:14:54.630
So the clinical advice is you
get a very important benefit

01:14:54.630 --> 01:14:57.210
from two to three-year-olds
in school-aged children.

01:14:57.210 --> 01:14:58.980
So that's an area that we are giving

01:14:58.980 --> 01:15:00.714
some particular thought to

01:15:00.714 --> 01:15:03.783
in terms of this coming winter.

01:15:04.680 --> 01:15:07.020
The cohort where we have lower uptake

01:15:07.020 --> 01:15:08.760
across all vaccine programmes

01:15:08.760 --> 01:15:11.760
is what we call the
clinical at-risk group.

01:15:11.760 --> 01:15:15.364
So it's people who are under 65

01:15:15.364 --> 01:15:18.282
and have a condition

01:15:18.282 --> 01:15:20.730
that puts them more at risk.

01:15:20.730 --> 01:15:23.850
Many of those people don't
see themselves as at risk

01:15:23.850 --> 01:15:27.090
and so there's quite he heavy
complacency in that cohort.

01:15:27.090 --> 01:15:28.110
But that is an area that we need

01:15:28.110 --> 01:15:30.660
to continue to try and focus on.

01:15:30.660 --> 01:15:33.735
Then just for completeness,
in case it's helpful,

01:15:33.735 --> 01:15:36.630
although flu vaccination uptake

01:15:36.630 --> 01:15:38.850
has broadly stayed the same

01:15:38.850 --> 01:15:41.790
or got be slightly better
in a couple of the cohorts,

01:15:41.790 --> 01:15:42.981
for COVID vaccination,

01:15:42.981 --> 01:15:44.330
that doesn't hold true

01:15:44.330 --> 01:15:47.310
and we've seen a decrease in uptake

01:15:47.310 --> 01:15:48.840
across pretty much all cohorts

01:15:48.840 --> 01:15:50.550
including the older age group

01:15:50.550 --> 01:15:53.483
which is not, again, it's
important to consider

01:15:53.483 --> 01:15:54.930
compared to last year.

01:15:54.930 --> 01:15:57.540
There's, again, quite a lot of complacency

01:15:57.540 --> 01:15:59.520
around the COVID vaccination there,

01:15:59.520 --> 01:16:00.870
so
- Thank you.

01:16:00.870 --> 01:16:02.250
Claire, you wanted to just come in?

01:16:02.250 --> 01:16:03.710
- Just building on the point

01:16:03.710 --> 01:16:05.070
about the importance of
clinical decision makers.

01:16:05.070 --> 01:16:07.710
So we talked about having those systems,

01:16:07.710 --> 01:16:09.030
those trust where it works well

01:16:09.030 --> 01:16:11.070
because they've got really
good connections between the ED

01:16:11.070 --> 01:16:12.780
and the specialties.

01:16:12.780 --> 01:16:15.240
I think the other place where
it works well in systems

01:16:15.240 --> 01:16:16.770
is if they've got really
good relationships

01:16:16.770 --> 01:16:18.740
between primary care doctors

01:16:18.740 --> 01:16:21.180
and into secondary care specialists.

01:16:21.180 --> 01:16:23.872
'Cause often then, a
conversation between the GP

01:16:23.872 --> 01:16:25.740
and the specialist actually means

01:16:25.740 --> 01:16:27.720
that the patient can remain at home

01:16:27.720 --> 01:16:29.370
or could be discharged earlier

01:16:29.370 --> 01:16:31.350
because actually the
communications are there

01:16:31.350 --> 01:16:32.250
if problems arise.

01:16:32.250 --> 01:16:34.749
So I think, it's important
to recognise the importance

01:16:34.749 --> 01:16:36.150
within the hospital,

01:16:36.150 --> 01:16:38.400
there is also that piece from the hospital

01:16:38.400 --> 01:16:39.900
coming out into the community.

01:16:40.800 --> 01:16:42.927
- Thank you, so should we move on?

01:16:42.927 --> 01:16:46.443
Can we move on Julian to
you on financial update?

01:16:47.610 --> 01:16:49.892
- So again, I assume
people have read the paper.

01:16:49.892 --> 01:16:52.029
But in summary,

01:16:52.029 --> 01:16:54.900
table one sets out the position at the end

01:16:54.900 --> 01:16:56.283
of month nine.
- Yeah.

01:16:57.810 --> 01:17:01.126
- There is an ongoing
sort of worsening position

01:17:01.126 --> 01:17:03.367
as you look at systems

01:17:03.367 --> 01:17:05.790
for their performance against plan,

01:17:05.790 --> 01:17:09.149
although the deterioration has
actually markedly slowed down

01:17:09.149 --> 01:17:11.940
as we've gone kind of through month eight

01:17:11.940 --> 01:17:13.233
and month nine.

01:17:14.340 --> 01:17:19.340
And in aggregate, we are
sort of 600 million pounds

01:17:20.100 --> 01:17:22.230
behind plan when you
sort of take the whole

01:17:22.230 --> 01:17:27.230
of NHS spend.

01:17:27.330 --> 01:17:30.210
Now I will say I'm absolutely confident

01:17:30.210 --> 01:17:32.859
we will hit the budget

01:17:32.859 --> 01:17:34.440
and in part at least,

01:17:34.440 --> 01:17:37.230
that is because of the action
we have taken centrally

01:17:37.230 --> 01:17:38.670
in anticipation a bit

01:17:38.670 --> 01:17:42.450
of the sort of further efficiencies

01:17:42.450 --> 01:17:46.440
and savings we are delivering
through NHS England,

01:17:46.440 --> 01:17:49.710
which, you know, will be
about half a billion pounds

01:17:49.710 --> 01:17:52.607
by the time we get to
the end of this year.

01:17:52.607 --> 01:17:54.300
And we are targeting about, you know,

01:17:54.300 --> 01:17:55.902
just over further 300 million pounds

01:17:55.902 --> 01:18:00.039
as we look ahead to next year.

01:18:00.039 --> 01:18:04.057
So, you know, I'm confident
we are going to hit,

01:18:04.057 --> 01:18:06.962
you know, we will live
within the total mandate,

01:18:06.962 --> 01:18:08.122
the financial mandate

01:18:08.122 --> 01:18:11.370
we've been given by government.

01:18:11.370 --> 01:18:14.145
And although we're behind plan,

01:18:14.145 --> 01:18:16.470
I will flag

01:18:16.470 --> 01:18:20.470
that systems have delivered

01:18:21.420 --> 01:18:25.410
5.7 billion pounds of cost improvements

01:18:25.410 --> 01:18:26.790
to the end of month nine

01:18:26.790 --> 01:18:29.334
compared to the same point last year

01:18:29.334 --> 01:18:32.700
that is 900 million pounds more.

01:18:32.700 --> 01:18:33.660
Last year at the same point,

01:18:33.660 --> 01:18:34.980
that would've about 4.8.

01:18:34.980 --> 01:18:35.813
Now that is a mixture

01:18:35.813 --> 01:18:37.350
of like genuine savings,

01:18:37.350 --> 01:18:38.954
productivity-
- Yeah.

01:18:38.954 --> 01:18:42.400
- gains and where they
have done more activity

01:18:43.549 --> 01:18:47.100
sort of earnings income benefit.

01:18:47.100 --> 01:18:50.040
Nonetheless, that is still a
pretty sizable improvement.

01:18:50.040 --> 01:18:51.600
However, they're 400 million pounds

01:18:51.600 --> 01:18:55.713
behind where they set their original plan.

01:18:56.970 --> 01:18:59.283
So people are really working hard.

01:19:00.750 --> 01:19:03.362
But, you know, we knew it
was going to be challenging

01:19:03.362 --> 01:19:06.180
and they are behind.

01:19:06.180 --> 01:19:07.572
Our biggest driver of cost

01:19:07.572 --> 01:19:10.293
is the workforce.
- Yeah.

01:19:12.707 --> 01:19:16.050
- End of the year compared
to the end of month nine,

01:19:16.050 --> 01:19:21.050
the total workforce cost is .6% lower

01:19:21.720 --> 01:19:24.630
than, you know, the sort
of run rate 0.6% lower

01:19:24.630 --> 01:19:27.720
than it was at the end of month nine.

01:19:27.720 --> 01:19:30.706
Now a lot of that is because
as we flagged before,

01:19:30.706 --> 01:19:32.490
people are really starting

01:19:32.490 --> 01:19:35.370
to bring down temporary staffing costs.

01:19:35.370 --> 01:19:38.850
Having seen quite big growth
in substantive staffing,

01:19:38.850 --> 01:19:42.690
agency we're anticipating

01:19:42.690 --> 01:19:47.690
will be about one point, you know,

01:19:47.980 --> 01:19:51.300
about a billion pounds lower

01:19:51.300 --> 01:19:52.380
than we ended last year.

01:19:52.380 --> 01:19:54.180
So you know, if you follow
the run rate through,

01:19:54.180 --> 01:19:55.770
we spent about 3 billion
the end of last year.

01:19:55.770 --> 01:19:56.820
It'll be about, you know,

01:19:56.820 --> 01:19:59.670
on the current run rate about 2.1

01:19:59.670 --> 01:20:00.840
at the end of this year.

01:20:00.840 --> 01:20:01.673
Over two years,

01:20:01.673 --> 01:20:04.170
that is about 38% lower.

01:20:04.170 --> 01:20:05.711
We can see bank costs

01:20:05.711 --> 01:20:08.550
which have been sort
of growing historically

01:20:08.550 --> 01:20:10.080
flattened at the beginning of this year

01:20:10.080 --> 01:20:15.080
are now about 5% lower year on year.

01:20:16.110 --> 01:20:19.980
So you know, with the substantive
staffing that has gone in,

01:20:19.980 --> 01:20:21.754
- Yeah.
- that's good.

01:20:21.754 --> 01:20:24.576
You know, people are now seriously

01:20:24.576 --> 01:20:26.700
taking out the temporary staffing cost.

01:20:26.700 --> 01:20:28.110
That's good for staff,

01:20:28.110 --> 01:20:29.490
good for productivity,

01:20:29.490 --> 01:20:30.753
good for patients.

01:20:32.633 --> 01:20:36.787
And you know, we can
see some of our systems

01:20:40.301 --> 01:20:41.643
who have been most,

01:20:43.678 --> 01:20:46.590
I mean, most challenging
like holding to the plans,

01:20:46.590 --> 01:20:49.890
we can see with the
interventions that have gone in

01:20:49.890 --> 01:20:52.200
and with the focus of
their leadership teams,

01:20:52.200 --> 01:20:54.540
you can see their monthly run rate-

01:20:54.540 --> 01:20:56.520
- Yeah.
- improving.

01:20:56.520 --> 01:20:59.187
Now we have still got a lot further to go

01:20:59.187 --> 01:21:02.790
and we can also see those systems

01:21:02.790 --> 01:21:07.650
where they have struggled
to really turn the corner

01:21:07.650 --> 01:21:09.780
and we are working with them to consider,

01:21:09.780 --> 01:21:12.861
you know, what further work is needed,

01:21:12.861 --> 01:21:16.440
what changes in support
and approach are needed

01:21:16.440 --> 01:21:18.150
that we can actually really turn

01:21:18.150 --> 01:21:21.390
the corner for some of those

01:21:21.390 --> 01:21:25.110
who have got the biggest
relative deficits.

01:21:25.110 --> 01:21:25.943
So, you know,

01:21:27.780 --> 01:21:30.153
it's not at all where we'd want it to be.

01:21:32.310 --> 01:21:33.990
People are genuinely delivering

01:21:33.990 --> 01:21:35.970
far more than they've done before.

01:21:35.970 --> 01:21:37.260
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:21:37.260 --> 01:21:39.336
- Nonetheless, in some places,

01:21:39.336 --> 01:21:41.126
we have got more work to do

01:21:41.126 --> 01:21:43.627
and so we are pretty focused

01:21:43.627 --> 01:21:44.940
on achieving that change,

01:21:44.940 --> 01:21:45.840
particularly as we look

01:21:45.840 --> 01:21:47.820
at '25/26
- Yeah.

01:21:47.820 --> 01:21:50.400
- Where clearly, we have issued

01:21:50.400 --> 01:21:51.570
all the kind of planning guidance,

01:21:51.570 --> 01:21:53.941
we've issued allocations.

01:21:53.941 --> 01:21:58.941
It took slightly longer to
get all the decisions agreed

01:21:58.981 --> 01:22:03.120
with the whole of
government in particular.

01:22:03.120 --> 01:22:04.260
We didn't get the final piece

01:22:04.260 --> 01:22:05.430
of the kind of financial jigsaw

01:22:05.430 --> 01:22:07.040
until just before Christmas.

01:22:07.040 --> 01:22:08.640
So unfortunately, we did not manage

01:22:08.640 --> 01:22:10.699
to get planning guidance out

01:22:10.699 --> 01:22:12.030
the other side of Christmas.

01:22:12.030 --> 01:22:13.824
Nonetheless, that is all out there now.

01:22:13.824 --> 01:22:16.600
We are trying to get
all the plans finalised

01:22:17.460 --> 01:22:18.810
by the end of March.

01:22:18.810 --> 01:22:20.820
That is really challenging
for people to do,

01:22:20.820 --> 01:22:25.350
but we are really focused
on achieving that goal

01:22:25.350 --> 01:22:26.490
and giving us the best chance

01:22:26.490 --> 01:22:27.840
of success.
- Yeah.

01:22:27.840 --> 01:22:29.390
- Through the rest of the year.

01:22:30.246 --> 01:22:31.290
If it's worth it,

01:22:31.290 --> 01:22:33.450
I might just, Richard,

01:22:33.450 --> 01:22:34.672
cover the productivity paper given.

01:22:34.672 --> 01:22:36.900
They all, you know, one
flows into the other.

01:22:36.900 --> 01:22:37.733
Unless you take

01:22:37.733 --> 01:22:38.901
any questions from me?
- Can I just pause

01:22:38.901 --> 01:22:40.701
and see if there are any questions?

01:22:40.701 --> 01:22:42.030
But as colleagues are thinking about them,

01:22:42.030 --> 01:22:44.230
we should be mindful that
we are going to go on

01:22:45.336 --> 01:22:47.045
and Julian's going to walk us through,

01:22:47.045 --> 01:22:49.380
talk to us about the productivity

01:22:49.380 --> 01:22:50.700
progress and challenges.

01:22:50.700 --> 01:22:51.840
But I just summarise,

01:22:51.840 --> 01:22:54.963
so we think we are hitting
the numbers for the year.

01:22:56.580 --> 01:22:58.920
You are delivering significant savings.

01:22:58.920 --> 01:23:01.770
Do you have an estimate
for the quantum of savings

01:23:01.770 --> 01:23:03.330
that we might have achieved this year

01:23:03.330 --> 01:23:06.660
when we've come to the
end of on the '24/25 year

01:23:06.660 --> 01:23:08.400
compared with the previous year?

01:23:08.400 --> 01:23:10.560
- Oh, I mean, we'll be
like a billion pounds plus

01:23:10.560 --> 01:23:13.020
higher than we were the previous year.

01:23:13.020 --> 01:23:14.850
That's one, I forget the precise number.

01:23:14.850 --> 01:23:16.416
I mean we're about-
- Like 7 billion, 8 billion?

01:23:16.416 --> 01:23:17.811
- Yeah, we're around like 7 and a half

01:23:17.811 --> 01:23:19.140
at the end of last year.

01:23:19.140 --> 01:23:20.760
So it'll be closer to kind of 8

01:23:20.760 --> 01:23:22.625
- 8 and a half, 9?
- Yeah.

01:23:22.625 --> 01:23:23.458
- Well, that's very good.

01:23:23.458 --> 01:23:26.490
And then, you are clearly
giving us a message in the paper

01:23:26.490 --> 01:23:27.933
that I don't want to be able,

01:23:27.933 --> 01:23:29.610
I don't want to be dramatic.

01:23:29.610 --> 01:23:31.299
But then next year,

01:23:31.299 --> 01:23:33.840
like '25/26 is going to be tougher

01:23:33.840 --> 01:23:35.640
than all previous years

01:23:35.640 --> 01:23:36.990
in terms of the level of challenge?

01:23:36.990 --> 01:23:37.927
Or in all the?

01:23:37.927 --> 01:23:39.210
- I mean, look.

01:23:39.210 --> 01:23:44.210
As, you know, next year is
going to be another tough year

01:23:44.907 --> 01:23:46.020
and I can cover that a bit

01:23:46.020 --> 01:23:47.607
when we do the qualitative stuff.

01:23:47.607 --> 01:23:50.400
- Can I jump in, I mean
if you follow the logic

01:23:50.400 --> 01:23:51.300
of what's being said,

01:23:51.300 --> 01:23:55.620
which is you delivered
even more cost savings.

01:23:55.620 --> 01:23:58.770
But then, there's more
money put into the system

01:23:58.770 --> 01:24:00.895
and that next year, we're pretty
much going to be the same,

01:24:00.895 --> 01:24:03.180
and I know you're talk about
productivity in a moment.

01:24:03.180 --> 01:24:06.180
And that tells that the
demand is the problem, right?

01:24:06.180 --> 01:24:07.161
As in you're constantly,

01:24:07.161 --> 01:24:08.310
we've heard it through UEC,

01:24:08.310 --> 01:24:12.930
we've heard it when Emily
was talking about electives.

01:24:12.930 --> 01:24:15.537
You know, demand is the problem here?

01:24:15.537 --> 01:24:16.980
And actually my question then

01:24:16.980 --> 01:24:20.121
is how do we sort of balance this?

01:24:20.121 --> 01:24:22.197
I'm not sure you can answer it completely,

01:24:22.197 --> 01:24:23.860
but it's a more open question

01:24:24.780 --> 01:24:27.480
in really thinking about at
what point do you have to start,

01:24:27.480 --> 01:24:29.250
say, we have to constrain demand

01:24:29.250 --> 01:24:31.170
in some way, shape, or form?

01:24:31.170 --> 01:24:32.880
- [Richard] Well my comment
was about tilting demand,

01:24:32.880 --> 01:24:35.160
which goes actually the
agenda around prevention

01:24:35.160 --> 01:24:37.080
and early engagement

01:24:37.080 --> 01:24:38.850
and other messaging.
- A constant running to

01:24:38.850 --> 01:24:40.000
- Yeah.
- stand still.

01:24:44.784 --> 01:24:46.452
- What you use as a segue productivity,

01:24:46.452 --> 01:24:51.452
I use that as the segue into
that productivity paper.

01:24:51.761 --> 01:24:53.640
Look, we said we would just continue

01:24:53.640 --> 01:24:54.990
to bring regular updates

01:24:54.990 --> 01:24:57.630
and like where we have got to given

01:24:57.630 --> 01:24:59.520
actually the focus on
productivity and reform

01:24:59.520 --> 01:25:02.820
and we'll get to Wol's question
is absolutely the heart

01:25:02.820 --> 01:25:04.216
of what we need to be doing,

01:25:04.216 --> 01:25:07.053
what the NHS needs to be doing now.

01:25:12.090 --> 01:25:13.680
I'm just going to repeat that we recognise

01:25:13.680 --> 01:25:15.570
the extra funding we've been given

01:25:15.570 --> 01:25:17.870
both for '24/25 and for '25/26

01:25:17.870 --> 01:25:20.700
as we have set out elsewhere.

01:25:20.700 --> 01:25:24.654
I mean a lot of that is
consumed by pay, inflation,

01:25:24.654 --> 01:25:26.370
higher pension contributions,

01:25:26.370 --> 01:25:29.670
employer national insurance contributions.

01:25:29.670 --> 01:25:31.860
And then effectively,

01:25:31.860 --> 01:25:35.370
we have been dealing with demand,

01:25:35.370 --> 01:25:38.430
recovering services,
improving performance,

01:25:38.430 --> 01:25:41.220
actually basically through productivity

01:25:41.220 --> 01:25:43.620
and through making choices

01:25:43.620 --> 01:25:47.490
about how to prioritise spend

01:25:47.490 --> 01:25:51.663
as we recover our core services.

01:25:53.550 --> 01:25:56.676
So you know, a real focus on productivity

01:25:56.676 --> 01:25:59.736
and we have to recognise
that in this year, '24/25

01:25:59.736 --> 01:26:02.493
and indeed as we've set
planning guidance for '25/26,

01:26:02.493 --> 01:26:04.656
we've had to make some pretty difficult

01:26:04.656 --> 01:26:07.290
trade-offs and choices.

01:26:07.290 --> 01:26:08.650
And you know, there are
certain places in investment

01:26:08.650 --> 01:26:10.680
where we would love to be going faster.

01:26:10.680 --> 01:26:12.330
And indeed, you know, we have to work out

01:26:12.330 --> 01:26:13.500
how we can still do that.

01:26:13.500 --> 01:26:17.313
Technology would be one of those.

01:26:18.570 --> 01:26:22.110
Planning Guidance sets
out a series of ambitious,

01:26:22.110 --> 01:26:23.070
and let's face it,

01:26:23.070 --> 01:26:25.216
really stretching productivity measures

01:26:25.216 --> 01:26:28.290
for the year ahead.

01:26:28.290 --> 01:26:32.610
Put simply, in order to maximise
spend on frontline care,

01:26:32.610 --> 01:26:35.010
Planning Guidance says we need to do

01:26:35.010 --> 01:26:36.710
about a 4% overall improvement

01:26:36.710 --> 01:26:38.040
in productivity and efficiency.

01:26:38.040 --> 01:26:39.870
If we're going to balance the money

01:26:39.870 --> 01:26:41.820
and the ongoing sort of recovery

01:26:41.820 --> 01:26:43.970
and improvement in performance

01:26:43.970 --> 01:26:47.690
and we need to be targeting
a reduction in our cost base

01:26:47.690 --> 01:26:52.690
of about 1% in '25/26,

01:26:53.250 --> 01:26:54.690
that's going to be hard.

01:26:54.690 --> 01:26:58.080
We have sent to every acute trust

01:26:58.080 --> 01:26:59.520
and ICB in the country,

01:26:59.520 --> 01:27:03.187
like we sort of benchmarked, you know,

01:27:03.187 --> 01:27:05.100
all of the kind of productivity measures

01:27:05.100 --> 01:27:07.292
where people are on length of stay

01:27:07.292 --> 01:27:09.451
throughput of staff, size of corporate,

01:27:09.451 --> 01:27:11.550
you know, the kind of corporate services

01:27:11.550 --> 01:27:14.136
where we think there's
opportunity on commercial spend,

01:27:14.136 --> 01:27:17.458
where we think there's opportunity
on their medicine spend,

01:27:17.458 --> 01:27:19.440
where we think there's an opportunity

01:27:19.440 --> 01:27:21.471
on like how people are, you know,

01:27:21.471 --> 01:27:23.560
using continuing healthcare.

01:27:23.560 --> 01:27:26.550
And that does actually
show that there are gains

01:27:26.550 --> 01:27:30.570
which are feasible within this, you know,

01:27:30.570 --> 01:27:32.011
at this sort of level.

01:27:32.011 --> 01:27:34.680
And we are not only looking

01:27:34.680 --> 01:27:36.270
at how we maximise the investment,

01:27:36.270 --> 01:27:39.270
we have managed to put in
on things like technology,

01:27:39.270 --> 01:27:40.230
for example the rollout

01:27:40.230 --> 01:27:43.110
of EPRs across the country,

01:27:43.110 --> 01:27:46.020
but also how we invest

01:27:46.020 --> 01:27:48.210
in national scale Improvement Programme

01:27:48.210 --> 01:27:49.620
to give people the basic skills

01:27:49.620 --> 01:27:50.910
and to bring people together

01:27:50.910 --> 01:27:52.680
to learn from each other

01:27:52.680 --> 01:27:54.115
we're doing it well.

01:27:54.115 --> 01:27:56.160
I mean, it's that
never-ending take the best,

01:27:56.160 --> 01:27:58.706
spread it to the rest.

01:27:58.706 --> 01:28:00.030
And that's one of the reasons

01:28:00.030 --> 01:28:01.740
for the changes in NHS England we'll do

01:28:01.740 --> 01:28:04.620
so we can create the funding to invest

01:28:04.620 --> 01:28:08.043
in that support to frontline care.

01:28:08.970 --> 01:28:12.169
But this year, it's like
we have made progress.

01:28:12.169 --> 01:28:15.600
Our latest data says for acute trusts,

01:28:15.600 --> 01:28:16.890
month eight to month eight,

01:28:16.890 --> 01:28:19.061
year-to-date productivity

01:28:19.061 --> 01:28:21.773
has improved by about 2.4%.

01:28:23.730 --> 01:28:25.959
We are now measuring

01:28:25.959 --> 01:28:27.960
the same for mental health trusts,

01:28:27.960 --> 01:28:29.730
ambulance trusts, community trusts.

01:28:29.730 --> 01:28:31.316
That's something we've not done

01:28:31.316 --> 01:28:32.790
before in real time.

01:28:32.790 --> 01:28:35.430
They would also show that they
have improved productivity

01:28:35.430 --> 01:28:38.290
by over 2%, maybe even slightly faster

01:28:39.990 --> 01:28:42.840
than the acute trusts this year.

01:28:42.840 --> 01:28:44.100
That data, I have to say,

01:28:44.100 --> 01:28:49.100
you know, it is in the
earlier stages of development.

01:28:49.200 --> 01:28:51.060
So we will, you know, that is one

01:28:51.060 --> 01:28:53.220
of those bits of data like we are really-

01:28:53.220 --> 01:28:54.510
- Once we clean it.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:28:54.510 --> 01:28:56.220
We are really sort of testing

01:28:56.220 --> 01:29:00.723
but that shows that we are improving.

01:29:02.910 --> 01:29:05.243
And therefore, you know,

01:29:05.243 --> 01:29:08.010
we're setting a really challenging goal.

01:29:08.010 --> 01:29:10.190
But we do think that particularly,

01:29:10.190 --> 01:29:11.890
if we can get the outliers

01:29:13.080 --> 01:29:14.913
sort of lifting up, you know,

01:29:18.480 --> 01:29:22.500
we can target the goals we have set out

01:29:22.500 --> 01:29:24.406
in Planning Guidance.

01:29:24.406 --> 01:29:27.510
Particularly if, again,

01:29:27.510 --> 01:29:29.460
with the growth in substantive staffing

01:29:30.870 --> 01:29:32.100
continue to take out,

01:29:32.100 --> 01:29:34.830
continue to reduce appropriately,

01:29:34.830 --> 01:29:36.930
temporary staffing costs,

01:29:36.930 --> 01:29:39.990
if we can get organisations to really look

01:29:39.990 --> 01:29:42.630
at where the growth has been,

01:29:42.630 --> 01:29:44.760
and indeed how they can use automation

01:29:44.760 --> 01:29:47.070
and you know, some of the tech investment

01:29:47.070 --> 01:29:49.050
that has already gone in

01:29:49.050 --> 01:29:50.100
to look at, you know,

01:29:51.652 --> 01:29:54.154
the sort of support and
infrastructure costs

01:29:54.154 --> 01:29:57.990
which have grown over recent years.

01:29:57.990 --> 01:30:00.120
And then again, if we
can really get the focus

01:30:00.120 --> 01:30:03.003
on length of stay,

01:30:04.740 --> 01:30:08.080
some of the basic management disciplines

01:30:09.900 --> 01:30:11.130
about the way we are using

01:30:11.130 --> 01:30:15.007
some of our clinical resources.

01:30:15.007 --> 01:30:18.240
And actually, we have seen
huge progress in the move

01:30:18.240 --> 01:30:21.690
from overnight to day case rates.

01:30:21.690 --> 01:30:23.790
They're up over 80%.

01:30:23.790 --> 01:30:25.886
We can still target 85,

01:30:25.886 --> 01:30:28.520
huge progress in the use of sort

01:30:28.520 --> 01:30:31.290
of outpatients with procedures.

01:30:31.290 --> 01:30:33.990
And just to go to Wol's question.

01:30:33.990 --> 01:30:35.490
So at this point,

01:30:35.490 --> 01:30:37.830
I do not think it is
necessarily a question

01:30:37.830 --> 01:30:38.670
of us having to have a debate

01:30:38.670 --> 01:30:39.960
about how you constrain care.

01:30:39.960 --> 01:30:42.093
I mean, I think,

01:30:44.850 --> 01:30:46.990
we know we've got further to recover

01:30:48.600 --> 01:30:50.430
from where we were pre-pandemic,

01:30:50.430 --> 01:30:53.490
and we're making progress.

01:30:53.490 --> 01:30:56.610
And as we look ahead,

01:30:56.610 --> 01:30:59.198
we know there are things we can do

01:30:59.198 --> 01:31:01.680
to get care into the right setting.

01:31:01.680 --> 01:31:03.240
We'll talk about one of them later

01:31:03.240 --> 01:31:07.860
when Tom and Amanda present
the work we are doing

01:31:07.860 --> 01:31:11.670
on how do we care most appropriately

01:31:11.670 --> 01:31:16.670
for people of frail,
end-of-life complex conditions.

01:31:18.330 --> 01:31:22.350
We know that even on planned care,

01:31:22.350 --> 01:31:23.640
the work we have done around-

01:31:23.640 --> 01:31:25.470
In fact, the work the NHS has done

01:31:25.470 --> 01:31:26.610
about the kind of growth

01:31:26.610 --> 01:31:28.757
of pre-referral advice and guidance

01:31:28.757 --> 01:31:30.180
where we think we've avoided

01:31:30.180 --> 01:31:32.940
about one and a half million
unnecessary referrals

01:31:32.940 --> 01:31:36.150
into acute care just by
getting better join up

01:31:36.150 --> 01:31:40.673
of primary and secondary care clinicians,

01:31:40.673 --> 01:31:43.470
you know, we think we
can go further there.

01:31:43.470 --> 01:31:48.240
And if we can create enough
of the right headroom,

01:31:48.240 --> 01:31:51.900
we know that technology
and science will enable

01:31:51.900 --> 01:31:54.900
actually massive changes in the way

01:31:54.900 --> 01:31:57.780
that actually, it isn't
just we do the care.

01:31:57.780 --> 01:31:59.280
It's actually we do the admin.

01:32:00.140 --> 01:32:01.800
Because the thing that will really create

01:32:01.800 --> 01:32:04.740
the paradigm shift in
the kind of shorter term

01:32:04.740 --> 01:32:06.570
is the more we can automate

01:32:06.570 --> 01:32:09.662
a lot of the kind of
basic administrative tasks

01:32:09.662 --> 01:32:11.113
because that's the thing

01:32:11.113 --> 01:32:13.386
which doesn't just target
little niches of care.

01:32:13.386 --> 01:32:18.386
It's the thing which applies
right across the board.

01:32:18.900 --> 01:32:21.090
- So, I know Mary, you
want to ask a question,

01:32:21.090 --> 01:32:21.923
make a good point.

01:32:21.923 --> 01:32:24.783
And then, I've got Mike and Mark, yep.

01:32:25.740 --> 01:32:29.670
- It's probably more a
reflection than a question.

01:32:29.670 --> 01:32:30.990
But, I mean, Amanda referred

01:32:30.990 --> 01:32:33.662
to this fantastic new treatment

01:32:33.662 --> 01:32:36.390
for people with sickle cell

01:32:36.390 --> 01:32:39.690
and that's very expensive and it's great.

01:32:39.690 --> 01:32:41.430
But further down the line,

01:32:41.430 --> 01:32:42.810
the total treatment

01:32:42.810 --> 01:32:44.130
and care pathway

01:32:44.130 --> 01:32:47.520
for that patient that receives it

01:32:47.520 --> 01:32:49.350
probably over a lifetime,

01:32:49.350 --> 01:32:51.600
not only will improve the
quality of their lives,

01:32:51.600 --> 01:32:53.340
it'll probably be cheaper

01:32:53.340 --> 01:32:55.410
than recurrent readmissions.

01:32:55.410 --> 01:32:57.150
So we're starting to invest

01:32:57.150 --> 01:32:58.320
at one point.
- Yeah.

01:32:58.320 --> 01:33:01.830
- But not yet seeing the
efficiency of outcomes.

01:33:01.830 --> 01:33:02.670
- There's an early bulge

01:33:02.670 --> 01:33:03.750
in the cost
- Absolutely.

01:33:03.750 --> 01:33:05.867
- for a long run benefit.
- Yes, absolutely.

01:33:05.867 --> 01:33:07.260
- And better benefit?
- And I think

01:33:07.260 --> 01:33:10.560
we really need to make
that clear to society,

01:33:10.560 --> 01:33:12.990
the success of some of those innovations

01:33:12.990 --> 01:33:14.220
that we're adopting now.

01:33:14.220 --> 01:33:16.320
Yeah and I was thinking historically,

01:33:16.320 --> 01:33:17.790
we used to have fever hospitals,

01:33:17.790 --> 01:33:20.250
and then along came antibiotics.

01:33:20.250 --> 01:33:22.138
So just as a point.

01:33:22.138 --> 01:33:24.917
- Mike, you were going to?
- Yeah, I mean,

01:33:24.917 --> 01:33:27.093
one of the things that's coming out

01:33:27.093 --> 01:33:28.590
from the conversations we're having

01:33:28.590 --> 01:33:31.380
is how demand, even in the last year,

01:33:31.380 --> 01:33:32.460
has increased substantially.

01:33:32.460 --> 01:33:35.567
But also if you project
forward for 10 years,

01:33:35.567 --> 01:33:39.423
3% a year is almost 40% more demand

01:33:39.423 --> 01:33:41.310
that currently exists.

01:33:41.310 --> 01:33:42.900
And you've also reflected on the fact

01:33:42.900 --> 01:33:44.220
that you can benchmark,

01:33:44.220 --> 01:33:45.990
you can see opportunities to benchmark

01:33:45.990 --> 01:33:48.630
if you can get the lowest
performers up to average.

01:33:48.630 --> 01:33:50.100
You can see your way to hit the numbers

01:33:50.100 --> 01:33:51.483
but that only goes so far.

01:33:52.980 --> 01:33:55.140
So maybe just like to reflect

01:33:55.140 --> 01:33:57.516
on our investment in technology

01:33:57.516 --> 01:33:59.790
and whether or not you feel

01:33:59.790 --> 01:34:02.350
or whether collectively
we feel that is enough

01:34:03.300 --> 01:34:07.200
currently to drive the
improvements that we will need in

01:34:07.200 --> 01:34:10.770
years three, four, five
as we look forward.

01:34:10.770 --> 01:34:11.980
- So can you manage,

01:34:11.980 --> 01:34:13.488
if we go down the line

01:34:13.488 --> 01:34:16.200
and get the series of
comments and questions?

01:34:16.200 --> 01:34:17.610
You've got Mary and you've got Mike.

01:34:17.610 --> 01:34:19.140
So okay, Duncan, then Mark,

01:34:19.140 --> 01:34:20.850
and then Jane I think, yeah.

01:34:20.850 --> 01:34:22.380
- Yeah, it was just to reflect

01:34:22.380 --> 01:34:24.720
we are, Steve and I are
doing a piece of work

01:34:24.720 --> 01:34:26.880
actually about how do we
make sure our clinicians

01:34:26.880 --> 01:34:30.543
are really engaged in the
productivity challenge.

01:34:31.590 --> 01:34:33.630
Be that in terms of saving money,

01:34:33.630 --> 01:34:35.072
but also about how do you

01:34:35.072 --> 01:34:37.078
better use your own time

01:34:37.078 --> 01:34:41.280
or how do we work not waste
patient's time as well.

01:34:41.280 --> 01:34:43.688
So we are going to be doing a lot of work

01:34:43.688 --> 01:34:46.360
around kind of minimising
waste, maximising care.

01:34:46.360 --> 01:34:47.607
- Yeah.
- And our approach to that

01:34:47.607 --> 01:34:50.400
and actually that bit about
how do we share some great work

01:34:50.400 --> 01:34:51.960
that's already going on in organisations,

01:34:51.960 --> 01:34:52.793
but how do we lift that?

01:34:52.793 --> 01:34:54.180
How do we share that with our clinicians

01:34:54.180 --> 01:34:55.530
and our clinical community?
- Very good.

01:34:55.530 --> 01:34:56.520
- Thank you.

01:34:56.520 --> 01:35:00.180
- So Mike's at least
partially stolen my thunder,

01:35:00.180 --> 01:35:02.640
which is at what stage do
we actually have to invest

01:35:02.640 --> 01:35:04.833
to cover a 40% growth in demand?

01:35:06.450 --> 01:35:08.610
The second question is
just a clarity point.

01:35:08.610 --> 01:35:09.750
It's very easy

01:35:09.750 --> 01:35:13.890
to talk about 2.4% efficiency this year,

01:35:13.890 --> 01:35:17.010
4% next year plus 1% cost saving.

01:35:17.010 --> 01:35:19.260
Could you turn that into pound notes

01:35:19.260 --> 01:35:21.940
that if we, this year I think
is the best year we've had

01:35:21.940 --> 01:35:24.090
for a very, very long time.

01:35:24.090 --> 01:35:26.070
If we perform next year
in line with this year,

01:35:26.070 --> 01:35:28.780
how many pound notes are we
short of the plan for next year?

01:35:28.780 --> 01:35:30.750
- Can I just say we've had fever hospitals

01:35:30.750 --> 01:35:31.710
and now pound notes?

01:35:31.710 --> 01:35:35.130
So it is, I think, you are
back in an earlier era,

01:35:35.130 --> 01:35:38.024
but anyway, good.

01:35:38.024 --> 01:35:41.610
That's a question for Julian, Jane?

01:35:41.610 --> 01:35:43.083
- Yeah, thank you.

01:35:44.229 --> 01:35:45.390
Earlier this week,

01:35:45.390 --> 01:35:46.920
the head of the National Audit Office

01:35:46.920 --> 01:35:47.790
made quite an interesting

01:35:47.790 --> 01:35:49.320
speech about innovation

01:35:49.320 --> 01:35:52.320
and risk-taking in public
services more generally.

01:35:52.320 --> 01:35:55.260
And obviously, reference
has been made to, you know,

01:35:55.260 --> 01:35:56.927
we will need to innovate

01:35:56.927 --> 01:36:00.600
and continue to innovate
a lot more, I think,

01:36:00.600 --> 01:36:02.190
to hit these productivity targets.

01:36:02.190 --> 01:36:04.380
So I just wondered if, you know,

01:36:04.380 --> 01:36:06.300
whether we can kind of
perhaps do a bit more

01:36:06.300 --> 01:36:07.590
to drive that culture.

01:36:07.590 --> 01:36:09.780
I mean, we've obviously got to make sure,

01:36:09.780 --> 01:36:11.040
he says, you know,

01:36:11.040 --> 01:36:12.480
he commits to an accountability

01:36:12.480 --> 01:36:14.176
and scrutiny framework

01:36:14.176 --> 01:36:16.740
that encourages well-managed risk-taking

01:36:16.740 --> 01:36:18.600
and that's obviously important.

01:36:18.600 --> 01:36:20.220
But I wonder whether we can do a bit more

01:36:20.220 --> 01:36:23.220
to drive that culture through the NHS

01:36:23.220 --> 01:36:28.163
given that sort of kind of umbrella signal

01:36:29.310 --> 01:36:30.150
just to, you know,

01:36:30.150 --> 01:36:31.770
'cause I suspect that, you know,

01:36:31.770 --> 01:36:33.990
using the phrase that
you mentioned earlier,

01:36:33.990 --> 01:36:35.370
Julian, about, you know,

01:36:35.370 --> 01:36:37.110
making sure the rest
are as good as the best.

01:36:37.110 --> 01:36:39.900
I'm sure innovation features very highly

01:36:39.900 --> 01:36:41.700
amongst those who are
doing things really well

01:36:41.700 --> 01:36:45.333
and sort of hitting
these exacting targets.

01:36:46.380 --> 01:36:47.940
- And then lastly,
Julian, if you could do,

01:36:47.940 --> 01:36:49.530
there was one more question coming at you,

01:36:49.530 --> 01:36:51.870
which is Andrew I think or comment.

01:36:51.870 --> 01:36:53.384
- Yeah, thank you.

01:36:53.384 --> 01:36:56.880
So just to amplify one part

01:36:56.880 --> 01:36:58.567
on the Recovery Support Programme

01:36:58.567 --> 01:37:00.052
that we run

01:37:00.052 --> 01:37:02.760
or Emily runs,

01:37:02.760 --> 01:37:05.539
we are seeing organisations
start to turn around.

01:37:05.539 --> 01:37:09.404
So if you take North and Mid Essex,

01:37:09.404 --> 01:37:12.377
they have a challenge
of taking out 600 people

01:37:12.377 --> 01:37:14.220
at the beginning of this year.

01:37:14.220 --> 01:37:16.020
They're on target to do so.

01:37:16.020 --> 01:37:20.544
Kings who've really struggled
historically financially,

01:37:20.544 --> 01:37:23.370
they're starting to turn the corner.

01:37:23.370 --> 01:37:24.720
You've got the Hampshire system

01:37:24.720 --> 01:37:29.386
that had about 600 people over budget.

01:37:29.386 --> 01:37:31.320
And in essence, they're starting

01:37:31.320 --> 01:37:33.780
to get that back into balance.

01:37:33.780 --> 01:37:37.020
So we are seeing people taking, you know,

01:37:37.020 --> 01:37:39.390
basic governance kind of issues

01:37:39.390 --> 01:37:40.223
around, you know,

01:37:40.223 --> 01:37:42.477
you can only spend money if you've got it

01:37:42.477 --> 01:37:44.400
and you can't spend it if you haven't.

01:37:44.400 --> 01:37:46.652
- Yeah.
- And that discipline

01:37:46.652 --> 01:37:48.330
is very simple.

01:37:48.330 --> 01:37:50.400
But, you know, it needs to be there

01:37:50.400 --> 01:37:51.900
if we're to deliver the numbers

01:37:51.900 --> 01:37:53.735
that we need to deliver for next year.

01:37:53.735 --> 01:37:57.330
And I think that's cheering actually

01:37:57.330 --> 01:37:59.902
and the, you know, the
programme that Emily runs

01:37:59.902 --> 01:38:03.390
is starting to, you know,
really, really bear,

01:38:03.390 --> 01:38:05.520
you know, some really,
really positive fruit.

01:38:05.520 --> 01:38:06.670
It's really good stuff.

01:38:07.530 --> 01:38:09.930
- So Julian, there were some comments

01:38:09.930 --> 01:38:11.610
and, but there were a couple of questions

01:38:11.610 --> 01:38:12.443
you want to have a go?

01:38:12.443 --> 01:38:15.887
- I mean, on the question,

01:38:15.887 --> 01:38:19.113
Mike's question is like,

01:38:20.190 --> 01:38:22.200
we will have to accelerate.

01:38:22.200 --> 01:38:23.463
I mean, the simpler of doing

01:38:23.463 --> 01:38:27.033
is we will have to accelerate spend soon.

01:38:31.500 --> 01:38:33.810
We are doing as much as
we can within the resource

01:38:33.810 --> 01:38:37.116
that we have and where
you are having to trade.

01:38:37.116 --> 01:38:38.640
I mean to put really simply,

01:38:38.640 --> 01:38:40.800
how do you keep drive, you know,

01:38:40.800 --> 01:38:43.260
enabling patients to be cared for today

01:38:43.260 --> 01:38:46.470
whilst trying to hold
onto the right investment

01:38:46.470 --> 01:38:48.840
for the future?

01:38:48.840 --> 01:38:52.770
It has to be a serious topic
where we really do agree

01:38:52.770 --> 01:38:55.170
what we're doing is we do
the next spending round,

01:38:55.170 --> 01:38:57.900
which will conclude in June.

01:38:57.900 --> 01:39:02.370
Because to deliver sort
of level of productivity

01:39:02.370 --> 01:39:04.230
the government will
definitely be asking for

01:39:04.230 --> 01:39:06.130
and that we said was possible to do

01:39:08.370 --> 01:39:11.952
12 months ago or so.

01:39:11.952 --> 01:39:13.350
You know, we will need

01:39:13.350 --> 01:39:16.410
to be increasing the amount
we are investing nationally

01:39:16.410 --> 01:39:18.060
and giving a platform for people to do

01:39:18.060 --> 01:39:19.980
what they also need to do locally.

01:39:19.980 --> 01:39:22.530
So I mean soon would be, you know,

01:39:22.530 --> 01:39:25.770
we will need to see
further step up in '26/27.

01:39:25.770 --> 01:39:28.680
We've increased in our
aggregate investment's about 30%

01:39:28.680 --> 01:39:31.470
higher than it was three years ago,

01:39:31.470 --> 01:39:33.000
three or four years ago.

01:39:33.000 --> 01:39:36.420
But like, we'll need to
see further steps at least

01:39:36.420 --> 01:39:38.430
of that sort of size, I think,

01:39:38.430 --> 01:39:42.087
over the next couple of years.

01:39:42.087 --> 01:39:45.720
In answer to Mark, your question,

01:39:45.720 --> 01:39:47.147
well, if we've done about two and a half,

01:39:47.147 --> 01:39:48.180
you know, we're on track to do

01:39:48.180 --> 01:39:50.310
about two and a half this year.

01:39:50.310 --> 01:39:51.143
So to get to four,

01:39:51.143 --> 01:39:52.380
that's like one and a half

01:39:52.380 --> 01:39:54.060
and you've want to turn
that into pounds billion,

01:39:54.060 --> 01:39:56.266
well, that's going to be
somewhere in the order

01:39:56.266 --> 01:39:57.600
of about 2 billion pounds of either cost

01:39:57.600 --> 01:40:00.900
or performance risk.

01:40:00.900 --> 01:40:03.540
And that's what we've got to face into.

01:40:03.540 --> 01:40:04.443
- Yeah.
- Now.

01:40:06.810 --> 01:40:08.670
- So I would say,

01:40:08.670 --> 01:40:10.305
'cause I mentioned earlier

01:40:10.305 --> 01:40:13.350
I was at the Black Country ICB last week

01:40:13.350 --> 01:40:16.740
and then in Sussex on Monday.

01:40:16.740 --> 01:40:18.150
And they see the challenge.

01:40:18.150 --> 01:40:19.950
I think they're clearly,

01:40:19.950 --> 01:40:21.660
they recognise the challenge.

01:40:21.660 --> 01:40:23.820
They're clearly up for the challenge

01:40:23.820 --> 01:40:25.950
but it is a sort of a 1% cost reduction,

01:40:25.950 --> 01:40:30.178
4% productivity challenge

01:40:30.178 --> 01:40:32.102
we are given across the systems.

01:40:32.102 --> 01:40:34.363
And some, of course, have got noncurrent

01:40:34.363 --> 01:40:38.433
and other challenges they enter next year.

01:40:38.433 --> 01:40:39.888
I'll just share one.

01:40:39.888 --> 01:40:42.021
It's not for a comment about June,

01:40:42.021 --> 01:40:45.930
just one sort of learning I've had

01:40:45.930 --> 01:40:47.280
coming into the NHS

01:40:47.280 --> 01:40:49.470
from actually the private sector.

01:40:49.470 --> 01:40:51.510
And actually, I know what to say

01:40:51.510 --> 01:40:54.960
'cause I use this glib phrase,

01:40:54.960 --> 01:40:56.880
but for me it captures very much

01:40:56.880 --> 01:40:58.650
what the NHS does,

01:40:58.650 --> 01:41:01.020
which is that quote from "Gladiator I"

01:41:01.020 --> 01:41:02.520
which you know, I've used before,

01:41:02.520 --> 01:41:06.595
which is "Whoever or whatever
comes through those gates,

01:41:06.595 --> 01:41:07.947
we deal with."

01:41:08.940 --> 01:41:11.100
And the difference with the private sector

01:41:11.100 --> 01:41:14.040
is I can choose to close a retail outlet

01:41:14.040 --> 01:41:14.873
because, you know,

01:41:14.873 --> 01:41:18.690
it costs me too much to
reinvest in it or I don't get,

01:41:18.690 --> 01:41:20.160
the footfall's not what I want

01:41:20.160 --> 01:41:23.760
or I can price the customer cohorts.

01:41:23.760 --> 01:41:26.790
I can choose what I offer.

01:41:26.790 --> 01:41:28.170
And so the independent sector,

01:41:28.170 --> 01:41:30.995
the very, you know, really good
independent sector, private,

01:41:30.995 --> 01:41:33.060
the health sector here, you know,

01:41:33.060 --> 01:41:34.630
they choose their cohorts

01:41:34.630 --> 01:41:37.230
and they choose their interventions.

01:41:37.230 --> 01:41:38.760
The NHS has to do
something very different.

01:41:38.760 --> 01:41:42.150
Whatever comes through
those gates, we deal with.

01:41:42.150 --> 01:41:43.350
And so actually some

01:41:43.350 --> 01:41:46.590
of the leavers you have
in the private sector

01:41:46.590 --> 01:41:49.560
of choosing your segments,

01:41:49.560 --> 01:41:51.420
it's much, much, much more difficult

01:41:51.420 --> 01:41:53.104
choosing where you compete.

01:41:53.104 --> 01:41:55.710
It's much more difficult for the NHS

01:41:55.710 --> 01:41:57.095
to think in those ways.

01:41:57.095 --> 01:42:00.015
And then actually, all the stuff around

01:42:00.015 --> 01:42:02.160
managing within the current resource.

01:42:02.160 --> 01:42:04.229
They will have huge ambition mark

01:42:04.229 --> 01:42:05.940
supporting your comment

01:42:05.940 --> 01:42:07.530
to invest in technology.

01:42:07.530 --> 01:42:08.610
The question is, okay,

01:42:08.610 --> 01:42:10.845
how can we get and free
up sufficient resource

01:42:10.845 --> 01:42:12.930
to invest in the technology?

01:42:12.930 --> 01:42:15.210
One to make our current
technology more efficient

01:42:15.210 --> 01:42:16.350
and more cost effective.

01:42:16.350 --> 01:42:19.260
But two, actually to
invest in new technologies

01:42:19.260 --> 01:42:20.820
that enable us to be more productive

01:42:20.820 --> 01:42:22.680
and better at what we do.

01:42:22.680 --> 01:42:26.220
But the private sector, you
know, I think they would,

01:42:26.220 --> 01:42:28.290
they have different levers

01:42:28.290 --> 01:42:30.090
they can pull than we do

01:42:30.090 --> 01:42:32.280
in terms of what they choose to deal with

01:42:32.280 --> 01:42:33.780
'cause that's how they compete.

01:42:33.780 --> 01:42:35.520
And we have a national obligation

01:42:35.520 --> 01:42:37.620
to deal with whatever
comes through those gates

01:42:37.620 --> 01:42:39.340
and it's really different.

01:42:39.340 --> 01:42:40.830
Julian, thank you very much.

01:42:40.830 --> 01:42:41.880
It's a very good paper actually.

01:42:41.880 --> 01:42:44.439
And also the, you know, I do think

01:42:44.439 --> 01:42:47.460
the rhetoric around productivity,

01:42:47.460 --> 01:42:49.740
it misses that point about
the private sector difference

01:42:49.740 --> 01:42:51.540
with I think the national
health challenge,

01:42:51.540 --> 01:42:53.550
but also actually what we measure.

01:42:53.550 --> 01:42:55.230
And it's good that you're
now bringing in measuring

01:42:55.230 --> 01:42:57.300
mental health and community and ambulance

01:42:57.300 --> 01:42:59.550
because the measures that get talked about

01:42:59.550 --> 01:43:00.660
are predominantly in acute.

01:43:00.660 --> 01:43:02.370
And we also know the dozen,

01:43:02.370 --> 01:43:03.840
for instance, capture virtual wards.

01:43:03.840 --> 01:43:05.580
It doesn't capture much of primary care.

01:43:05.580 --> 01:43:07.800
It doesn't capture the
growth in diagnostics

01:43:07.800 --> 01:43:09.118
as fully as it might.

01:43:09.118 --> 01:43:10.620
So we are not measuring it

01:43:10.620 --> 01:43:12.954
quite accurately, appropriately enough.

01:43:12.954 --> 01:43:14.636
But that's not an excuse.

01:43:14.636 --> 01:43:16.500
It's not being defensive.

01:43:16.500 --> 01:43:18.499
We're getting better and
better at the measurement.

01:43:18.499 --> 01:43:20.498
But actually, I still think these levels

01:43:20.498 --> 01:43:22.170
and these actions you are taking,

01:43:22.170 --> 01:43:24.900
which are getting us two,
two and a half percent

01:43:24.900 --> 01:43:27.090
of productivity improvement in this year,

01:43:27.090 --> 01:43:28.680
and then we're going for 4% next year.

01:43:28.680 --> 01:43:31.980
You know, I think it's a very tough

01:43:31.980 --> 01:43:33.630
but necessary challenge.

01:43:33.630 --> 01:43:35.790
So, thank you very much.

01:43:35.790 --> 01:43:36.623
Let's go on.

01:43:38.460 --> 01:43:40.590
So the children and young people update.

01:43:40.590 --> 01:43:41.580
Duncan, I think you are leading,

01:43:41.580 --> 01:43:43.170
we delight to have Simon Kenny with us.

01:43:43.170 --> 01:43:44.553
So good to see you again.

01:43:45.449 --> 01:43:48.150
Do you want to introduce or
we go straight to Simon, or?

01:43:48.150 --> 01:43:48.983
- I'll frame it,

01:43:48.983 --> 01:43:50.340
and then hand over to Simon.

01:43:50.340 --> 01:43:52.830
But thank you for the opportunity

01:43:52.830 --> 01:43:54.736
to talk about children
and young people today.

01:43:54.736 --> 01:43:55.920
You talked at the beginning

01:43:55.920 --> 01:43:57.996
about the work of our subcommittees

01:43:57.996 --> 01:44:00.971
and the quality subcommittee

01:44:00.971 --> 01:44:02.520
recently had a conversation

01:44:02.520 --> 01:44:03.776
about children and young people

01:44:03.776 --> 01:44:04.770
and the work we do and I'm sure Robert

01:44:04.770 --> 01:44:07.560
will want to come in
on that at some point.

01:44:07.560 --> 01:44:09.930
But we also talked at the beginning

01:44:09.930 --> 01:44:10.877
about how ambitious we are.

01:44:10.877 --> 01:44:13.260
We are ambitious for
the care that we provide

01:44:13.260 --> 01:44:14.550
to children and young people.

01:44:14.550 --> 01:44:15.870
The government has set an ambition,

01:44:15.870 --> 01:44:16.703
one of its ambitions

01:44:16.703 --> 01:44:18.765
around raising the healthiest generation

01:44:18.765 --> 01:44:20.643
of children ever.

01:44:21.690 --> 01:44:23.670
But we are against a backdrop

01:44:23.670 --> 01:44:25.230
of some significant challenges

01:44:25.230 --> 01:44:26.789
for children and young people

01:44:26.789 --> 01:44:27.622
and the health of children, young people.

01:44:28.650 --> 01:44:30.980
So we've seen a increase in the complexity

01:44:30.980 --> 01:44:33.329
of health needs for our children.

01:44:33.329 --> 01:44:34.680
You know, there's been a doubling

01:44:34.680 --> 01:44:36.300
of those number of children

01:44:36.300 --> 01:44:38.130
with eight or more chronic conditions

01:44:38.130 --> 01:44:39.775
over a period of about six years.

01:44:39.775 --> 01:44:42.168
We've seen increase in obesity levels,

01:44:42.168 --> 01:44:44.276
mental health needs.

01:44:44.276 --> 01:44:46.647
You know, Darzi in his report recommend,

01:44:46.647 --> 01:44:48.420
highlighted the pressures

01:44:48.420 --> 01:44:51.420
that were on acute and community services

01:44:51.420 --> 01:44:54.600
and making sure also through
our elective recovery plan,

01:44:54.600 --> 01:44:55.680
for example, about making sure

01:44:55.680 --> 01:44:57.767
we've got equitable recovery

01:44:57.767 --> 01:45:00.061
for children and young people;

01:45:00.061 --> 01:45:02.570
and also, you know, our
access and waiting times

01:45:02.570 --> 01:45:03.900
in community services.

01:45:03.900 --> 01:45:07.740
So I think it's really
important for us to remember

01:45:07.740 --> 01:45:09.690
that all without,

01:45:09.690 --> 01:45:12.090
with probably one or two exceptions,

01:45:12.090 --> 01:45:13.290
all of our services

01:45:13.290 --> 01:45:15.652
and everything that we
talk about in this board,

01:45:15.652 --> 01:45:18.840
whether it be about cancer, diagnostics,

01:45:18.840 --> 01:45:21.030
primary care, children and young people

01:45:21.030 --> 01:45:22.920
are seen through those services.

01:45:22.920 --> 01:45:26.670
So really important that we
continue our focus on that.

01:45:26.670 --> 01:45:29.820
And also mindful that our ICBs

01:45:29.820 --> 01:45:32.100
and our trusts have to work closely

01:45:32.100 --> 01:45:34.894
with services such as education

01:45:34.894 --> 01:45:37.680
because obviously, the
importance of children's health

01:45:37.680 --> 01:45:40.658
for educational attainment, employability,

01:45:40.658 --> 01:45:44.730
and then obviously the wider
implications for society

01:45:44.730 --> 01:45:47.144
and moving into adulthood and employment.

01:45:47.144 --> 01:45:47.977
We do have

01:45:47.977 --> 01:45:49.890
a Children Young People
Transformation Programme,

01:45:49.890 --> 01:45:51.510
which is doing some fantastic work

01:45:51.510 --> 01:45:54.540
and I'm sure Simon will
talk about that in a moment,

01:45:54.540 --> 01:45:57.746
which is focused on some particular areas

01:45:57.746 --> 01:46:00.754
to do with improving care for
children and young people.

01:46:00.754 --> 01:46:02.377
But one of the jobs that we have

01:46:02.377 --> 01:46:03.660
within the work that we do

01:46:03.660 --> 01:46:05.700
is to try and bring together

01:46:05.700 --> 01:46:07.082
across NHS England

01:46:07.082 --> 01:46:08.760
all of our teams

01:46:08.760 --> 01:46:10.488
that are working across areas

01:46:10.488 --> 01:46:14.050
to make sure that we are
focusing clearly on the needs

01:46:14.050 --> 01:46:15.360
of children and young people,

01:46:15.360 --> 01:46:17.460
but also importantly giving voice

01:46:17.460 --> 01:46:19.065
to those children and young people.

01:46:19.065 --> 01:46:20.005
I was really,

01:46:20.005 --> 01:46:21.941
it was great to join an event

01:46:21.941 --> 01:46:24.943
in November around the 10-Year Plan,

01:46:24.943 --> 01:46:27.480
which was focused very much
on children and young people

01:46:27.480 --> 01:46:29.730
and the team have been working
with the 10-Year Plan team

01:46:29.730 --> 01:46:32.427
to make sure that the voices
of children and young people

01:46:32.427 --> 01:46:33.990
and the needs of children and young people

01:46:33.990 --> 01:46:35.280
are reflected through that.

01:46:35.280 --> 01:46:38.760
But it was started by six
of our youth board members

01:46:38.760 --> 01:46:41.104
who, I have to say,

01:46:41.104 --> 01:46:43.440
have no filter and just say it how it is.

01:46:43.440 --> 01:46:44.970
And it is really refreshing

01:46:44.970 --> 01:46:47.370
to hear actually their views around this.

01:46:47.370 --> 01:46:48.930
So one of my challenges to the board today

01:46:48.930 --> 01:46:50.970
will be around what more do we need to do

01:46:50.970 --> 01:46:52.560
to make sure that we are hearing

01:46:52.560 --> 01:46:54.460
the voice of children and young people

01:46:55.530 --> 01:46:57.570
in our interactions

01:46:57.570 --> 01:46:59.276
and the work that we do.

01:46:59.276 --> 01:47:00.330
But also then,

01:47:00.330 --> 01:47:01.440
how are we making sure

01:47:01.440 --> 01:47:04.250
that all of our discussions
around any of our work

01:47:04.250 --> 01:47:07.800
thinks about the needs of
our children and young people

01:47:07.800 --> 01:47:09.870
and also then the work that we're doing

01:47:09.870 --> 01:47:11.700
to influence the 10-Year Plan.

01:47:11.700 --> 01:47:13.440
So I'm going to stop talking now.

01:47:13.440 --> 01:47:14.460
I'm going to hand over to Simon,

01:47:14.460 --> 01:47:15.630
who's our National Clinical Director

01:47:15.630 --> 01:47:17.520
for Children and Young People.

01:47:17.520 --> 01:47:19.500
- Thanks, Duncan, and for
those who don't know me,

01:47:19.500 --> 01:47:21.360
I'm as well as been
national clinical director.

01:47:21.360 --> 01:47:23.430
I'm a practising paediatric surgeon

01:47:23.430 --> 01:47:24.960
at Alder Hey Children's Hospital.

01:47:24.960 --> 01:47:27.690
Using AI for the first time
this week in my clinics,

01:47:27.690 --> 01:47:28.710
is a game-changer.

01:47:28.710 --> 01:47:30.090
I can say it.

01:47:30.090 --> 01:47:31.950
And what I would say is
everything you've heard

01:47:31.950 --> 01:47:34.320
this afternoon about
increasing productivity,

01:47:34.320 --> 01:47:36.110
it's played through into the front line.

01:47:36.110 --> 01:47:38.000
- [Richard] In what way are you using it?

01:47:38.000 --> 01:47:39.750
- Using it in clinics

01:47:39.750 --> 01:47:42.338
to basically summarise

01:47:42.338 --> 01:47:45.450
what's discussions take place in clinic.

01:47:45.450 --> 01:47:46.740
- Is that so voice-to-text?

01:47:46.740 --> 01:47:47.790
Is that the diagnosis

01:47:47.790 --> 01:47:49.140
or the examining?
- It just,

01:47:49.140 --> 01:47:50.370
it listens to the consultation.

01:47:50.370 --> 01:47:52.050
It summarises.
- Brilliant, yeah.

01:47:52.050 --> 01:47:53.610
Voice-to-text, ambient documentation.

01:47:53.610 --> 01:47:55.232
- And I am the world's slowest typist.

01:47:55.232 --> 01:47:57.419
- [Richard] You haven't seen me.

01:47:57.419 --> 01:48:00.030
- We can have a competition.

01:48:00.030 --> 01:48:01.800
Anyways, just going back though,

01:48:01.800 --> 01:48:03.340
we've talked about demand

01:48:04.464 --> 01:48:05.580
and really what we're looking at here

01:48:05.580 --> 01:48:07.260
is super forward demand

01:48:07.260 --> 01:48:09.477
about what's happening with
children and young people.

01:48:09.477 --> 01:48:11.910
And so just to put into
context with what Duncan said

01:48:11.910 --> 01:48:14.370
about, for instance,
children living with obesity

01:48:14.370 --> 01:48:16.249
and excess weight,

01:48:16.249 --> 01:48:19.080
what we start to see is the signals

01:48:19.080 --> 01:48:20.430
in that start to play through

01:48:20.430 --> 01:48:22.650
within every single organ system,

01:48:22.650 --> 01:48:24.090
not necessarily during childhood,

01:48:24.090 --> 01:48:25.860
but certainly in early 20s, 30s,

01:48:25.860 --> 01:48:27.510
where we see for instance,

01:48:27.510 --> 01:48:30.420
people need joint replacements,
et cetera, et cetera.

01:48:30.420 --> 01:48:32.700
And so if we don't get children right,

01:48:32.700 --> 01:48:34.230
then the burden on the health service

01:48:34.230 --> 01:48:35.460
will get longer and longer

01:48:35.460 --> 01:48:38.130
because the number of people who spend

01:48:38.130 --> 01:48:39.922
lots of their life with
living with illness

01:48:39.922 --> 01:48:42.240
rather than living their lives

01:48:42.240 --> 01:48:43.470
is going to suffer.

01:48:43.470 --> 01:48:45.930
Not only that is that every single parent,

01:48:45.930 --> 01:48:47.460
and we all want, it's
an natural human thing

01:48:47.460 --> 01:48:48.750
to want the best for our children

01:48:48.750 --> 01:48:51.600
and our children to be
emotionally resilient,

01:48:51.600 --> 01:48:54.986
mentally resilient and
have a healthy life.

01:48:54.986 --> 01:48:57.000
And again, if we don't get that right,

01:48:57.000 --> 01:48:58.650
we won't have the workforce of the future.

01:48:58.650 --> 01:49:00.810
It's a competitive world out there.

01:49:00.810 --> 01:49:02.354
And again, what Duncan says

01:49:02.354 --> 01:49:06.420
is that we also have strong
links across government

01:49:06.420 --> 01:49:09.480
because the implications
of what we do with the NHS

01:49:09.480 --> 01:49:10.980
will have implications

01:49:10.980 --> 01:49:13.800
for the future health
and wealth of our nation.

01:49:13.800 --> 01:49:17.160
So just running through
our programme priorities,

01:49:17.160 --> 01:49:18.870
it's a slightly mixed bag.

01:49:18.870 --> 01:49:20.340
So there's five areas

01:49:20.340 --> 01:49:22.380
we're going to focus on in the report.

01:49:22.380 --> 01:49:24.423
The first one is reducing
elective waiting times.

01:49:24.423 --> 01:49:27.030
And the number of children
relative to adults

01:49:27.030 --> 01:49:29.970
waiting for elective
treatment is actually less.

01:49:29.970 --> 01:49:32.087
So it could be easy to oversee,

01:49:32.087 --> 01:49:34.167
to lose sight of that.

01:49:34.167 --> 01:49:36.270
And we do see that children are waiting

01:49:36.270 --> 01:49:38.433
longer for elective treatment than adults.

01:49:39.690 --> 01:49:41.820
And we've recognised that
over the last two years.

01:49:41.820 --> 01:49:45.360
And I'm also the GIRFT
responsible lead for children.

01:49:45.360 --> 01:49:46.553
So we've worked within the GIRFT Programme

01:49:46.553 --> 01:49:49.170
and has set up a dedicated governance

01:49:49.170 --> 01:49:51.057
to respond to that.

01:49:51.057 --> 01:49:52.890
And in particular, we're going to use

01:49:52.890 --> 01:49:54.137
all the innovations
that are coming through

01:49:54.137 --> 01:49:55.890
in the elective plan

01:49:55.890 --> 01:49:57.807
to sort of drive through that

01:49:57.807 --> 01:50:01.050
and make sure children
get part of the action.

01:50:01.050 --> 01:50:03.420
So one example is
challenging surgical hubs

01:50:03.420 --> 01:50:05.250
to take on children's surgical activity.

01:50:05.250 --> 01:50:07.260
At the moment, 1% of children

01:50:07.260 --> 01:50:09.540
are going through surgical hubs.

01:50:09.540 --> 01:50:11.910
If you did it on a per-capita
basis, it would be 5%.

01:50:11.910 --> 01:50:14.820
And so the challenge is how
can we put children through

01:50:14.820 --> 01:50:17.130
and get 'em through so they
can get treated faster.

01:50:17.130 --> 01:50:19.979
Similarly with community
diagnostic centres,

01:50:19.979 --> 01:50:22.439
the emphasis has been
on adult diagnostics.

01:50:22.439 --> 01:50:24.900
So we're working with the CDC team

01:50:24.900 --> 01:50:27.240
on how we can accelerate diagnosis

01:50:27.240 --> 01:50:29.700
in asthma and epilepsy

01:50:29.700 --> 01:50:32.880
and children with
musculoskeletal conditions.

01:50:32.880 --> 01:50:35.382
And this also saves families money

01:50:35.382 --> 01:50:37.680
because every time we bring people

01:50:37.680 --> 01:50:39.263
into centres for treatment,

01:50:39.263 --> 01:50:42.224
it's a huge cost for parents and families.

01:50:42.224 --> 01:50:44.760
We've got the working with advice

01:50:44.760 --> 01:50:46.350
and guidance to make sure

01:50:46.350 --> 01:50:50.130
that we can reduce unnecessary
referrals into hospital.

01:50:50.130 --> 01:50:51.810
And the electronic referral system,

01:50:51.810 --> 01:50:53.530
we're working with them to put

01:50:54.990 --> 01:50:57.450
prioritisation tools or
that are built into it

01:50:57.450 --> 01:50:59.240
so that we can see the
priority of referral,

01:50:59.240 --> 01:51:01.113
those children are referred in.

01:51:03.510 --> 01:51:05.127
Moving on to community

01:51:05.127 --> 01:51:07.080
and mental health services waiting times,

01:51:07.080 --> 01:51:10.140
this is an area which I
think we are concerned about.

01:51:10.140 --> 01:51:12.690
We know we haven't done enough about.

01:51:12.690 --> 01:51:14.584
And there's a number of reasons for this

01:51:14.584 --> 01:51:17.490
which could take up the
rest of the afternoon,

01:51:17.490 --> 01:51:18.323
to be honest.

01:51:18.323 --> 01:51:19.156
It's certainly an area

01:51:19.156 --> 01:51:21.430
we really need to focus
on in the 10-Year Plan.

01:51:22.770 --> 01:51:24.990
Looking at the Darzi three shifts,

01:51:24.990 --> 01:51:28.140
the areas that we see huge benefit in that

01:51:28.140 --> 01:51:30.990
is moving children out
of hospital to community

01:51:30.990 --> 01:51:33.780
using the integrated
neighbourhood teams model.

01:51:33.780 --> 01:51:34.980
And the advantage with children

01:51:34.980 --> 01:51:37.560
is that that's been being
done for the last decade.

01:51:37.560 --> 01:51:39.360
And so we've got an evidence base

01:51:39.360 --> 01:51:41.160
that it's not just theory,

01:51:41.160 --> 01:51:41.993
it's actual practise.

01:51:41.993 --> 01:51:43.530
And we know that if we do that,

01:51:43.530 --> 01:51:46.440
we can reduce A&amp;E admissions by 22%.

01:51:46.440 --> 01:51:50.070
We can reduce hospital
outpatient appointments by 42%

01:51:50.070 --> 01:51:53.550
and elective admissions by 17%.

01:51:53.550 --> 01:51:56.489
And that's by embedding
paediatricians into primary care

01:51:56.489 --> 01:51:58.143
and working in partnership.

01:51:59.820 --> 01:52:02.842
In terms of looking at
sickness to prevention,

01:52:02.842 --> 01:52:06.405
we've got a number of
specialised clinics for children

01:52:06.405 --> 01:52:08.670
with the most extreme weight.

01:52:08.670 --> 01:52:10.290
But we've built in that a data set

01:52:10.290 --> 01:52:12.480
which allows us to capture the
experience of those children

01:52:12.480 --> 01:52:14.130
and work out what worked.

01:52:14.130 --> 01:52:15.900
And the National Audit Office

01:52:15.900 --> 01:52:18.270
has long criticised

01:52:18.270 --> 01:52:20.460
our interventions in terms of obesity

01:52:20.460 --> 01:52:22.140
for the lack of evidence base.

01:52:22.140 --> 01:52:23.340
And the sort of issues

01:52:23.340 --> 01:52:24.570
we're sort of working with that

01:52:24.570 --> 01:52:27.000
is what's the role of GLP-1 agonists,

01:52:27.000 --> 01:52:30.150
what's the role which models work best

01:52:30.150 --> 01:52:33.630
in terms of providing
support to those children.

01:52:33.630 --> 01:52:34.934
And also, we've got

01:52:34.934 --> 01:52:38.190
a novel apps that we are introducing

01:52:38.190 --> 01:52:40.500
which can play a role.

01:52:40.500 --> 01:52:43.080
And the early data shows that
the earlier we intervene,

01:52:43.080 --> 01:52:45.120
the more impact we can have.

01:52:45.120 --> 01:52:48.180
And so early recognition
is really important.

01:52:48.180 --> 01:52:50.190
One sort, it's quite bleak,

01:52:50.190 --> 01:52:51.856
a lot of this summary, I know.

01:52:51.856 --> 01:52:53.953
But one area where we
think we can celebrate

01:52:53.953 --> 01:52:56.842
is the early warning system in hospitals.

01:52:56.842 --> 01:52:59.820
So in the last year, we've introduced

01:52:59.820 --> 01:53:02.100
the National Paediatric
Early Warning System

01:53:02.100 --> 01:53:04.440
and it's the largest early warning system

01:53:04.440 --> 01:53:05.850
in the world.

01:53:05.850 --> 01:53:07.380
So we're the biggest nation

01:53:07.380 --> 01:53:09.480
that's done this and standardised,

01:53:09.480 --> 01:53:11.700
the ability to detect a
deteriorating patient.

01:53:11.700 --> 01:53:13.770
And we've launching that

01:53:13.770 --> 01:53:15.600
into emergency departments

01:53:15.600 --> 01:53:17.250
in the coming months,

01:53:17.250 --> 01:53:18.690
and then turning our attention

01:53:18.690 --> 01:53:20.790
to how do we detect the 3,000 children

01:53:20.790 --> 01:53:22.976
that deteriorate in the community annually

01:53:22.976 --> 01:53:24.293
so we can get out there.

01:53:24.293 --> 01:53:26.790
And that that's going to
involve use of machine learning

01:53:26.790 --> 01:53:28.320
and artificial intelligence as well.

01:53:28.320 --> 01:53:30.273
And then finally,

01:53:31.290 --> 01:53:33.570
I'd just like to emphasise the key role

01:53:33.570 --> 01:53:35.520
of the shift from analogue to digital

01:53:35.520 --> 01:53:38.010
in providing improved healthcare

01:53:38.010 --> 01:53:39.597
and outcomes for our children.

01:53:39.597 --> 01:53:41.760
And in particular, the most
exciting thing at the moment

01:53:41.760 --> 01:53:43.546
is the NHS proxy access

01:53:43.546 --> 01:53:46.410
which is where in the NHS App,

01:53:46.410 --> 01:53:49.530
parents can access their child.

01:53:49.530 --> 01:53:52.110
And that is the fundamental building block

01:53:52.110 --> 01:53:54.660
for a digital child health record.

01:53:54.660 --> 01:53:56.340
And I'm confident in the next few years,

01:53:56.340 --> 01:53:59.130
we'll start to have the
first digital citizens

01:53:59.130 --> 01:54:00.600
that their entire healthcare record

01:54:00.600 --> 01:54:02.163
will be kept within the NHS App.

01:54:03.810 --> 01:54:07.950
And so on that, I will probably stop.

01:54:07.950 --> 01:54:09.000
- And we'll have the questions.

01:54:09.000 --> 01:54:09.833
Well, thank you.

01:54:09.833 --> 01:54:11.250
I mean, I can see a
flood of hands going up.

01:54:11.250 --> 01:54:13.035
So Mark, I'll go Mark, then Vin,

01:54:13.035 --> 01:54:14.556
then I'll come this side if that's-

01:54:14.556 --> 01:54:15.770
- Thank you very much.

01:54:15.770 --> 01:54:19.683
So firstly, I welcome the paper.

01:54:21.000 --> 01:54:22.200
Secondly, just picking up something

01:54:22.200 --> 01:54:23.970
that Mary said earlier as well.

01:54:23.970 --> 01:54:25.950
The return on investment

01:54:25.950 --> 01:54:27.960
in young children and young people

01:54:27.960 --> 01:54:30.150
who have their whole lives ahead of them

01:54:30.150 --> 01:54:33.504
is far greater than the
return on investment

01:54:33.504 --> 01:54:36.810
on me as I approach my second childhood.

01:54:36.810 --> 01:54:38.403
So I can be unfiltered as well.

01:54:40.410 --> 01:54:44.220
So we were asked to approve
the strategic priorities.

01:54:44.220 --> 01:54:46.440
There's one area that I think is absent.

01:54:46.440 --> 01:54:48.330
You talked about paediatricians,

01:54:48.330 --> 01:54:52.020
but actually it's teeth, dentistry.

01:54:52.020 --> 01:54:53.250
That is one of the major things

01:54:53.250 --> 01:54:55.110
that put children in hospital.

01:54:55.110 --> 01:54:57.270
There are relatively simple interventions

01:54:57.270 --> 01:54:59.311
that can help that prevent that.

01:54:59.311 --> 01:55:04.311
And dental health has lifelong impacts

01:55:04.380 --> 01:55:05.400
actually.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:55:05.400 --> 01:55:07.500
- And so it seems to me
that it's really important

01:55:07.500 --> 01:55:09.810
that that is included as well.

01:55:09.810 --> 01:55:11.594
And I think that, you know,

01:55:11.594 --> 01:55:14.760
you rightly identify

01:55:14.760 --> 01:55:16.050
all the challenges there are.

01:55:16.050 --> 01:55:18.750
I think personally we speak
far too little of the board

01:55:18.750 --> 01:55:21.018
about children and young people

01:55:21.018 --> 01:55:22.800
and I think the challenge now

01:55:22.800 --> 01:55:24.423
is to turn this into a plan.

01:55:25.830 --> 01:55:29.307
- Okay, so Simon and Duncan,

01:55:29.307 --> 01:55:31.260
we are going to go around and batch.

01:55:31.260 --> 01:55:33.600
Otherwise, it becomes a
big game of ping pong.

01:55:33.600 --> 01:55:34.890
So hard luck.

01:55:34.890 --> 01:55:36.240
You have to sort of keep a note

01:55:36.240 --> 01:55:37.440
of the questions or the points.

01:55:37.440 --> 01:55:39.060
And then if I can get you to respond

01:55:39.060 --> 01:55:40.153
with Duncan at the end,

01:55:40.153 --> 01:55:40.986
if that's okay as we go around,

01:55:40.986 --> 01:55:43.440
pick up the questions, so Vin?

01:55:43.440 --> 01:55:46.200
- Yeah, this is a comment
rather than a question actually,

01:55:46.200 --> 01:55:47.033
Chair, thank you.

01:55:47.033 --> 01:55:48.810
So I've been a paediatrician

01:55:48.810 --> 01:55:50.190
for I think more than 30 years now

01:55:50.190 --> 01:55:51.810
as medical director at
Birmingham Children's

01:55:51.810 --> 01:55:53.490
and then at Great Ormond Street.

01:55:53.490 --> 01:55:55.710
And so I've been trying to
improve things in this area

01:55:55.710 --> 01:55:56.790
for that period of time.

01:55:56.790 --> 01:55:58.980
I have to say, and I just
want to pay tribute to Simon

01:55:58.980 --> 01:56:01.770
and Sarah-Jane and Duncan's
leadership in this area,

01:56:01.770 --> 01:56:03.390
there as a team have made more progress

01:56:03.390 --> 01:56:05.340
on some of these issues
in the last five years

01:56:05.340 --> 01:56:07.406
than in any of the previous 20.

01:56:07.406 --> 01:56:08.850
So I think it's been an outstanding team

01:56:08.850 --> 01:56:10.320
that's worked on this.

01:56:10.320 --> 01:56:13.260
And I do really want to
support Simon's point

01:56:13.260 --> 01:56:15.441
that the report itself really illustrates

01:56:15.441 --> 01:56:17.490
and brings out quite starkly the range

01:56:17.490 --> 01:56:18.720
of challenges across both

01:56:18.720 --> 01:56:20.160
- physical, dental,
- Scalable need.

01:56:20.160 --> 01:56:21.777
- and mental health for
children and young people.

01:56:21.777 --> 01:56:23.363
And this is an area we have to tackle

01:56:23.363 --> 01:56:26.070
as part of the 10-Year Health Plan.

01:56:26.070 --> 01:56:28.800
Because as Mark says,
if we don't tackle that,

01:56:28.800 --> 01:56:30.630
we are stacking up a load of issues

01:56:30.630 --> 01:56:32.760
that then the future board
will be talking about

01:56:32.760 --> 01:56:35.490
which of the conversation
we've just had with Julian.

01:56:35.490 --> 01:56:36.717
- Thank you, so sometimes the comments,

01:56:36.717 --> 01:56:38.400
one just can reflect that,

01:56:38.400 --> 01:56:40.560
but listen out for questions.

01:56:40.560 --> 01:56:42.006
So we'll come up with Robert I think.

01:56:42.006 --> 01:56:42.981
- Then?
- Yeah.

01:56:42.981 --> 01:56:44.918
I just, this came to the committee

01:56:44.918 --> 01:56:46.170
as Duncan mentioned,

01:56:46.170 --> 01:56:49.367
and I just wanted the whole
board to be cited on this

01:56:49.367 --> 01:56:51.630
'cause I think Simon used
the word bleak, I mean,

01:56:51.630 --> 01:56:53.130
- it's really challenging.
- Yeah.

01:56:53.130 --> 01:56:54.720
- Some great stuff going on,

01:56:54.720 --> 01:56:55.800
but it's very challenging.

01:56:55.800 --> 01:56:58.350
And I mean the fact that elective waits

01:56:58.350 --> 01:56:59.790
are longer in kids than adults

01:56:59.790 --> 01:57:02.050
just has to be seen as inappropriate

01:57:03.360 --> 01:57:06.120
and it's important for
the sake of our kids.

01:57:06.120 --> 01:57:08.376
But as you pointed out,

01:57:08.376 --> 01:57:10.560
it's important for the rest of life.

01:57:10.560 --> 01:57:12.540
And if we don't solve these problems

01:57:12.540 --> 01:57:14.311
and address them properly,

01:57:14.311 --> 01:57:17.847
then we're just making problems
for ourselves downstream.

01:57:17.847 --> 01:57:21.390
There's also a big inequality agenda here

01:57:21.390 --> 01:57:23.010
and some of the statistics there,

01:57:23.010 --> 01:57:24.600
again, are very concerning.

01:57:24.600 --> 01:57:25.650
And I think the point,

01:57:25.650 --> 01:57:26.940
I'm sure not sure who made it,

01:57:26.940 --> 01:57:29.280
but you know, kids don't
have the loudest voice

01:57:29.280 --> 01:57:31.290
and that's why it's really important,

01:57:31.290 --> 01:57:32.220
I think, that we hear it.

01:57:32.220 --> 01:57:34.480
I think the youth board is fantastic

01:57:34.480 --> 01:57:35.787
and that is a voice

01:57:35.787 --> 01:57:38.970
and they certainly make their voice heard.

01:57:38.970 --> 01:57:41.077
But fundamentally, I mean when the paper

01:57:41.077 --> 01:57:42.480
came to the Quality Committee,

01:57:42.480 --> 01:57:44.177
there was a very good set
of actions at the end.

01:57:44.177 --> 01:57:45.330
They're not here,

01:57:45.330 --> 01:57:47.460
but there are clearly
actions and solutions

01:57:47.460 --> 01:57:48.692
that you have in mind.

01:57:48.692 --> 01:57:50.760
It's a matter of whether we can invest

01:57:50.760 --> 01:57:51.960
appropriately in them.

01:57:51.960 --> 01:57:53.520
So I think the reason,

01:57:53.520 --> 01:57:55.320
I think, this is timely and important

01:57:55.320 --> 01:57:57.900
is because we are thinking 10 years ahead.

01:57:57.900 --> 01:57:59.160
- Yeah.
- With the 10-Year Plan.

01:57:59.160 --> 01:58:01.800
And I just really think
we have to give this

01:58:01.800 --> 01:58:04.920
enormous, enormous emphasis.

01:58:04.920 --> 01:58:06.261
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

01:58:06.261 --> 01:58:08.430
So the leap on the bandwagon,

01:58:08.430 --> 01:58:09.630
I'm delighted to see the emphasis

01:58:09.630 --> 01:58:11.141
on children and young people

01:58:11.141 --> 01:58:13.366
because I just don't talk
about young people enough.

01:58:13.366 --> 01:58:15.990
I chaired one of the work
streams of the 10-Year Plan

01:58:15.990 --> 01:58:18.030
and Simon and the team came and spoke

01:58:18.030 --> 01:58:20.130
to our work stream

01:58:20.130 --> 01:58:21.780
and it was incredibly impactful.

01:58:21.780 --> 01:58:23.520
And we took three
things, I think, from it.

01:58:23.520 --> 01:58:25.380
One, this was that, yes,

01:58:25.380 --> 01:58:27.600
children are involved in
all bits of our service,

01:58:27.600 --> 01:58:29.910
including frailty, which I
think we sometimes forget.

01:58:29.910 --> 01:58:30.743
Some of our children can be some

01:58:30.743 --> 01:58:32.880
of our most complex children.

01:58:32.880 --> 01:58:36.054
And just by assuming that
they've got the same condition,

01:58:36.054 --> 01:58:39.030
unless you specifically
design the services

01:58:39.030 --> 01:58:40.220
to work for children and young people,

01:58:40.220 --> 01:58:42.450
we will it and we'll get it wrong.

01:58:42.450 --> 01:58:44.280
The second thing was the
importance of transition

01:58:44.280 --> 01:58:46.230
from children's services
into adult services

01:58:46.230 --> 01:58:49.047
and you can't start planning
early enough for that.

01:58:49.047 --> 01:58:51.030
And then thirdly, it was really just this,

01:58:51.030 --> 01:58:51.930
if we get this wrong,

01:58:51.930 --> 01:58:53.980
this will impact upon a whole generation.

01:58:55.740 --> 01:58:59.835
- Thank you, Mike? You, Wol?

01:58:59.835 --> 01:59:03.030
- Yeah, so thank you for the paper.

01:59:03.030 --> 01:59:04.380
Thank you for the comments.

01:59:05.957 --> 01:59:09.793
So when I was chairing Guy's
and St Thomas' Foundation,

01:59:09.793 --> 01:59:11.760
I did a huge amount of work

01:59:11.760 --> 01:59:14.380
on things like childhood obesity,

01:59:14.380 --> 01:59:17.280
children's mental health.

01:59:17.280 --> 01:59:20.310
And the bit which I'm
sort of wanting more from

01:59:20.310 --> 01:59:21.720
is just to understand more

01:59:21.720 --> 01:59:23.850
about how you are working with people

01:59:23.850 --> 01:59:26.040
out of our system

01:59:26.040 --> 01:59:27.900
that have already done a lot of this work

01:59:27.900 --> 01:59:30.090
and got sort of areas of expertise

01:59:30.090 --> 01:59:32.190
and understanding of how some stuff works.

01:59:33.240 --> 01:59:34.620
Because I didn't get enough of it

01:59:34.620 --> 01:59:36.960
from what you actually
wrote or what you said.

01:59:36.960 --> 01:59:39.180
Because, I mean, I could almost,

01:59:39.180 --> 01:59:41.490
you know, in my head I know
the phrases we're using,

01:59:41.490 --> 01:59:43.500
which is, you know, it's
not about willpower,

01:59:43.500 --> 01:59:44.640
it's about environment

01:59:44.640 --> 01:59:46.110
when it comes to obesity.

01:59:46.110 --> 01:59:48.564
It's about the housing situation.

01:59:48.564 --> 01:59:50.100
It's about the parental situation.

01:59:50.100 --> 01:59:51.750
It's about the cultural situation,

01:59:51.750 --> 01:59:52.920
which is far more determined

01:59:52.920 --> 01:59:54.510
than anything we say to children

01:59:54.510 --> 01:59:57.411
and that crossover with
the inequalities as well.

01:59:57.411 --> 01:59:59.550
So the Core5PLUS20 or 20PLUS5,

01:59:59.550 --> 02:00:01.170
whichever way around is, I can't remember.

02:00:01.170 --> 02:00:04.020
But that also was in that,

02:00:04.020 --> 02:00:05.580
it's a very complex soup

02:00:05.580 --> 02:00:06.960
and I'm just sort of puzzled

02:00:06.960 --> 02:00:09.360
why it wasn't more about
what other people are doing

02:00:09.360 --> 02:00:10.710
in your paper.

02:00:10.710 --> 02:00:13.680
Maybe you tell me it's just
haven't said it, thank you.

02:00:13.680 --> 02:00:14.760
- You're okay picking these up?

02:00:14.760 --> 02:00:16.575
And then I think Mary,
I think you wanted to.

02:00:16.575 --> 02:00:18.163
- Thank you, just two things.

02:00:18.163 --> 02:00:21.030
I think under 10C where you are developing

02:00:21.030 --> 02:00:22.170
a metric for measuring

02:00:22.170 --> 02:00:23.250
waiting times
- Yeah.

02:00:23.250 --> 02:00:25.110
- for community mental health services,

02:00:25.110 --> 02:00:28.080
absolutely fantastic, we've never done it.

02:00:28.080 --> 02:00:30.987
And I just want to commend you on that.

02:00:30.987 --> 02:00:32.970
And my other question

02:00:32.970 --> 02:00:35.318
is this paper covering
the nought to fives?

02:00:35.318 --> 02:00:37.666
Because we do know that children

02:00:37.666 --> 02:00:40.050
are going to primary school

02:00:40.050 --> 02:00:42.840
without sufficient language skills.

02:00:42.840 --> 02:00:45.000
I mean not a high proportion

02:00:45.000 --> 02:00:47.070
but a concerning proportion

02:00:47.070 --> 02:00:50.433
and some even not out of nappies.

02:00:51.300 --> 02:00:52.641
There is some evidence

02:00:52.641 --> 02:00:56.640
that health visiting numbers
have been significantly cut

02:00:56.640 --> 02:00:58.890
disproportionately across the country.

02:00:58.890 --> 02:01:01.490
And I just wonder whether
you could comment on that.

02:01:02.670 --> 02:01:04.167
- So Simon and Duncan,

02:01:04.167 --> 02:01:06.410
I don't know how you
want to pick those up,

02:01:06.410 --> 02:01:07.993
but you'll have to sort
of signal across each,

02:01:07.993 --> 02:01:08.857
I think.
- Yeah, seamlessly as I go.

02:01:08.857 --> 02:01:11.486
- So now we get some of
those comments or questions.

02:01:11.486 --> 02:01:12.900
- [Simon] I'll answer the easy questions.

02:01:12.900 --> 02:01:14.259
- Good boy, good move, yeah.

02:01:14.259 --> 02:01:18.330
- So, it you could,

02:01:18.330 --> 02:01:21.090
and actually it addresses
several of the comments actually,

02:01:21.090 --> 02:01:23.970
which is this starts off
as a much longer paper

02:01:23.970 --> 02:01:26.374
and it necessary has to be trimmed down.

02:01:26.374 --> 02:01:28.560
Because essentially, what we're covering

02:01:28.560 --> 02:01:31.334
is almost every activity
that the NHS does.

02:01:31.334 --> 02:01:33.690
So it could be a separate board in itself

02:01:33.690 --> 02:01:34.523
doing the whole thing

02:01:34.523 --> 02:01:37.681
and I'm very willing for us to do that.

02:01:37.681 --> 02:01:39.380
But teeth are definitely there

02:01:39.380 --> 02:01:41.131
and there's a bit about elective recovery,

02:01:41.131 --> 02:01:45.690
but every child that has its teeth removed

02:01:45.690 --> 02:01:48.360
is a failure of public health.

02:01:48.360 --> 02:01:51.000
And it's how we work with
public health colleagues

02:01:51.000 --> 02:01:52.078
in terms of reducing that.

02:01:52.078 --> 02:01:53.970
There's things like
Mini Mouth Care Matters,

02:01:53.970 --> 02:01:56.010
assisted toothbrushing in schools.

02:01:56.010 --> 02:01:56.910
- Yeah.
- There are initiatives.

02:01:56.910 --> 02:02:01.200
And in fact, the oral health
of children is improving

02:02:01.200 --> 02:02:05.430
that there is a massive deprivation signal

02:02:05.430 --> 02:02:06.978
coming out of there.

02:02:06.978 --> 02:02:09.637
With regards to external review,

02:02:09.637 --> 02:02:13.590
we've got within the governance,

02:02:13.590 --> 02:02:15.510
we've have external stakeholder bodies

02:02:15.510 --> 02:02:18.960
that provide us with professional
assistance and support.

02:02:18.960 --> 02:02:22.271
We work with things like the
Academy of Medical Sciences

02:02:22.271 --> 02:02:24.690
and the Royal Foundation

02:02:24.690 --> 02:02:26.701
to generate best evidence to guide us

02:02:26.701 --> 02:02:30.990
in where we go in terms
of future strategies.

02:02:30.990 --> 02:02:33.000
And the metrics

02:02:33.000 --> 02:02:34.890
for community waiting
times, absolutely agree.

02:02:34.890 --> 02:02:36.420
I think we could probably go further,

02:02:36.420 --> 02:02:38.190
it would be great to have something

02:02:38.190 --> 02:02:40.610
like secondary user
statistics in the community

02:02:40.610 --> 02:02:43.380
as sort of real live
recording at the moment.

02:02:43.380 --> 02:02:45.150
This is still quite a manual process

02:02:45.150 --> 02:02:46.920
which we'll be going through

02:02:46.920 --> 02:02:50.190
and I'll hand over to
Duncan for health visitors.

02:02:50.190 --> 02:02:51.586
- Great, just before I do that,

02:02:51.586 --> 02:02:54.342
'cause somebody mentioned electives,

02:02:54.342 --> 02:02:55.290
so there's something,

02:02:55.290 --> 02:02:56.689
actually it's in the paper,

02:02:56.689 --> 02:02:58.560
but the importance of visibility of this

02:02:58.560 --> 02:02:59.550
and the visibility data

02:02:59.550 --> 02:03:00.720
and there's obviously a commitment

02:03:00.720 --> 02:03:02.040
within the elective recovery plan

02:03:02.040 --> 02:03:05.010
about the visibility of
children young people's weights,

02:03:05.010 --> 02:03:07.120
but also about the importance of ICBs

02:03:09.835 --> 02:03:11.310
focused on that as well.

02:03:11.310 --> 02:03:13.050
So I think that's a really critical thing

02:03:13.050 --> 02:03:15.900
that we need to make sure is there.

02:03:15.900 --> 02:03:17.400
And then also, just
reflecting on the point

02:03:17.400 --> 02:03:20.545
about this isn't a paper
that's covering every element,

02:03:20.545 --> 02:03:22.691
'cause we'd be here all afternoon,

02:03:22.691 --> 02:03:24.210
but there is something then

02:03:24.210 --> 02:03:25.980
about perhaps how do we do that

02:03:25.980 --> 02:03:27.375
when we do see a paper about dental

02:03:27.375 --> 02:03:28.756
or how we see a paper about

02:03:28.756 --> 02:03:30.462
as we go forward is just thinking

02:03:30.462 --> 02:03:34.197
about what goes in our
papers in future things

02:03:34.197 --> 02:03:35.850
and about the needs of children,

02:03:35.850 --> 02:03:37.110
like we try and cover the needs

02:03:37.110 --> 02:03:39.760
of inequalities, et cetera.

02:03:39.760 --> 02:03:41.745
On health visiting, absolutely,

02:03:41.745 --> 02:03:43.710
and there's clearly a
government commitment

02:03:43.710 --> 02:03:45.270
around health visiting.

02:03:45.270 --> 02:03:47.400
This is obviously local
authority commission,

02:03:47.400 --> 02:03:50.130
but you may probably know

02:03:50.130 --> 02:03:51.630
that the professional leadership

02:03:51.630 --> 02:03:53.805
of public health nursing has moved

02:03:53.805 --> 02:03:55.979
to the chief nursing
officer about a year ago.

02:03:55.979 --> 02:03:57.960
And so I'm working very closely

02:03:57.960 --> 02:04:01.297
with the Office of the
Chief Public Health Nurse

02:04:01.297 --> 02:04:05.143
on what we do around health
visiting particularly.

02:04:05.143 --> 02:04:06.180
And as Simon mentioned,

02:04:06.180 --> 02:04:08.450
the Royal Foundation is doing
some really interesting work

02:04:08.450 --> 02:04:09.990
in supporting health system as well

02:04:09.990 --> 02:04:11.367
and we've been talking to them.

02:04:11.367 --> 02:04:13.872
So I think there's a lot
more to do in this space.

02:04:13.872 --> 02:04:16.110
And partly that is about the visibility

02:04:16.110 --> 02:04:17.100
of the work that they do,

02:04:17.100 --> 02:04:18.870
it's evidence-based.

02:04:18.870 --> 02:04:21.720
And certainly, that's something
that I'll be focusing on.

02:04:23.380 --> 02:04:26.280
- One question I think
is a Julian question

02:04:26.280 --> 02:04:27.280
as much as anything.

02:04:28.500 --> 02:04:30.900
There's something Mark said there.

02:04:30.900 --> 02:04:35.670
This population has the highest

02:04:35.670 --> 02:04:38.470
net present cost of their entire lifetime

02:04:39.690 --> 02:04:42.810
and the lowest voice versus,

02:04:42.810 --> 02:04:46.440
and Mark, you said an
older cohort which has-

02:04:46.440 --> 02:04:49.230
- Your second childhood.
- In their second childhood,

02:04:49.230 --> 02:04:51.420
probably the largest voice
in our society at the moment

02:04:51.420 --> 02:04:54.120
because of demographics and voting

02:04:54.120 --> 02:04:56.310
and yet the least benefit.

02:04:56.310 --> 02:04:58.710
How do we, as a board,

02:04:58.710 --> 02:05:00.660
face into that funding mismatch?

02:05:00.660 --> 02:05:02.880
And because this is really
a marginal allocation

02:05:02.880 --> 02:05:04.560
of resources question.

02:05:04.560 --> 02:05:08.070
And in a world where there
isn't enough funding,

02:05:08.070 --> 02:05:10.020
- It's prioritisation.
- It feels like

02:05:11.010 --> 02:05:13.020
how do we make this much more explicit

02:05:13.020 --> 02:05:15.720
in our recommendations in
the way we make decisions?

02:05:15.720 --> 02:05:17.100
- So I think it's a very good-

02:05:17.100 --> 02:05:18.000
I mean, I do think,

02:05:18.000 --> 02:05:20.730
and I then I might get Amanda to comment,

02:05:20.730 --> 02:05:22.980
but I think it is a terrible word

02:05:22.980 --> 02:05:24.540
of prioritisation, isn't it?

02:05:24.540 --> 02:05:27.326
It's what do we choose to do,

02:05:27.326 --> 02:05:28.830
you know, guided by government,

02:05:28.830 --> 02:05:29.910
in conversation with government,

02:05:29.910 --> 02:05:33.240
what do we choose to prioritise

02:05:33.240 --> 02:05:35.130
because we can't do everything.

02:05:35.130 --> 02:05:37.560
So what do we choose to do?

02:05:37.560 --> 02:05:39.660
And I actually hear you on the, you know,

02:05:39.660 --> 02:05:41.010
this is perhaps the smallest voice,

02:05:41.010 --> 02:05:44.760
but the best return investment.

02:05:44.760 --> 02:05:48.990
So it is that debate on prioritisation.

02:05:48.990 --> 02:05:50.190
The other one, which we
didn't really pick up

02:05:50.190 --> 02:05:51.840
in that conversation, was asked I think,

02:05:51.840 --> 02:05:54.393
but actually it's partnering.

02:05:55.290 --> 02:05:58.050
And so if there's one cohort,

02:05:58.050 --> 02:05:59.760
I mean the many cohorts of the population,

02:05:59.760 --> 02:06:00.840
but there's one cohort I think

02:06:00.840 --> 02:06:03.990
where actually that partnering
is so really important.

02:06:03.990 --> 02:06:05.010
Whether it's I know you do it,

02:06:05.010 --> 02:06:07.800
whether it's with key workers in schools,

02:06:07.800 --> 02:06:09.750
you know, going into schools.

02:06:09.750 --> 02:06:11.520
But it's also in policy

02:06:11.520 --> 02:06:13.200
that actually when we think
about what's happening,

02:06:13.200 --> 02:06:14.640
how many centres have we now got

02:06:14.640 --> 02:06:16.950
for severely obese children?

02:06:16.950 --> 02:06:18.367
Is it 30?

02:06:18.367 --> 02:06:20.589
- 37.
- 37 clinics we've opened

02:06:20.589 --> 02:06:25.589
for severely obese young children.

02:06:26.820 --> 02:06:29.394
So there's a lot of policy
stuff actually in here

02:06:29.394 --> 02:06:33.429
where we really need to
partner with government.

02:06:33.429 --> 02:06:34.770
'Cause see, there's a cohort

02:06:34.770 --> 02:06:36.270
where, you know, what's that stat?

02:06:36.270 --> 02:06:39.360
20% of health conditions
that derived from health

02:06:39.360 --> 02:06:41.370
and 80% of health conditions derived

02:06:41.370 --> 02:06:43.830
from external factors.

02:06:43.830 --> 02:06:45.960
So I'm probably naive,

02:06:45.960 --> 02:06:47.750
but it strikes me as a cohort

02:06:47.750 --> 02:06:50.580
of the population where absolutely,

02:06:50.580 --> 02:06:53.850
one should be encouraging those partners,

02:06:53.850 --> 02:06:56.706
the partners who are an act as of policy.

02:06:56.706 --> 02:06:58.752
It would be for this cohort,

02:06:58.752 --> 02:07:00.420
whether it's ultra-processed foods,

02:07:00.420 --> 02:07:01.890
whether it's, you know, I don't know,

02:07:01.890 --> 02:07:03.487
Simon would have a better
view social media on them.

02:07:03.487 --> 02:07:07.920
You know, the surging levels
of demand in mental health,

02:07:07.920 --> 02:07:10.487
the private market in diagnosis,

02:07:10.487 --> 02:07:12.163
you know, these things

02:07:12.163 --> 02:07:14.820
actually are the things that we can do

02:07:14.820 --> 02:07:17.347
that can actually alleviate
some of these pressures

02:07:17.347 --> 02:07:21.510
and look after these young
people better and quicker,

02:07:21.510 --> 02:07:23.010
I think would be really important.

02:07:23.010 --> 02:07:25.557
But you can react to that

02:07:25.557 --> 02:07:27.579
or Simon, you want to come in

02:07:27.579 --> 02:07:29.311
or ignore me.

02:07:29.311 --> 02:07:31.350
But Simon, do you want
come in, then Amanda?

02:07:31.350 --> 02:07:34.452
- I mean, just say obviously
the bits on mental health

02:07:34.452 --> 02:07:36.990
don't put much in about

02:07:36.990 --> 02:07:39.270
the remarkable increase in demand

02:07:39.270 --> 02:07:41.125
in a remarkably short period,

02:07:41.125 --> 02:07:43.440
which if it continues,

02:07:43.440 --> 02:07:45.540
which is every sign it might,

02:07:45.540 --> 02:07:48.840
there's no way we'll ever
catch up our tail ever.

02:07:48.840 --> 02:07:51.030
What we should be doing about that?

02:07:51.030 --> 02:07:52.230
There's nothing in that.

02:07:53.580 --> 02:07:56.466
- Yeah, so I think it's a really long,

02:07:56.466 --> 02:07:58.740
it's difficult question

02:07:58.740 --> 02:08:00.000
and there's not any evidence.

02:08:00.000 --> 02:08:02.670
So we need to make a diagnosis
as to why it's changed.

02:08:02.670 --> 02:08:04.620
- Yeah.
- It's taking place

02:08:04.620 --> 02:08:06.540
and we have to make
that diagnosis in order

02:08:06.540 --> 02:08:09.960
and start to establish an
evidence base about what works

02:08:09.960 --> 02:08:12.180
and start to think about how we can-

02:08:12.180 --> 02:08:13.260
And I think a lot of it

02:08:13.260 --> 02:08:15.872
will probably go back to the early years

02:08:15.872 --> 02:08:19.050
and how we look after our children

02:08:19.050 --> 02:08:20.835
in the first five years of life, you know.

02:08:20.835 --> 02:08:22.605
And I just reiterate

02:08:22.605 --> 02:08:24.476
earlier intervention.
- Yeah.

02:08:24.476 --> 02:08:28.680
- And gendering a generation of children

02:08:28.680 --> 02:08:30.870
that have got mental resilience

02:08:30.870 --> 02:08:32.610
and emotional resilience

02:08:32.610 --> 02:08:34.110
and that's not something

02:08:34.110 --> 02:08:37.110
NHS England can prescribe, we can help.

02:08:37.110 --> 02:08:39.237
I think that's where a lot of
the effort will need to go.

02:08:39.237 --> 02:08:41.610
- And you helped me with that
speech I had to make, I know,

02:08:41.610 --> 02:08:45.000
three or four months ago and
actually that the deprivation

02:08:45.000 --> 02:08:46.800
and the connectivity into deprivation

02:08:46.800 --> 02:08:48.180
and the inequalities here

02:08:48.180 --> 02:08:50.220
and the number of children,
I forget the stat now,

02:08:50.220 --> 02:08:52.617
but it's an appalling stat actually,

02:08:52.617 --> 02:08:54.488
the number of children

02:08:54.488 --> 02:08:56.130
living in poverty and absolute poverty.

02:08:56.130 --> 02:08:57.900
You know, it's a real indictment

02:08:57.900 --> 02:09:00.030
of the way our society actually operates.

02:09:00.030 --> 02:09:02.883
I think we need to find
ways to intervene, I think.

02:09:04.860 --> 02:09:06.090
Are we okay to?

02:09:06.090 --> 02:09:07.608
Amanda, sorry, yes, please do.

02:09:07.608 --> 02:09:10.170
- Right, so just 'cause you've asked

02:09:10.170 --> 02:09:12.003
some really great questions

02:09:12.003 --> 02:09:14.160
that I think are probably worth me

02:09:14.160 --> 02:09:15.810
just giving a bit of a perspective on.

02:09:15.810 --> 02:09:17.580
But firstly, can I just say, again,

02:09:17.580 --> 02:09:19.691
thank you to Simon and the
team who I think have done

02:09:19.691 --> 02:09:22.020
and you just have to continue to do

02:09:22.020 --> 02:09:23.880
just the most brilliant job

02:09:23.880 --> 02:09:26.373
and for bringing such a, well,

02:09:27.600 --> 02:09:28.650
covering so much ground

02:09:28.650 --> 02:09:31.920
in such a very well
crafted and focused paper.

02:09:31.920 --> 02:09:34.983
So I know it could have
been many times longer.

02:09:36.360 --> 02:09:38.610
Clearly, I think this question

02:09:38.610 --> 02:09:41.100
about what is the approach
prioritisation, et cetera,

02:09:41.100 --> 02:09:43.500
just to kind of take that one head on.

02:09:43.500 --> 02:09:46.410
So when we were talking earlier
about planning guidance,

02:09:46.410 --> 02:09:48.960
this year we have asked the NHS

02:09:48.960 --> 02:09:51.150
to focus on fewer priorities

02:09:51.150 --> 02:09:52.950
and we have collapsed ring fences

02:09:52.950 --> 02:09:54.630
because we have said

02:09:54.630 --> 02:09:57.885
to give our local leaders
maximum opportunity

02:09:57.885 --> 02:10:01.410
to work out locally
what the priorities are

02:10:01.410 --> 02:10:03.240
and to deliver the things that matter most

02:10:03.240 --> 02:10:05.220
in the ways it make best sense.

02:10:05.220 --> 02:10:07.890
We have to untie their hands.

02:10:07.890 --> 02:10:10.410
So we have to see that through.

02:10:10.410 --> 02:10:13.165
And what that actually means,
I think, is two things,

02:10:13.165 --> 02:10:14.970
three things for us.

02:10:14.970 --> 02:10:18.030
One, and I think Duncan,
absolutely bang on.

02:10:18.030 --> 02:10:22.530
We have got to embed explicitly
children and young people

02:10:22.530 --> 02:10:25.230
and inequalities in our core business.

02:10:25.230 --> 02:10:29.040
So we should not be able
to talk about elective UEC,

02:10:29.040 --> 02:10:31.590
primary care access without
talking about those things.

02:10:31.590 --> 02:10:33.840
And obviously, in our big data packs,

02:10:33.840 --> 02:10:36.515
we do cut the data by inequalities

02:10:36.515 --> 02:10:38.850
and we've made a commitment to do

02:10:38.850 --> 02:10:40.767
that specifically on elective care

02:10:40.767 --> 02:10:42.150
for children and young people,

02:10:42.150 --> 02:10:43.350
just need to keep going,

02:10:43.350 --> 02:10:44.508
I would say all that.

02:10:44.508 --> 02:10:46.350
Second thing is we do,

02:10:46.350 --> 02:10:49.410
there need to be clear
how we need to adapt

02:10:49.410 --> 02:10:53.160
the approach that's
happening in adult services

02:10:53.160 --> 02:10:54.444
so that it also works for children

02:10:54.444 --> 02:10:56.747
because one size does not fit all here.

02:10:56.747 --> 02:10:58.260
And I'm thinking of things

02:10:58.260 --> 02:11:00.704
like virtual wards for kids,

02:11:00.704 --> 02:11:03.840
the NDTs for children and
young people, you know,

02:11:03.840 --> 02:11:05.130
there's a lot of similarities

02:11:05.130 --> 02:11:06.720
but they will need to work differently.

02:11:06.720 --> 02:11:08.550
And so I think there's an and

02:11:08.550 --> 02:11:10.140
which is the bit that says, right,

02:11:10.140 --> 02:11:11.552
if we're doing a set of things

02:11:11.552 --> 02:11:14.550
which are set out very clearly

02:11:14.550 --> 02:11:17.490
for tackling these big things,

02:11:17.490 --> 02:11:20.310
what's the specific guidance
or the shape of that

02:11:20.310 --> 02:11:21.300
that needs to be in place

02:11:21.300 --> 02:11:22.740
so that it's going to deliver

02:11:22.740 --> 02:11:24.810
for children and young people.

02:11:24.810 --> 02:11:26.550
And then I think thirdly,

02:11:26.550 --> 02:11:27.690
exactly as this paper,

02:11:27.690 --> 02:11:30.700
is we need to be sort
of really crystal clear.

02:11:30.700 --> 02:11:33.420
It's your point about diagnosis, Simon,

02:11:33.420 --> 02:11:38.420
where the and above that is
there is a specific thing

02:11:38.940 --> 02:11:39.840
for children and young people

02:11:39.840 --> 02:11:41.880
isn't covered by those other things

02:11:41.880 --> 02:11:45.965
where we would actually say
this one needs attention.

02:11:45.965 --> 02:11:47.730
And whilst local areas

02:11:47.730 --> 02:11:49.769
might have a slightly
different priority set,

02:11:49.769 --> 02:11:53.010
we would be surprised if
this wasn't included in it,

02:11:53.010 --> 02:11:55.350
given what we know about what's
going across the country.

02:11:55.350 --> 02:11:56.670
So for example,

02:11:56.670 --> 02:11:58.111
the specialist obesity service

02:11:58.111 --> 02:11:59.370
would be a good example of that

02:11:59.370 --> 02:12:00.420
where it is different

02:12:00.420 --> 02:12:01.830
to something that we're
doing for adult services,

02:12:01.830 --> 02:12:04.322
completely different and a clear priority.

02:12:04.322 --> 02:12:07.800
I think two more things, if I might.

02:12:07.800 --> 02:12:10.620
So data is key

02:12:10.620 --> 02:12:11.850
and I actually think that's, again,

02:12:11.850 --> 02:12:12.840
it comes through the paper,

02:12:12.840 --> 02:12:14.790
but the more that we can do

02:12:14.790 --> 02:12:17.380
to make sure that we are
capturing the right data,

02:12:17.380 --> 02:12:20.190
the right data quality and publishing it,

02:12:20.190 --> 02:12:22.080
thinking about community services

02:12:22.080 --> 02:12:24.150
as well as I suppose
some of the other things

02:12:24.150 --> 02:12:25.308
we've talked about,

02:12:25.308 --> 02:12:26.442
I think that is one of the things

02:12:26.442 --> 02:12:28.617
that we, as NHS England, can do to help.

02:12:28.617 --> 02:12:30.150
And then my other point,

02:12:30.150 --> 02:12:31.170
which I think you made Richard,

02:12:31.170 --> 02:12:32.580
is just to keep coming back

02:12:32.580 --> 02:12:35.100
to how much of this requires partnership.

02:12:35.100 --> 02:12:36.661
So I mean, speaking personally

02:12:36.661 --> 02:12:39.120
and others might be thinking it, you know,

02:12:39.120 --> 02:12:40.860
I think there was huge merit

02:12:40.860 --> 02:12:42.390
in some of the Sure Start work

02:12:42.390 --> 02:12:44.460
that was done many years ago.

02:12:44.460 --> 02:12:46.230
But of course, that was
a partnership approach.

02:12:46.230 --> 02:12:47.940
And you know, some of that, I think,

02:12:47.940 --> 02:12:51.240
it, you know, has huge
potential for the future.

02:12:51.240 --> 02:12:52.939
That's not our decision to make,

02:12:52.939 --> 02:12:54.480
but it's something that I think

02:12:54.480 --> 02:12:55.470
where that's to happen,

02:12:55.470 --> 02:12:58.200
we would hugely enthusiastic about.

02:12:58.200 --> 02:12:59.700
- It's interesting that one of the new

02:12:59.700 --> 02:13:01.500
and newly announced non-execs

02:13:01.500 --> 02:13:02.520
on the Department of Health

02:13:02.520 --> 02:13:05.791
was the first director
of Sure Start as well.

02:13:05.791 --> 02:13:07.741
- Right, well perhaps,

02:13:07.741 --> 02:13:10.607
we're not misaligned then on thinking

02:13:10.607 --> 02:13:13.431
about the importance of
that kind of initiative.

02:13:13.431 --> 02:13:16.430
So I think there are some
huge opportunities in future

02:13:16.430 --> 02:13:18.330
in the partnership space,

02:13:18.330 --> 02:13:20.130
but I think we also just
have to be, you know,

02:13:20.130 --> 02:13:21.930
we just have to recognise
how much pressure

02:13:21.930 --> 02:13:23.520
other partners are under.

02:13:23.520 --> 02:13:24.660
So partners in education,

02:13:24.660 --> 02:13:26.730
partners in local
government, voluntary sector,

02:13:26.730 --> 02:13:27.960
you know, we are not alone

02:13:27.960 --> 02:13:31.704
in needing to think
about how we best respond

02:13:31.704 --> 02:13:33.870
to some of the growing
demands on our services.

02:13:33.870 --> 02:13:35.670
But I think partnership's going to be key.

02:13:35.670 --> 02:13:37.630
And finally, Richard, I was just thinking

02:13:37.630 --> 02:13:39.382
in light of this conversation

02:13:39.382 --> 02:13:41.790
and the reflection,
which I think is right,

02:13:41.790 --> 02:13:44.340
that this board probably has not spent

02:13:44.340 --> 02:13:46.350
as much time as colleagues would like

02:13:46.350 --> 02:13:48.107
on to issues related to known people.

02:13:48.107 --> 02:13:50.341
Perhaps, we could agree to come back

02:13:50.341 --> 02:13:52.650
to this agenda more frequently.

02:13:52.650 --> 02:13:54.195
- Pick up the rhythm.
- Just given

02:13:54.195 --> 02:13:55.530
this is my last board

02:13:55.530 --> 02:13:57.150
and I wouldn't be part that discussion,

02:13:57.150 --> 02:14:00.330
just add one one wider to
that, which is, I think,

02:14:00.330 --> 02:14:02.760
Mark, you started touching on this,

02:14:02.760 --> 02:14:04.830
and Mary, the investment you make

02:14:04.830 --> 02:14:06.330
for the return down the road

02:14:06.330 --> 02:14:08.220
is the big challenge here

02:14:08.220 --> 02:14:09.849
and how that actually plays in

02:14:09.849 --> 02:14:11.520
when you're talking about
the financial pressures

02:14:11.520 --> 02:14:13.800
which Julian's so clearly outlined for us,

02:14:13.800 --> 02:14:15.330
I think that is part
of the conundrum here.

02:14:15.330 --> 02:14:17.880
You have current day spending going on

02:14:17.880 --> 02:14:19.920
and all the pressures that come from that.

02:14:19.920 --> 02:14:21.900
But actually when you think I
need to put that extra pound

02:14:21.900 --> 02:14:24.501
into putting more towards
children and young people

02:14:24.501 --> 02:14:26.580
or towards the elderly offer,

02:14:26.580 --> 02:14:27.630
what's the balance here?

02:14:27.630 --> 02:14:28.463
What's the immediate return?

02:14:28.463 --> 02:14:29.670
Because if I don't do it,

02:14:29.670 --> 02:14:31.710
I'm just giving up
opportunities down the road.

02:14:31.710 --> 02:14:33.540
So I think finding a
way of describing that

02:14:33.540 --> 02:14:35.190
and discussing that in
your next conversation,

02:14:35.190 --> 02:14:36.333
he says very happily.

02:14:37.364 --> 02:14:38.395
- Yeah, at the next

02:14:38.395 --> 02:14:39.840
board discussion.
- The next board discussion.

02:14:39.840 --> 02:14:41.103
- Load up the agenda.

02:14:41.103 --> 02:14:43.380
Well, would be great, thank you.

02:14:43.380 --> 02:14:46.260
Okay, Simon, thank you very much.

02:14:46.260 --> 02:14:48.090
Really very good, Duncan, thank you.

02:14:48.090 --> 02:14:49.800
So should we move on to genomics?

02:14:49.800 --> 02:14:51.560
- I think you give him the last word.

02:14:51.560 --> 02:14:52.732
- Oh yes, sorry,

02:14:52.732 --> 02:14:54.042
would you like the last word

02:14:54.042 --> 02:14:58.380
on that conversation? Final word?

02:14:58.380 --> 02:14:59.730
The last word at this board.

02:14:59.730 --> 02:15:01.856
- Just thanks for hearing

02:15:01.856 --> 02:15:04.020
and it's always difficult as an adult

02:15:04.020 --> 02:15:06.030
representing children and young people

02:15:06.030 --> 02:15:07.200
and I'd also really like,

02:15:07.200 --> 02:15:09.180
I'd have liked to have had
some of the youth board members

02:15:09.180 --> 02:15:10.013
sit here against me.
- Right.

02:15:10.013 --> 02:15:12.000
- There's at least one potential

02:15:12.000 --> 02:15:13.400
prime minister in the group.

02:15:15.060 --> 02:15:16.560
I'll relay what the conversation,

02:15:16.560 --> 02:15:17.970
I'm meeting them just after this meeting.

02:15:17.970 --> 02:15:19.101
So.
- Excellent.

02:15:19.101 --> 02:15:21.150
- [Amanda] Should we
do that for next time?

02:15:21.150 --> 02:15:22.200
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

02:15:22.200 --> 02:15:25.200
Well, yes but it's in
Leeds so it's probably.

02:15:25.200 --> 02:15:28.389
- Ah, but then for the next
time we have this conversation.

02:15:28.389 --> 02:15:29.689
- Yeah then that-
- So I think

02:15:29.689 --> 02:15:31.599
- definitely.
- that wouldn't be the next

02:15:31.599 --> 02:15:32.730
board meeting.
- No,

02:15:32.730 --> 02:15:34.230
next time they come up on the agenda,

02:15:34.230 --> 02:15:36.270
then we should bring that
into that discussion.

02:15:36.270 --> 02:15:38.790
Yep, very good.

02:15:38.790 --> 02:15:40.740
- Chair, I was just going
to say in the same vein.

02:15:40.740 --> 02:15:43.290
If there's an opportunity
when we next come back to this

02:15:43.290 --> 02:15:45.810
to kind of hear about some
of the other initiatives

02:15:45.810 --> 02:15:48.570
that in the wider kind of society

02:15:48.570 --> 02:15:50.010
that might be going on to support this,

02:15:50.010 --> 02:15:51.780
whether it's around, you know,

02:15:51.780 --> 02:15:54.804
early year support or reducing
the obesogenic environment,

02:15:54.804 --> 02:15:57.300
some of that kind things.
- Well, school workers.

02:15:57.300 --> 02:15:58.980
- I think it would be
really, really interesting

02:15:58.980 --> 02:16:01.260
to see if we could have
a kind of conversation

02:16:01.260 --> 02:16:02.820
in the round around that.

02:16:02.820 --> 02:16:04.247
- Very good, thank you.

02:16:04.247 --> 02:16:06.210
Okay, so we are going to move on

02:16:06.210 --> 02:16:09.128
to NHS Genomic Medicine Service.

02:16:09.128 --> 02:16:11.910
Sue, it's over to you.

02:16:11.910 --> 02:16:13.170
- Thank you very much Richard

02:16:13.170 --> 02:16:15.440
for asking me to speak this afternoon

02:16:15.440 --> 02:16:20.400
about our world-leading NHS
Genomic Medicine Service

02:16:20.400 --> 02:16:22.680
or GMS for short.

02:16:22.680 --> 02:16:24.630
And we are really world-leading.

02:16:24.630 --> 02:16:25.950
We are five years ahead

02:16:25.950 --> 02:16:28.680
of any other country across the world.

02:16:28.680 --> 02:16:30.194
And I really want to thank

02:16:30.194 --> 02:16:35.010
Richard, Amanda, the
board and the exec team

02:16:35.010 --> 02:16:37.470
for all their support they've given me

02:16:37.470 --> 02:16:39.604
in driving forward this agenda

02:16:39.604 --> 02:16:42.810
as well as the staff of the NHS GMS

02:16:42.810 --> 02:16:45.060
for their tremendous hard work

02:16:45.060 --> 02:16:48.930
since we launched the
service back in 2018.

02:16:48.930 --> 02:16:53.930
In the paper, we set out the
commissioned infrastructure

02:16:53.970 --> 02:16:55.140
of the NHS.

02:16:55.140 --> 02:16:57.120
And essentially, this has created

02:16:57.120 --> 02:17:01.290
seven clinical innovation
hubs for genomics.

02:17:01.290 --> 02:17:03.630
And that's important as I come back

02:17:03.630 --> 02:17:05.910
to some of the things
I'm going to highlight

02:17:05.910 --> 02:17:08.250
in the next couple of minutes.

02:17:08.250 --> 02:17:11.490
So Richard mentioned
earlier that we've delivered

02:17:11.490 --> 02:17:16.490
over 810,000 genomic tests last year.

02:17:17.370 --> 02:17:21.600
Now that represents
world-leading prenatal testing,

02:17:21.600 --> 02:17:25.830
world-leading rapid whole
genome sequencing services

02:17:25.830 --> 02:17:30.030
for acutely unwell babies and children.

02:17:30.030 --> 02:17:32.580
And it also represents an expansion

02:17:32.580 --> 02:17:37.110
in the more comprehensive
genomic testing we do in cancer

02:17:37.110 --> 02:17:39.360
and including in paediatric cancer.

02:17:39.360 --> 02:17:43.017
And that's enabling precision treatments

02:17:43.017 --> 02:17:45.450
for first-line chemotherapy

02:17:45.450 --> 02:17:47.700
rather than later down the line.

02:17:47.700 --> 02:17:51.960
And included in that number of
40,000 pharmacogenomic tests

02:17:51.960 --> 02:17:56.280
that are preventing sometimes
fatal adverse drug reactions

02:17:56.280 --> 02:17:59.070
to some chemotherapy agents.

02:17:59.070 --> 02:18:01.710
Last year, we reached
the significant milestone

02:18:01.710 --> 02:18:05.850
of a hundred thousand whole
genome sequencing equivalents

02:18:05.850 --> 02:18:10.597
in our whole genome sequencing
service for the NHS.

02:18:10.597 --> 02:18:13.020
And that, again, is world-leading.

02:18:13.020 --> 02:18:15.900
This is a partnership
with Genomics England

02:18:15.900 --> 02:18:18.270
that we launched in 2020

02:18:18.270 --> 02:18:22.020
and it includes NHS patients

02:18:22.020 --> 02:18:23.820
consenting for their data

02:18:23.820 --> 02:18:27.810
to enter into the National
Genomic Research Library

02:18:27.810 --> 02:18:30.600
that supports diagnostic discovery

02:18:30.600 --> 02:18:32.700
and ongoing research.

02:18:32.700 --> 02:18:34.530
We're continuing to really drive

02:18:34.530 --> 02:18:39.060
operational performance
improvements as we deliver

02:18:39.060 --> 02:18:42.600
clinically meaningful
turnaround times for patients.

02:18:42.600 --> 02:18:43.433
In the paper,

02:18:43.433 --> 02:18:47.790
we set out that we've made 350 changes

02:18:47.790 --> 02:18:51.283
to the National Genomic
Test Directory since 2018.

02:18:51.283 --> 02:18:53.760
And I want to highlight a couple of those

02:18:53.760 --> 02:18:56.970
to show the breadth of the applications

02:18:56.970 --> 02:19:00.960
and the things that we introduced in 2024.

02:19:00.960 --> 02:19:05.430
So that included BRCA1 and
BRCA2 testing in breast cancer,

02:19:05.430 --> 02:19:07.822
following the testing that we introduced

02:19:07.822 --> 02:19:11.277
for prostate cancer in 2023

02:19:11.277 --> 02:19:16.277
for access to new cancer
drugs called PARP inhibitors.

02:19:16.590 --> 02:19:19.290
We introduced testing for the APOL1 gene

02:19:19.290 --> 02:19:21.600
for kidney donors of Black African

02:19:21.600 --> 02:19:23.610
and Black Caribbean heritage,

02:19:23.610 --> 02:19:27.480
helping to identify a
potential donors carry genes

02:19:27.480 --> 02:19:29.670
that they may mean they have a risk

02:19:29.670 --> 02:19:33.513
of kidney disease after donation.

02:19:34.530 --> 02:19:38.640
We introduced testing for RNU4-2 gene,

02:19:38.640 --> 02:19:42.775
newly found at the beginning of 2024,

02:19:42.775 --> 02:19:47.775
and shown to be associated
with developmental disorders.

02:19:47.940 --> 02:19:50.430
And it was introduced following research

02:19:50.430 --> 02:19:54.390
on NHS-consented data

02:19:54.390 --> 02:19:59.280
entering into the Research
Library at Genomics England.

02:19:59.280 --> 02:20:01.680
And that was introduced within six months

02:20:01.680 --> 02:20:03.813
of the research discovery.

02:20:05.208 --> 02:20:10.208
We have also introduced
cancer clinical trial targets

02:20:10.381 --> 02:20:13.620
as part of routine genomic testing

02:20:13.620 --> 02:20:17.880
to provide greater treatment
options for cancer patients.

02:20:17.880 --> 02:20:21.960
And as part of the NHS
Cancer Vaccines Launch Pad,

02:20:21.960 --> 02:20:23.970
that's part of it is done

02:20:23.970 --> 02:20:26.820
within the NHS Genomic Laboratory hubs.

02:20:26.820 --> 02:20:29.820
We are repatriating sequencing

02:20:29.820 --> 02:20:32.591
to the laboratory at the Royal Marsden

02:20:32.591 --> 02:20:35.700
that the sequencing will
be done much earlier,

02:20:35.700 --> 02:20:38.460
and then the individualised cancer vaccine

02:20:38.460 --> 02:20:41.910
can be made by the company.

02:20:41.910 --> 02:20:46.170
We've been piloting the use
of non-circulating tumour DNA

02:20:46.170 --> 02:20:49.620
or liquid biopsies for
non-small cell lung cancer.

02:20:49.620 --> 02:20:52.080
It's shown a considerable reduction

02:20:52.080 --> 02:20:53.906
in the testing pathway

02:20:53.906 --> 02:20:57.300
compared to conventional testing,

02:20:57.300 --> 02:20:59.192
tissue-based testing.

02:20:59.192 --> 02:21:01.155
And we're now rolling that out

02:21:01.155 --> 02:21:03.870
to other cancer types.

02:21:03.870 --> 02:21:06.210
And we're supporting Genomics England

02:21:06.210 --> 02:21:09.360
in the delivery of the generation study,

02:21:09.360 --> 02:21:13.770
which will be sequencing a
hundred thousand newborn babies

02:21:13.770 --> 02:21:18.770
to look for defined set of
rare disease much earlier on,

02:21:18.900 --> 02:21:22.980
given the high rate of
mortality and morbidity

02:21:22.980 --> 02:21:27.980
in the under of 5s with
some of these diseases.

02:21:28.350 --> 02:21:30.210
And you'll see in the paper,

02:21:30.210 --> 02:21:34.080
we've also driven pathway
transformation projects

02:21:34.080 --> 02:21:36.510
like in sudden cardiac death,

02:21:36.510 --> 02:21:40.590
where through identifying 108 index cases,

02:21:40.590 --> 02:21:44.910
we've got nearly 500
family members identified

02:21:44.910 --> 02:21:47.820
that previously didn't know
their risk and they've entered

02:21:47.820 --> 02:21:52.820
in to inherited cardiac
clinics within the NHS.

02:21:53.700 --> 02:21:56.250
So you'll see again in the paper

02:21:56.250 --> 02:21:59.520
that we're working very
close with Vin and Ming

02:21:59.520 --> 02:22:01.380
in the Transformation Directorate

02:22:01.380 --> 02:22:04.860
on the transformation of genomics data

02:22:04.860 --> 02:22:07.530
and digital and the infrastructure

02:22:07.530 --> 02:22:11.190
that needs to support our
world-leading infrastructure.

02:22:11.190 --> 02:22:13.350
And that includes the alignment

02:22:13.350 --> 02:22:15.600
with the federated data platform,

02:22:15.600 --> 02:22:17.580
but also the introduction

02:22:17.580 --> 02:22:19.290
and we're scoping this at the moment

02:22:19.290 --> 02:22:21.496
of a unified genomic record

02:22:21.496 --> 02:22:23.670
that will enable genomic data

02:22:23.670 --> 02:22:27.003
to be brought together with clinical data.

02:22:28.410 --> 02:22:30.012
Through the infrastructure

02:22:30.012 --> 02:22:33.390
that we've created as
clinical innovation hubs,

02:22:33.390 --> 02:22:38.040
we were recognised by the
Innovation Ecosystem Review

02:22:38.040 --> 02:22:40.860
and also by the Accelerated
Access Collaborative

02:22:40.860 --> 02:22:45.267
as an exemplar for adopting
innovation into the NHS.

02:22:45.267 --> 02:22:48.270
And we introduced and established

02:22:48.270 --> 02:22:52.350
eight NHS genomic networks of excellence

02:22:52.350 --> 02:22:55.110
that have been working
with industry partners

02:22:55.110 --> 02:22:57.540
with research-funding bodies

02:22:57.540 --> 02:23:01.950
to leverage their resources
in areas of unmet need

02:23:01.950 --> 02:23:04.800
to drive the model of adoption

02:23:04.800 --> 02:23:08.013
for commissioning in the NHS.

02:23:09.000 --> 02:23:12.630
One of those is in severe presentation

02:23:12.630 --> 02:23:14.490
of infectious disease

02:23:14.490 --> 02:23:16.150
that last year

02:23:16.150 --> 02:23:21.150
gained another 34 million
from the government

02:23:21.780 --> 02:23:26.280
looking at sequencing
respiratory pathogens

02:23:26.280 --> 02:23:29.730
from patients who are
in intensive care units,

02:23:29.730 --> 02:23:32.813
a pilot trialled at Guy's and St Thomas's

02:23:36.000 --> 02:23:37.860
but St. Thomas's actually

02:23:37.860 --> 02:23:41.820
that's really shown you can
reduce intensive care bed days

02:23:41.820 --> 02:23:44.310
but also use the signal,

02:23:44.310 --> 02:23:46.620
the respiratory pathogen signal

02:23:46.620 --> 02:23:51.620
as an early warning system
for biosecurity purposes.

02:23:52.530 --> 02:23:55.770
So this is a really important development

02:23:55.770 --> 02:23:58.890
and that development and
that network is also looking

02:23:58.890 --> 02:24:03.630
at more comprehensive
genomic testing on pathogens,

02:24:03.630 --> 02:24:07.560
I mean, on the actual
individual, in sepsis,

02:24:07.560 --> 02:24:10.953
and also in other clinical presentations.

02:24:11.790 --> 02:24:15.780
We continue to share our good
practise with other countries

02:24:15.780 --> 02:24:17.670
and just in 10 days,

02:24:17.670 --> 02:24:20.520
we'll be co-convening with WHO,

02:24:20.520 --> 02:24:24.030
a global network on education

02:24:24.030 --> 02:24:26.640
and training and looking
to share resources,

02:24:26.640 --> 02:24:28.560
share best practise.

02:24:28.560 --> 02:24:30.690
We are now at an inflexion point

02:24:30.690 --> 02:24:33.000
for genomics in the NHS

02:24:33.000 --> 02:24:34.200
where the demand

02:24:34.200 --> 02:24:36.480
and the application is
showing that we need

02:24:36.480 --> 02:24:39.510
to increase significantly

02:24:39.510 --> 02:24:41.280
over and above where we are

02:24:41.280 --> 02:24:44.370
at around 800,000

02:24:44.370 --> 02:24:46.233
to several million.

02:24:47.160 --> 02:24:51.060
And so there is much
more that we need to do

02:24:51.060 --> 02:24:52.890
than where we've been to date.

02:24:52.890 --> 02:24:56.820
So with the right investment
and prioritisation,

02:24:56.820 --> 02:24:59.370
genomics as an important role to play

02:24:59.370 --> 02:25:03.570
in those three shifts
articulated in the 10-Year Plan

02:25:03.570 --> 02:25:07.549
in the life sciences sector plan

02:25:07.549 --> 02:25:12.549
and also driving
transformation in healthcare

02:25:13.350 --> 02:25:15.870
such that by 2035,

02:25:15.870 --> 02:25:19.740
50% of all healthcare
episodes would include

02:25:19.740 --> 02:25:24.317
some form of genomic testing
across the life course,

02:25:25.260 --> 02:25:29.013
prenatal right through
to end-of-life care.

02:25:30.090 --> 02:25:31.770
In the paper, we've outlined

02:25:31.770 --> 02:25:35.250
some of the considerations for the future

02:25:35.250 --> 02:25:38.640
and these are some of
the priority enablers

02:25:38.640 --> 02:25:42.570
that need to be supported
through the spending review

02:25:42.570 --> 02:25:46.320
that will drive the step
change that's required.

02:25:46.320 --> 02:25:48.930
And some of the challenges we still face

02:25:48.930 --> 02:25:52.920
are raising awareness across the NHS,

02:25:52.920 --> 02:25:55.110
ensuring that there's equity of access,

02:25:55.110 --> 02:25:57.060
not just to genomic testing

02:25:57.060 --> 02:26:00.480
but to the associated precision medicines,

02:26:00.480 --> 02:26:02.850
the development of the whole workforce,

02:26:02.850 --> 02:26:04.860
not just the specialist workforce,

02:26:04.860 --> 02:26:07.200
and especially in primary care

02:26:07.200 --> 02:26:09.630
and really outlining the role

02:26:09.630 --> 02:26:11.880
that primary care will play in the future

02:26:11.880 --> 02:26:14.190
and there's lots of opportunities.

02:26:14.190 --> 02:26:17.220
And then finally, the number
of precision medicines

02:26:17.220 --> 02:26:19.380
that are coming down the pipeline

02:26:19.380 --> 02:26:24.220
that all require genomic
target testing to be done

02:26:25.230 --> 02:26:27.630
and will need to be on the front foot

02:26:27.630 --> 02:26:29.610
to make sure that we can plan for that

02:26:29.610 --> 02:26:32.730
and we've got the
investment to support that.

02:26:32.730 --> 02:26:34.680
So thank you, Richard.

02:26:34.680 --> 02:26:35.627
- Well, thank you very much.

02:26:35.627 --> 02:26:37.080
So I'm going to open up

02:26:37.080 --> 02:26:38.100
for comments and questions.

02:26:38.100 --> 02:26:41.520
I mean, we're world leading,
it's transformational,

02:26:41.520 --> 02:26:43.620
it's evidence of innovation.

02:26:43.620 --> 02:26:45.090
And then, there's always
going to be a question

02:26:45.090 --> 02:26:47.670
of prioritisation and resourcing.

02:26:47.670 --> 02:26:50.007
But you know, we are in
a really good position.

02:26:50.007 --> 02:26:54.060
For me, it's one of the
delights of this role actually,

02:26:54.060 --> 02:26:55.260
when I was introduced to this.

02:26:55.260 --> 02:26:57.480
I'm not a scientist, I'm not medical,

02:26:57.480 --> 02:26:59.130
I don't really understand it very well,

02:26:59.130 --> 02:27:02.010
but actually all I can tell
you is the energy behind it,

02:27:02.010 --> 02:27:03.210
the progress we've seen

02:27:03.210 --> 02:27:04.707
and the potential for it

02:27:04.707 --> 02:27:06.240
are quite remarkable actually.

02:27:06.240 --> 02:27:07.500
So thank you, let's go around.

02:27:07.500 --> 02:27:09.210
So, we'll go, can I come this way round?

02:27:09.210 --> 02:27:11.397
I'll go Robert, then you Wol.

02:27:11.397 --> 02:27:12.930
So, Robert first?

02:27:12.930 --> 02:27:15.330
- Yeah, a couple of comments
then a question, Sue.

02:27:15.330 --> 02:27:17.850
So, I mean the first
comment is congratulations

02:27:17.850 --> 02:27:19.320
on what you do in leading this.

02:27:19.320 --> 02:27:21.570
I think it is something nationally

02:27:21.570 --> 02:27:24.630
we should be very proud of
as you've quite rightly said.

02:27:24.630 --> 02:27:26.610
I would imagine people
around the table know

02:27:26.610 --> 02:27:28.698
just what a dramatic acceleration

02:27:28.698 --> 02:27:30.420
this whole field has seen.

02:27:30.420 --> 02:27:34.230
Just to remind you, the
first human sequence,

02:27:34.230 --> 02:27:36.260
genome name sequence was 2003.

02:27:36.260 --> 02:27:39.300
So 20 years ago, it took about 13 years,

02:27:39.300 --> 02:27:40.953
it cost $3 billion.

02:27:42.360 --> 02:27:45.600
Now it costs a few hundred pounds

02:27:45.600 --> 02:27:46.770
and you can do it in hours.

02:27:46.770 --> 02:27:48.840
So the pace of change,
- Pace of change.

02:27:48.840 --> 02:27:50.313
- is quite extraordinary.

02:27:51.540 --> 02:27:53.010
The second comment I was going to make,

02:27:53.010 --> 02:27:55.277
and you refer to it at the end, Sue,

02:27:55.277 --> 02:27:57.990
about multi-omics and
that's where we need to get,

02:27:57.990 --> 02:28:00.210
so that's combining
genomics with proteomics,

02:28:00.210 --> 02:28:02.130
with clinical data and so on.

02:28:02.130 --> 02:28:04.710
UK Biobank is just a
fantastic illustration

02:28:04.710 --> 02:28:07.740
as how powerful that is in
getting insights into disease.

02:28:07.740 --> 02:28:10.323
So I think that's absolutely
the right way to go.

02:28:11.190 --> 02:28:13.890
My question for you is
how long do you think

02:28:13.890 --> 02:28:15.990
it'll be before we sequence every newborn?

02:28:16.980 --> 02:28:18.780
- Sequence every newborn?

02:28:18.780 --> 02:28:20.220
Yeah, good question, Sue, Wol?

02:28:20.220 --> 02:28:22.617
- Well again, sorry to be dull about this

02:28:22.617 --> 02:28:24.780
and I do agree with Robert's comments

02:28:24.780 --> 02:28:27.603
about the extraordinary work
being done in this area.

02:28:28.774 --> 02:28:30.540
There's a sort of foundational element

02:28:30.540 --> 02:28:31.560
that needs to be put in place

02:28:31.560 --> 02:28:34.320
to achieve some of the futurist
stuff you were talking about

02:28:34.320 --> 02:28:38.970
and it really rests on data,
digital infrastructure.

02:28:38.970 --> 02:28:41.040
And it'd be really interesting
to understand what,

02:28:41.040 --> 02:28:43.050
you know, where are we now in your view

02:28:43.050 --> 02:28:46.260
and what do we need to put
in place going forward?

02:28:46.260 --> 02:28:48.360
- And then if you can batch the question.

02:28:48.360 --> 02:28:51.900
So Vin, I think you were going
to comment or ask a question.

02:28:51.900 --> 02:28:53.310
- Yeah, thank you, Richard.

02:28:53.310 --> 02:28:56.490
So firstly, can I just say
without wishing to be obsequious,

02:28:56.490 --> 02:28:58.050
can I just acknowledge the personal role

02:28:58.050 --> 02:28:59.520
you've played in championing genomics

02:28:59.520 --> 02:29:00.630
during your time as chair?

02:29:00.630 --> 02:29:02.670
Thank you very much taken that thing.

02:29:02.670 --> 02:29:04.167
- Don't ask me to explain it though.

02:29:07.860 --> 02:29:09.518
- The second thing, I think,

02:29:09.518 --> 02:29:11.070
is there are going to be three things

02:29:11.070 --> 02:29:13.080
that are going to completely
transform healthcare

02:29:13.080 --> 02:29:14.160
in the years to come.

02:29:14.160 --> 02:29:15.660
One is genomics, one is AI,

02:29:15.660 --> 02:29:17.220
and one is automation.

02:29:17.220 --> 02:29:18.750
And so as we look ahead as a board

02:29:18.750 --> 02:29:20.550
to what's going to come down the line,

02:29:20.550 --> 02:29:21.540
those are the three areas

02:29:21.540 --> 02:29:23.403
we should pay a lot of attention to.

02:29:24.480 --> 02:29:27.090
An area that I think Sue
hasn't talked about as well

02:29:27.090 --> 02:29:31.830
is that the UK genomics
industry is also world-leading.

02:29:31.830 --> 02:29:35.850
So the turnover of the UK
genomics companies is 2.4 billion.

02:29:35.850 --> 02:29:37.890
Market capitalization's 5 billion,

02:29:37.890 --> 02:29:40.980
and it's got the fastest
compound growth rate

02:29:40.980 --> 02:29:42.480
of many other industries

02:29:42.480 --> 02:29:45.390
and when you compare it
to the rest of the world.

02:29:45.390 --> 02:29:46.860
So I think when the government's talking

02:29:46.860 --> 02:29:50.307
about growth being its
number one priority area

02:29:50.307 --> 02:29:52.110
and we know the future
of the health service

02:29:52.110 --> 02:29:54.357
is tied to growth, actually investment,

02:29:54.357 --> 02:29:57.360
and the work that Sue's done
puts us in a strong position

02:29:57.360 --> 02:30:00.492
as a way that we actively
contribute to growth.

02:30:00.492 --> 02:30:02.310
And I think the way in
which we've done that,

02:30:02.310 --> 02:30:03.660
which should be a template actually

02:30:03.660 --> 02:30:05.760
for the way we think about
the future of health,

02:30:05.760 --> 02:30:07.500
which I think Sue's paper illustrates,

02:30:07.500 --> 02:30:10.920
is the way that basic science research,

02:30:10.920 --> 02:30:15.240
education, innovation, service delivery,

02:30:15.240 --> 02:30:17.760
and data are all linked
up with each other.

02:30:17.760 --> 02:30:19.410
So there's a comprehensive service

02:30:19.410 --> 02:30:21.210
and those are all the
ingredients that you need

02:30:21.210 --> 02:30:22.530
to create really great services.

02:30:22.530 --> 02:30:24.210
And actually that is a template

02:30:24.210 --> 02:30:26.126
in the way you might look at ICB

02:30:26.126 --> 02:30:28.080
or the way you might look at a region.

02:30:28.080 --> 02:30:30.180
You know, it's the way we
should think about health

02:30:30.180 --> 02:30:31.800
and going into the future.

02:30:31.800 --> 02:30:33.507
So just to finish up on the data

02:30:33.507 --> 02:30:36.365
and the answers to the question.

02:30:36.365 --> 02:30:39.150
So firstly, I think
good work is being done.

02:30:39.150 --> 02:30:43.650
We've got fantastic world-class
data assets like UK Biobank.

02:30:43.650 --> 02:30:46.470
We've got growing areas
like our future health,

02:30:46.470 --> 02:30:48.090
our secure data environments,

02:30:48.090 --> 02:30:50.130
and the unified genomics record

02:30:50.130 --> 02:30:51.803
that Sue and the team are
working with mine to develop

02:30:51.803 --> 02:30:56.310
and also to talk to government
about in the spending review.

02:30:56.310 --> 02:30:59.487
But I think it's also true
that one of our major gaps

02:30:59.487 --> 02:31:04.487
is the poor digital maturity
of the core NHS itself.

02:31:04.590 --> 02:31:05.875
- Yeah.
- And Lord Darzi

02:31:05.875 --> 02:31:07.585
called that out in his report

02:31:07.585 --> 02:31:10.080
about underinvestment in technology

02:31:10.080 --> 02:31:11.640
and that will be a challenge.

02:31:11.640 --> 02:31:13.020
Lack of interoperability

02:31:13.020 --> 02:31:14.820
between primary and secondary care,

02:31:14.820 --> 02:31:17.190
and genomics report is cited by clinicians

02:31:17.190 --> 02:31:19.980
as being one of the major
barriers to interoperability

02:31:19.980 --> 02:31:21.750
and it's one of the
major causes of friction

02:31:21.750 --> 02:31:23.730
between primary and secondary care.

02:31:23.730 --> 02:31:27.810
And so this is going to be a
major area for transformation.

02:31:27.810 --> 02:31:29.217
It's amazing work that Sue's done,

02:31:29.217 --> 02:31:32.070
but actually the underlying
poor technical infrastructure

02:31:32.070 --> 02:31:34.170
of the NHS, I think, will be a challenge

02:31:34.170 --> 02:31:36.900
if we are to remain
world-leading in this area.

02:31:36.900 --> 02:31:39.532
- So Sue, I mean, Vin's
probably mainly answered

02:31:39.532 --> 02:31:41.850
the question on data,

02:31:41.850 --> 02:31:43.320
but you may have some reflection on that.

02:31:43.320 --> 02:31:45.480
And then, there's
Robert's question as well

02:31:45.480 --> 02:31:49.230
about by when will we be sequencing

02:31:49.230 --> 02:31:52.380
all newborns as a sort of a question

02:31:52.380 --> 02:31:55.190
for your level of aspiration and ambition.

02:31:55.190 --> 02:31:57.360
- Yes, thank you.

02:31:57.360 --> 02:32:00.150
Just in terms of the data and digital,

02:32:00.150 --> 02:32:04.170
there's no doubt about it
that there's a variability

02:32:04.170 --> 02:32:06.600
in digital maturity across the NHS.

02:32:06.600 --> 02:32:09.270
I think some of the ways that
though that we're working

02:32:09.270 --> 02:32:11.250
in the Genomic Medicine Service

02:32:11.250 --> 02:32:13.885
means that some of the work can be done

02:32:13.885 --> 02:32:18.120
outside of that without a
level of digital development

02:32:18.120 --> 02:32:21.145
that needs to happen.

02:32:21.145 --> 02:32:25.840
Some of this is about lining
up pathology systems better

02:32:26.970 --> 02:32:28.337
that we need to work on.

02:32:28.337 --> 02:32:32.550
We are also starting to work
with global organisations

02:32:32.550 --> 02:32:36.690
on the adoption of some
important standards

02:32:36.690 --> 02:32:39.750
for genomics into EPR systems.

02:32:39.750 --> 02:32:41.820
And that will be a game-changer actually

02:32:41.820 --> 02:32:45.090
in this area.

02:32:45.090 --> 02:32:48.870
But the Unified Genomic
Record will enable us

02:32:48.870 --> 02:32:52.890
to provide data for multiple
sources including research

02:32:52.890 --> 02:32:55.860
and also for clinical trial purposes

02:32:55.860 --> 02:32:57.900
and population health.

02:32:57.900 --> 02:32:59.495
The second question,

02:32:59.495 --> 02:33:02.490
well, the first question from Robert

02:33:02.490 --> 02:33:05.670
was about newborn sequencing.

02:33:05.670 --> 02:33:07.650
I think there'll be another two years

02:33:07.650 --> 02:33:09.690
for the generation study

02:33:09.690 --> 02:33:14.040
that will be really important
in understanding the impact

02:33:14.040 --> 02:33:16.860
not just for the disease
they're looking at now,

02:33:16.860 --> 02:33:19.650
but the diseases that are
in the research environment

02:33:19.650 --> 02:33:21.378
at the moment.

02:33:21.378 --> 02:33:23.490
And as we come out of that,

02:33:23.490 --> 02:33:25.470
that will give us the evidence hopefully

02:33:25.470 --> 02:33:26.670
because of the impact,

02:33:26.670 --> 02:33:31.020
the understanding of the
impact on sequencing.

02:33:31.020 --> 02:33:33.360
I would like to think
I'm very aspirational

02:33:33.360 --> 02:33:35.700
not just for newborn sequencing

02:33:35.700 --> 02:33:39.180
but also in conjunction
with Genomics England,

02:33:39.180 --> 02:33:43.350
how we look at adult
population genome testing

02:33:43.350 --> 02:33:48.270
potentially in conjunction
with the NHS Health test.

02:33:48.270 --> 02:33:50.370
And particularly, that
would give us an opportunity

02:33:50.370 --> 02:33:53.310
to introduce pharmacogenomic profiles

02:33:53.310 --> 02:33:56.190
and look for diseases of a genomic origin,

02:33:56.190 --> 02:33:58.680
not just monogenic well,
but that, you know,

02:33:58.680 --> 02:34:03.540
but polygenic it that occur
in the adult population.

02:34:03.540 --> 02:34:08.160
And I think finally, this
whole coming together,

02:34:08.160 --> 02:34:10.320
this requirement to automate,

02:34:10.320 --> 02:34:12.870
we set out in the paper,

02:34:12.870 --> 02:34:15.150
our laboratories have got to now

02:34:15.150 --> 02:34:19.380
change fundamentally to
industrialise and automate

02:34:19.380 --> 02:34:22.410
to really drive efficiency
and productivity

02:34:22.410 --> 02:34:23.760
and meet the demand.

02:34:23.760 --> 02:34:24.613
- Yeah.
- It's not going

02:34:24.613 --> 02:34:27.780
to be more about putting more money in

02:34:27.780 --> 02:34:29.220
and expecting more out.

02:34:29.220 --> 02:34:33.240
We have to do things
fundamentally differently.

02:34:33.240 --> 02:34:36.330
And also use artificial intelligence,

02:34:36.330 --> 02:34:38.700
hence why we have a network of excellence

02:34:38.700 --> 02:34:41.580
that's focusing on what can be applied

02:34:41.580 --> 02:34:44.460
and what gains we can
get from applying AI.

02:34:44.460 --> 02:34:47.793
- Very good, so Sue, thank you very much.

02:34:47.793 --> 02:34:50.970
And you know, thank you for
your leadership in this space

02:34:50.970 --> 02:34:55.470
and I think it is
transformational and innovative

02:34:55.470 --> 02:34:57.920
and it's part of the future
of medicine actually.

02:34:58.860 --> 02:35:02.223
So thank you very much for what you do.

02:35:04.020 --> 02:35:06.780
So let's go on to the
last substantive item.

02:35:06.780 --> 02:35:09.900
I'm not in any way
demeaning the last item,

02:35:09.900 --> 02:35:11.670
which is the delegation of
the commissioning functions,

02:35:11.670 --> 02:35:13.170
which is a long running programme

02:35:13.170 --> 02:35:16.800
and process of delegation
out into the ICBs.

02:35:16.800 --> 02:35:21.600
But Amanda, can we pick up with
Tom on the Frailty Pathway?

02:35:21.600 --> 02:35:22.619
- We can, thank you.

02:35:22.619 --> 02:35:25.983
So people have got the short paper.

02:35:26.880 --> 02:35:27.750
Essentially as the population ages,

02:35:27.750 --> 02:35:28.583
the numbers of people who are suffering

02:35:28.583 --> 02:35:33.583
from multiple long-term conditions

02:35:34.857 --> 02:35:38.823
and consequent extreme
frailty is increasing.

02:35:38.823 --> 02:35:42.029
And therefore, the proportion
of healthcare resource

02:35:42.029 --> 02:35:46.680
that we use on this relatively small

02:35:46.680 --> 02:35:48.996
but very frail cohort of people

02:35:48.996 --> 02:35:50.640
is increasing significantly.

02:35:50.640 --> 02:35:55.640
And that's largely due to
increasing urgent acute

02:35:55.650 --> 02:35:57.750
or urgent acute activity.

02:35:57.750 --> 02:35:58.621
So hospital stays

02:35:58.621 --> 02:36:03.621
for largely elderly, very frail people.

02:36:04.830 --> 02:36:08.040
And not only do we use
a significant amount

02:36:08.040 --> 02:36:10.347
of our resource in this way,

02:36:10.347 --> 02:36:12.030
but actually we're not spending

02:36:12.030 --> 02:36:14.130
that resource in an effective way

02:36:14.130 --> 02:36:14.963
to give those patients

02:36:14.963 --> 02:36:18.150
either a good experience of care

02:36:18.150 --> 02:36:19.710
or the best possible outcomes.

02:36:19.710 --> 02:36:22.050
And in fact, for many of those patients,

02:36:22.050 --> 02:36:25.050
prolonged stays in hospitals actually,

02:36:25.050 --> 02:36:27.493
I mean, their overall condition worsens

02:36:27.493 --> 02:36:31.287
and reduces their ability to
live independently at home.

02:36:31.287 --> 02:36:35.490
And so we're spending a lot
of money on not serving well

02:36:35.490 --> 02:36:37.860
a very vulnerable
proportion of our population

02:36:37.860 --> 02:36:39.183
who are very frail.

02:36:40.470 --> 02:36:43.878
And what we are updating the board on here

02:36:43.878 --> 02:36:45.170
is the steps we are taking

02:36:45.170 --> 02:36:48.869
to offer more coordinated,

02:36:48.869 --> 02:36:50.244
better care and support to those patients

02:36:50.244 --> 02:36:54.043
largely in their own
home or in the community.

02:36:54.043 --> 02:36:55.890
- Thank you, so I mean, we can assume

02:36:55.890 --> 02:36:56.723
it's quite brief paper,
- Yes.

02:36:56.723 --> 02:36:58.950
- to raise it?

02:36:58.950 --> 02:36:59.910
Tom, do you want to say anything

02:36:59.910 --> 02:37:01.950
by way of introduction

02:37:01.950 --> 02:37:04.091
before we open to any
comments or questions?

02:37:04.091 --> 02:37:06.624
- Okay, so we had an earlier discussion

02:37:06.624 --> 02:37:09.141
at the private board on sort

02:37:09.141 --> 02:37:11.743
of a slightly longer paper

02:37:11.743 --> 02:37:14.326
attaching to this topic.

02:37:14.326 --> 02:37:19.326
So comments or questions
for Amanda or Tom?

02:37:19.440 --> 02:37:21.539
I mean, if I open from my perspective

02:37:21.539 --> 02:37:26.070
and I'm perfectly content

02:37:26.070 --> 02:37:27.750
to be completely wrong.

02:37:27.750 --> 02:37:29.658
But if it were me,

02:37:29.658 --> 02:37:32.940
this would be, not because of
the return on investment point

02:37:32.940 --> 02:37:35.850
because I can sense from
a both sides of the table,

02:37:35.850 --> 02:37:38.116
there'll be probably disagreement

02:37:38.116 --> 02:37:42.210
about what you invest in
for the longer term return.

02:37:42.210 --> 02:37:44.850
And from an old banking perspective,

02:37:44.850 --> 02:37:47.100
that's often the difference
between the net present value

02:37:47.100 --> 02:37:49.050
and the cash profile.

02:37:49.050 --> 02:37:51.330
So actually, it's what's
the upfront investment

02:37:51.330 --> 02:37:53.430
for long positive NPV?

02:37:53.430 --> 02:37:55.050
That's fine as long as you've got

02:37:55.050 --> 02:37:57.930
the available cash profile to invest.

02:37:57.930 --> 02:38:01.230
So I think that return
investment's a really strong one.

02:38:01.230 --> 02:38:03.138
I don't think about this like that.

02:38:03.138 --> 02:38:05.097
I think about this in terms

02:38:05.097 --> 02:38:08.430
of just how our capacity
is being utilised.

02:38:08.430 --> 02:38:11.160
So it's a relatively small
cohort of the population,

02:38:11.160 --> 02:38:13.680
but as you said in your
introductory remarks,

02:38:13.680 --> 02:38:16.760
it's a very intensive use

02:38:16.760 --> 02:38:17.728
of particular our acute care,

02:38:17.728 --> 02:38:20.550
but actually more broadly.

02:38:20.550 --> 02:38:23.000
So I think it's a really,
really important topic.

02:38:24.300 --> 02:38:28.500
So other comments, other questions,

02:38:28.500 --> 02:38:30.660
other thoughts about
how we engage? Robert?

02:38:30.660 --> 02:38:33.693
- Just a simple one, in paragraph seven,

02:38:34.890 --> 02:38:37.410
you talk about system partners

02:38:37.410 --> 02:38:39.720
will be provided with facilitation support

02:38:39.720 --> 02:38:42.120
and robust evaluation.

02:38:42.120 --> 02:38:44.103
The evaluation is obviously crucial

02:38:44.103 --> 02:38:46.020
if we're going to learn
what we want to learn.

02:38:46.020 --> 02:38:48.270
I wondered, this is a really

02:38:48.270 --> 02:38:49.830
health services research territory.

02:38:49.830 --> 02:38:54.210
Whether the NIHR ARCs are
getting engaged with this,

02:38:54.210 --> 02:38:56.700
they would seem to be a logical
source of evaluation skills.

02:38:56.700 --> 02:38:59.109
But I just my question is who's doing,

02:38:59.109 --> 02:39:01.276
who's going to do the evaluation?

02:39:01.276 --> 02:39:04.271
- So, we don't have an
answer for that yet.

02:39:04.271 --> 02:39:05.820
Paragraph seven refers

02:39:05.820 --> 02:39:07.710
to the neighbourhood health
implementation programme,

02:39:07.710 --> 02:39:09.600
which is part of our commitment

02:39:09.600 --> 02:39:12.210
to develop a neighbourhood health service

02:39:12.210 --> 02:39:14.970
shift activity and resource

02:39:14.970 --> 02:39:17.160
out of acute into primary
community services

02:39:17.160 --> 02:39:18.630
and focus on prevention.

02:39:18.630 --> 02:39:20.043
This is a key part of

02:39:20.043 --> 02:39:21.420
- Yeah
- the initial stages

02:39:21.420 --> 02:39:24.040
of that work, but we haven't completed

02:39:25.440 --> 02:39:28.590
the design of the programme

02:39:28.590 --> 02:39:31.260
and haven't yet agreed who
will do the evaluation.

02:39:31.260 --> 02:39:35.013
- So I would lean heavily on
NIHR and the ARCs to do so.

02:39:36.390 --> 02:39:37.770
- Because if we're going to shift

02:39:37.770 --> 02:39:41.880
out of acute into community

02:39:41.880 --> 02:39:45.623
and three quarters of our
beds are currently occupied,

02:39:45.623 --> 02:39:48.687
so 74% of bed occupancy is people over 65

02:39:48.687 --> 02:39:52.183
and 25% of beds are people with dementia.

02:39:52.183 --> 02:39:54.180
As I said in the mornings conversation,

02:39:54.180 --> 02:39:57.480
almost half of whom are initiated

02:39:57.480 --> 02:40:00.930
into the acute hospital because of a UTI

02:40:00.930 --> 02:40:03.720
or chest infection are
national level stats

02:40:03.720 --> 02:40:05.250
and those two conditions

02:40:05.250 --> 02:40:07.020
don't need to be treated in hospital.

02:40:07.020 --> 02:40:08.100
It doesn't mean all of those people

02:40:08.100 --> 02:40:10.200
shouldn't be in hospital
for other reasons,

02:40:10.200 --> 02:40:11.870
but the trigger of them
going into hospital

02:40:11.870 --> 02:40:13.980
is a UTI or chest infection.

02:40:13.980 --> 02:40:15.510
And if we want to shift capacity

02:40:15.510 --> 02:40:18.750
and shift therefore
resource into the community,

02:40:18.750 --> 02:40:20.040
and this is the population

02:40:20.040 --> 02:40:23.160
that largely occupies that capacity,

02:40:23.160 --> 02:40:24.750
that's why I think it's sort of like,

02:40:24.750 --> 02:40:27.840
it's an industrial question
about how do we do it.

02:40:27.840 --> 02:40:29.270
And are there some sort of,

02:40:29.270 --> 02:40:32.021
do you think there's some
quick-win interventions

02:40:32.021 --> 02:40:35.498
that can emerge within the
neighbourhood team structure

02:40:35.498 --> 02:40:37.620
that could accelerate that?

02:40:37.620 --> 02:40:38.790
Or?
- So we've got,

02:40:38.790 --> 02:40:39.870
and Tom may be able to talk,

02:40:39.870 --> 02:40:42.160
but we've got lots and lots of examples

02:40:42.160 --> 02:40:43.500
- Yeah.
- of real,

02:40:43.500 --> 02:40:45.648
- Good practise, yeah.
- of work

02:40:45.648 --> 02:40:46.481
that's really good practise

02:40:46.481 --> 02:40:48.510
that's really delivering
what we need to see.

02:40:48.510 --> 02:40:50.010
What we are not seeing

02:40:50.010 --> 02:40:52.145
is anywhere that's consistently delivering

02:40:52.145 --> 02:40:54.327
all the components well.

02:40:54.327 --> 02:40:58.139
And so, this is back to
finding good practise,

02:40:58.139 --> 02:41:00.030
scaling it up and spreading

02:41:00.030 --> 02:41:01.860
and particularly for the sites

02:41:01.860 --> 02:41:05.250
which we want to work intensively
as part of this programme,

02:41:05.250 --> 02:41:07.080
the learning and evidence sites,

02:41:07.080 --> 02:41:09.060
really seeing what it takes to put

02:41:09.060 --> 02:41:12.155
all of the components in at some pace

02:41:12.155 --> 02:41:15.390
and evaluate what difference that makes.

02:41:15.390 --> 02:41:16.890
And some of this stuff is here now,

02:41:16.890 --> 02:41:18.402
the ability to treat those patients

02:41:18.402 --> 02:41:21.390
at home exists now.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

02:41:21.390 --> 02:41:23.134
Jane, and then, Wol?

02:41:23.134 --> 02:41:24.797
- Thanks, I mean, really
just to that last point.

02:41:24.797 --> 02:41:28.050
I mean, I think the next
time we sort of come back

02:41:28.050 --> 02:41:30.076
to look at this work, it'd just be really,

02:41:30.076 --> 02:41:31.590
it'll be really interesting

02:41:31.590 --> 02:41:33.364
to understand what those timescales are

02:41:33.364 --> 02:41:35.490
in that test and learn environment.

02:41:35.490 --> 02:41:38.670
I mentioned earlier that
encouragement to public services

02:41:38.670 --> 02:41:41.035
to, you know, take risks,
innovate, et cetera.

02:41:41.035 --> 02:41:43.290
So it'd just be really good to get

02:41:43.290 --> 02:41:45.036
a handle on what that looks like,

02:41:45.036 --> 02:41:48.395
what that potential for stepping appears

02:41:48.395 --> 02:41:51.420
when we find things
that are really working,

02:41:51.420 --> 02:41:54.750
and you know, I think for
all of us to just understand

02:41:54.750 --> 02:41:56.223
what that looks like.

02:41:56.223 --> 02:41:57.757
- Yeah, thank you.
- And you, Wol?

02:41:57.757 --> 02:41:59.156
- And my point is just, again,

02:41:59.156 --> 02:42:00.267
I wanted to a bit it earlier this morning,

02:42:00.267 --> 02:42:01.890
but I think it's really important

02:42:01.890 --> 02:42:03.690
is when you come up with that plan,

02:42:03.690 --> 02:42:05.760
the timeline Jane's talking about

02:42:05.760 --> 02:42:06.960
is around how you're going to work

02:42:06.960 --> 02:42:09.267
with the private sector, you know,

02:42:09.267 --> 02:42:12.186
385,000 beds that are
in the private sector

02:42:12.186 --> 02:42:14.520
that are occupied by elderly people

02:42:14.520 --> 02:42:15.930
compared to our, what is it?

02:42:15.930 --> 02:42:18.420
16, 17,000, 20,000?
- 20,000.

02:42:18.420 --> 02:42:19.980
- So there's got to be connectivity there

02:42:19.980 --> 02:42:21.390
in predicting what demands coming

02:42:21.390 --> 02:42:22.860
at you and why and when

02:42:22.860 --> 02:42:24.720
and what learn you can get from that.

02:42:24.720 --> 02:42:27.450
So I strongly urge you to
figure out a way of using

02:42:27.450 --> 02:42:29.760
digital data and coming back
the same boring stuff again,

02:42:29.760 --> 02:42:32.613
but it's foundational in
order to drive improvement.

02:42:34.380 --> 02:42:35.508
- So do you want to add?

02:42:35.508 --> 02:42:38.652
- Yeah, I just think you're
absolutely right, Wol.

02:42:38.652 --> 02:42:41.010
And I think the important
thing we have to recognise

02:42:41.010 --> 02:42:43.590
is we will never support and care

02:42:43.590 --> 02:42:45.300
for this cohort of the population

02:42:45.300 --> 02:42:48.570
unless we work with social
care in the care sector.

02:42:48.570 --> 02:42:51.210
Both care homes, domiciliary care,

02:42:51.210 --> 02:42:56.210
also some of the wider
independent contract services,

02:42:56.331 --> 02:43:00.030
pharmacy services, medicine
services, diagnostics,

02:43:00.030 --> 02:43:01.973
- Voluntary community,
- Yeah, yeah.

02:43:01.973 --> 02:43:03.720
- and faith sector prior to support.

02:43:03.720 --> 02:43:06.180
So the benefits of taking

02:43:06.180 --> 02:43:07.770
this neighbourhood health service approach

02:43:07.770 --> 02:43:11.760
is about being able to work across sectors

02:43:11.760 --> 02:43:14.449
to coordinate the way
in which we offer care.

02:43:14.449 --> 02:43:15.757
- Yeah, Andrew?

02:43:15.757 --> 02:43:17.026
- It can be really, really effective.

02:43:17.026 --> 02:43:21.060
I mean, Claire, you work in my old system.

02:43:21.060 --> 02:43:24.510
And as a GP, if you come across a patient,

02:43:24.510 --> 02:43:27.810
you know, that you know needs help

02:43:27.810 --> 02:43:29.760
but doesn't necessarily need admission,

02:43:29.760 --> 02:43:30.990
you make one call,

02:43:30.990 --> 02:43:32.891
you get an assessment done on the day,

02:43:32.891 --> 02:43:34.860
you get access to social care,

02:43:34.860 --> 02:43:36.531
you get access to physio support,

02:43:36.531 --> 02:43:39.148
access to a geriatrician, it's superb,

02:43:39.148 --> 02:43:42.660
and it's reduced the work
going into the acute sector

02:43:42.660 --> 02:43:43.860
and it's one of the best things

02:43:43.860 --> 02:43:45.840
that I think the local leadership

02:43:45.840 --> 02:43:47.086
has done in that part of the world.

02:43:47.086 --> 02:43:48.400
- So that's Surrey Heartlands?

02:43:48.400 --> 02:43:49.233
- No.
- No?

02:43:49.233 --> 02:43:50.919
- That's fabulous Frimley.

02:43:50.919 --> 02:43:52.527
- Fabulous Frimley.
- Fabulous Frimley.

02:43:52.527 --> 02:43:54.847
but used to run Surrey Heartlands, yes.

02:43:54.847 --> 02:43:56.640
- Okay, and also actually last week,

02:43:56.640 --> 02:43:57.600
I mean Sandwell,

02:43:57.600 --> 02:44:00.057
the Frailty initiative in Sandwell,

02:44:00.057 --> 02:44:00.890
remarkable the

02:44:00.890 --> 02:44:02.248
Jean Gene Bishop Centre,

02:44:02.248 --> 02:44:03.782
we talk about a lot, don't we?

02:44:03.782 --> 02:44:06.840
In Hull. Sussex ICB on Monday,

02:44:06.840 --> 02:44:07.920
I mean, they'd be to pick up

